"""Original Security teaching packs. Each body is instructor prose."""

BODIES = {
    'Preparation': {
        'body': "Preparation is the incident work you finish while the building is quiet. It is the contacts, the logs, the backups, and the written plan that people have actually read, so the first hour of a bad day is not spent hunting for a phone number. In the NIST incident-handling order, this phase comes first. Detection and analysis come next. Containment, eradication, recovery, and lessons learned all assume this work already exists.\n\nThe work is concrete. You name who is on call, who may declare an incident, and who speaks to leadership. You keep authentication logs, firewall denies, and application errors long enough to cover a weekend away. You test that a backup can be restored, and you store the restore steps where the on-call person can open them without waking the only person who knows the password. A small kit holds a spare disk, a write blocker, and labeled bags, because memory on a running computer will not wait while someone orders tools. You also pick an out-of-band way to talk, such as a phone tree, so a compromised mailbox is not the only way to reach the team.\n\nPriya Shah leads security for a regional clinic. On a quiet Tuesday she prints the on-call list and the imaging-server restore steps and clips both inside the operations binder. The list names her, Luis Ortiz on the network team, and the clinic director's mobile number. Last month the team restored one test file from the Friday-night backup onto a spare virtual machine and wrote the elapsed time, forty minutes, on that same sheet. The sheet also says the log server keeps ninety days of firewall and sign-in events. When a real incident starts, the sheet is already true.\n\nA defender treats a missing log source or an untested backup as a hole in this phase, not as a surprise to discover at midnight. Priya checks that the central log server is still receiving the firewall and the electronic-record application, and she replaces any on-call name that has left the clinic. She does not wait for encrypted file shares to learn that the backup password lives only in one person's head. The plan on the shelf is useful only if the people who will use it have read it once while nothing was broken.\n\nStudents confuse this phase with lessons learned. Lessons learned happen after the service is back, and they name a change with an owner. This ready state is what you build before any incident exists. Isolating a laptop is not this phase either. That act is containment, and it comes only after detection and analysis have shown which machine is involved.",
        'r0': 'Preparation is the first NIST incident-handling phase, and it is finished before detection and analysis begin. Name the on-call roster, the person who may declare an incident, and a phone path that does not depend on the corporate mailbox. Keep firewall denies and authentication events for at least ninety days so a weekend intrusion is still visible on Monday. Restore one file from backup onto a spare system and write down the minutes it took. Put a write blocker and a blank evidence disk in a kit you can carry. A plan file that nobody has opened is not this phase. Containment, such as disabling a stolen account, is a later phase and is not a substitute for the roster, the logs, or the tested restore.',
        'r1': 'Get ready before the bad day starts. Write the on-call names and phone numbers. Print the steps to restore a backup. Test one restore while nothing is broken. Keep logs long enough to cover a weekend. Store a spare disk and a write blocker. Say who may declare an incident. Read the plan on a quiet day. A missing phone number is a ready-state gap. Do not invent the plan after the alarm. This work is not the meeting after recovery. Practice finding the binder with the lights on.',
        'r2': 'A firehouse stocks hose, names the night crew, and walks the hydrant route on a calm afternoon. The wrench has a hook. The phone list hangs by the door, and a new firefighter reads it before any bell rings. Nobody opens a catalog while a room is filling with smoke, because that catalog moment already happened on a drill. If the wrench is missing, the crew learns it during practice, not in a burning doorway. The posted crew list and the walked route make the first minute orderly. Preparation is that stocked firehouse: contacts, logs, backups, and a plan people have already read. The printed restore steps are the hydrant route, walked before anything is on fire.',
        'r4': "Write the on-call names, the phone numbers, and who may declare an incident. Print the restore steps for the systems the business cannot lose. Run one restore test and record how long it took. Check that logs are still arriving and that retention covers a weekend. Stock a write blocker and a spare disk in a place the night person can open. Do not wait for an alert to learn that a password lives in one person's head. Leave the plan where that person can read it without opening someone else's mailbox.",
        'exam': 'This phase is first, and it happens before any incident. The meeting after the service returns is lessons learned, not this ready work. Isolating a host, disabling a stolen account, and blocking a hostile domain are containment. They are not this phase, and they do not delete malware. A printed contact list and a tested backup are the ready work. A plan file nobody has read is not ready. Detection and analysis still have to happen before anyone isolates a machine.',
    },
    'Detection': {
        'body': "Detection is the moment you notice that something may be wrong. The notice can be an alert, a person's report, or a line in a log. It is the front half of the NIST phase called detection and analysis, which sits after preparation and before containment. Noticing is not the same as measuring how far the problem reaches. It is also not a decision to unplug a machine or to delete a file.\n\nSignals become an incident when they agree. A single odd event can be a mistake or a test. When a person and a record describe the same harm, you have a start time you can write down. That clock matters because every later phase is measured from it. You record who noticed the problem, what they saw, and which system produced the matching alert. You do not edit the log to make the story cleaner, and you do not treat a quiet console as proof that nothing happened.\n\nLuis Ortiz works the morning shift for a school district. A teacher calls the desk and says files on the shared drive are changing their names so they end in .locked. In the same minute the endpoint console raises an alert that a ransom note was written on file server FS-02. Luis opens ticket 4188, pastes the teacher's words beside alert id RN-2207, and marks the start as 08:12. The incident has begun because the person and the alert describe one event, not because anyone has yet listed every affected share.\n\nAt this point the defender captures the notice and protects the scene for analysis. Luis leaves FS-02 powered on and does not delete the renamed files. He asks the teacher not to reboot the classroom computer she was using. He pages the on-call lead with the ticket number and the two agreeing facts. Those steps keep memory and disk available so the rest of this NIST phase can measure scope. Wiping the server would destroy the only evidence and would skip the work that has not happened yet.\n\nThe trap is to treat the alert as containment, or as proof the cause is already gone. An alert only detects. Disabling an account or isolating a host comes later, in containment, after analysis says how wide the problem is. Eradication, which deletes malware after a copy is saved, is further still. A missed phone call is a detection failure even when the product was licensed and the plan was printed.",
        'r0': "Detection opens the NIST phase named detection and analysis. It is the notice, not the scope. Ticket 4188 starts at 08:12 because a teacher report and alert RN-2207 both describe files renamed to end in .locked on server FS-02. Write the caller's words, the alert id, and the host before you change anything. Leave the server powered on. Do not disable accounts yet, and do not isolate the host yet. Those acts are containment, the next phase. Do not delete the ransom note. Deleting malware is eradication, and only after evidence is preserved. A silent dashboard does not mean this phase succeeded.",
        'r1': 'Notice that something is wrong. An alert can be the notice. A person can be the notice. A log line can be the notice. Write down who saw it and when. Keep the machine turned on. Do not delete the strange files yet. Do not unplug the server yet. Matching reports mean you should start a ticket. A quiet screen is not proof of health. Scope comes after the notice. Stopping the spread is a later step. Page the person who is on call. Put the ticket number in your note.',
        'r2': 'In a clinic, a nurse smells smoke in the hallway and a ceiling sensor chirps at the same moment. She writes the time on the board and tells the charge nurse which corridor. She does not yet know whether the source is a toaster or a store room, and she does not hose the whole floor. Other staff keep the door closed only after someone looks. The smell plus the chirp is the notice. Detection is that paired notice: a person, an alert, or a log, with a start time written down. Measuring how far it spread is the next half of the same phase, not this first bell.',
        'r4': "Record the start time, the caller's words, and the alert or log line that agrees. Name the host or account in the note. Leave the affected computer powered on. Ask the user not to reboot and not to delete files. Page the on-call lead with the ticket number. Do not disable accounts, isolate hosts, or block names until analysis has listed the spread. Do not treat a quiet dashboard as an all-clear.",
        'exam': 'Detection is the notice inside the NIST phase detection and analysis. It is not the scope, which is analysis, and it is not containment. Disabling a stolen account, isolating a host, and blocking a hostile domain do not belong in this step. An alert does not delete malware. Eradication is the later removal of malware, the hole, and reusable secrets, after evidence is kept. A user report that matches a log is enough to open the incident. Silence on a dashboard is not proof that nothing is wrong.',
    },
    'Analysis': {
        'body': 'Analysis is the second half of the NIST phase detection and analysis. It decides what you are looking at, how far it reaches, and how bad it is. You finish that picture before anyone reimages a machine or deletes the only copy of the evidence. The products are a host list, a timeline, and an impact statement the business can recognize. A hunch is not a product. Containment, eradication, and recovery wait until this picture exists.\n\nYou build the picture from logs that preparation already kept. Which hosts talked to the strange name. Which accounts succeeded after a burst of failures. Which files changed, and the earliest timestamp. You separate what you know from what you guess, and you write both in the ticket. Severity follows from what the business loses if this continues, not from how loud the alert sounded. A single noisy laptop can matter less than a quiet jump box that touched every file server.\n\nNora Berg is the analyst on a credit-union incident, ticket 5521. The detection note says one teller computer showed a new scheduled task at 01:40. Nora searches the log server and lists four systems, not one: the teller computer, two branch file servers, and a jump box that authenticated with service account svc-night at that same minute. She writes that payroll processing is stopped and that member files may have moved. She refuses a reimage until that four-host list is in the ticket. The list is the difference between a story and a scope.\n\nDefenders use the list to choose containment that fits the spread, and they still do not perform eradication. Nora recommends isolating those four hosts and disabling svc-night so the next sign-in fails. She asks operations not to rebuild anything yet, because a rebuild would destroy memory and the disk she still needs copied. She also notes a hostile name the jump box resolved, so a later block has a target. Analysis that skips the list produces a clean machine and a timeline nobody can prove.\n\nStudents call this work eradication because it feels like progress. Eradication removes malware and patches the hole after evidence is preserved. This half of the phase does not delete the cause. It measures it. Disabling svc-night, when someone does it, will be containment, not this measurement and not eradication. If you reimage first, you have skipped the scope and you have also skipped the evidence the later removal is supposed to follow.',
        'r0': 'Analysis completes NIST detection and analysis by naming scope before any wipe. On ticket 5521 the detection note mentioned one teller computer. The log search showed four systems and service account svc-night at 01:40. Write the host list, the earliest time, and the business impact, here a stopped payroll run. Recommend containment next: isolate those hosts and disable the stolen or abused account. Do not reimage yet. Do not delete the scheduled task yet. Deleting malware and patching the hole are eradication, and only after a copy of the evidence exists. Recovery is the restore, later still. A loud alert on one PC is not a finished scope.',
        'r1': 'Figure out how far it goes before you erase anything. List every host the logs support. Write the earliest time you can prove. Separate facts from guesses. Say what the business cannot do right now. Leave the machines on. Do not rebuild them yet. Do not delete the bad program yet. A bigger list changes the next action. One loud computer may not be the whole story. Measurement is not removal. Ask what work has stopped. Keep the notes in the ticket.',
        'r2': 'A clinic doctor sees a rash and does not scrub the skin clean in the first minute. She notes which arm, how far the marks travel, and whether the patient can still work a shift. She writes those facts on the chart before anyone applies a treatment that would hide the edges. Washing first would make the afternoon guess. The chart is the point of the pause. Analysis is that chart: which systems, how far, and how bad, written before a rebuild hides the evidence. Containment can close doors afterward. Removal of the cause waits until a copy exists. The pause is what keeps the edges visible.',
        'r4': 'List every affected host, account, and suspicious name the logs support. Write the earliest timestamp you can defend. State the business impact in the ticket in plain words. Mark unknowns as unknowns. Recommend which hosts to isolate and which accounts to disable, and label those recommendations as the next phase. Do not reimage, do not delete malware, and do not patch yet. Keep the original systems available for a copy.',
        'exam': "Analysis is the scope half of NIST detection and analysis, not a later phase. It lists hosts and impact before anyone wipes a disk. Reimaging is not this step. Disabling a stolen account and isolating a host are containment, even if this writeup recommends them. Deleting malware, patching the hole, and resetting secrets are eradication, and only after evidence is preserved. Recovery restores clean service. An answer that calls a four-host list 'removal of the cause' has mixed this step with eradication.",
    },
    'Containment': {
        'body': "Containment is the NIST phase that stops the spread while you keep what you need to understand the incident. It comes after detection and analysis, and it comes before eradication. The goal is to deny the attacker more ground without destroying the evidence of how they got in. Isolating a host, disabling a stolen account, and blocking a hostile domain are this phase. None of those steps deletes the malware. The files can remain on disk while the paths they use are cut.\n\nShort-term action is fast and local. You remove the sick laptop from the network, or you place it on a segment that cannot reach file servers, and you leave it powered on so memory remains. You disable the account the attacker is using so the next sign-in fails. You block the domain or address the host is calling, at the firewall or the resolver, so other computers cannot fetch the same payload. You write the clock time of each action in the ticket. You do not format the disk, and you do not run a cleaner that removes files you have not copied.\n\nJonah Patel handles city-hall laptop LAP-19 on ticket 3904. Analysis has already named that laptop, the account j.okonkwo, and the name update-sync.example. Jonah disables j.okonkwo in the directory, disconnects LAP-19 from the office network, and leaves the lid open so the machine stays up. He adds a block for update-sync.example on the internal resolver. The malicious files are still on the disk. That is the right result for this phase. The account, the host, and the domain are stopped. The cause is not yet removed.\n\nA defender checks that the block and the disable actually took effect. Jonah looks for a new successful sign-in by j.okonkwo and for any other host still resolving the blocked name. A second host, if one appears, gets the same network cut. He notes the on-screen ransom text and the clock time without powering the laptop off. He does not 'clean' LAP-19 to feel finished, because cleaning would be an attempt at eradication done too early and done on the only copy.\n\nThe exam trap is to call these acts eradication. Eradication comes later: delete the malware, patch the hole, and reset secrets the attacker could use, after a preserved copy exists. Recovery is later still, when clean service returns. If a choice says isolating the host removed the program, it has mixed two phases. This phase keeps the program in place and cuts off its path.",
        'r0': 'Containment is the NIST phase after detection and analysis and before eradication. On ticket 3904, Jonah disables stolen account j.okonkwo, isolates laptop LAP-19 while leaving it powered on, and blocks update-sync.example. Those three acts stop spread. They do not delete malware. Do not format the disk and do not run a cleaner. Memory is still evidence, so power stays on until acquisition captures it. Eradication, later, deletes the program, patches the hole, and resets secrets the attacker could use. Recovery restores service from a clean point after that removal. Confirm the disable and the block actually hold before you move on. A second host that still calls out needs the same cut.',
        'r1': 'Stop the spread and keep the evidence. Take the sick computer off the network. Leave the power on. Disable the account the attacker is using. Block the name it keeps calling. Write the time of each action. Leave the bad program where it is for now. Do not wipe the disk yet. Check that the account cannot sign in. Check that other computers are not still calling out. A second sick computer gets the same cut. Cleaning comes later. Write each action in the ticket.',
        'r2': 'A warehouse finds a leaking drum in one aisle. The crew closes the aisle doors, stops the forklift route, and shuts the valve on that drum. They leave the spill on the floor so a photo can show how far it ran. Nobody mops yet, because mopping would erase the trail and might push liquid under the next rack. Doors and a closed valve halt the spread. Containment is those doors: isolate the host, disable the stolen account, and block the hostile name. The spill, the malware, stays until a record exists and a later phase removes it. Mopping early would pretend the leak was already solved.',
        'r4': 'Isolate each host that analysis named, and leave it powered on if memory still matters. Disable the stolen or abused account in the directory. Block the hostile domain or address at the resolver or the firewall. Record the time of each action in the ticket. Confirm the account cannot sign in and that other hosts are not still resolving the name. Do not delete malware, do not patch, and do not restore from backup in this phase.',
        'exam': 'Disabling a stolen account is containment. Isolating a host is containment. Blocking a hostile domain is containment. Those steps stop spread and keep evidence. They do not delete the malware. Eradication comes after a preserved copy and removes the malware, patches the hole, and resets secrets the attacker could use. Recovery is the later return of clean service. An answer that says the network cut deleted the program has mixed this phase with eradication. Power stays on when memory is still needed.',
    },
    'Eradication': {
        'body': "Eradication is the NIST phase that removes the cause, and it starts only after evidence has been preserved. Containment has already stopped the spread. The stolen account is already disabled, the host is already isolated, and the hostile domain is already blocked. Those earlier acts belong to containment, not here. In this phase you delete the malware, patch the hole that let it in, and reset secrets the attacker could use. Recovery, which restores clean service, is still ahead.\n\nThe mechanism is removal with a record. You copy the malicious file, the scheduled task, and the relevant logs into the ticket or onto the evidence image first. Then you delete the malware, remove the attacker's scheduled job, and close the vulnerable setting or missing patch that gave them a start. You reset passwords, keys, and tokens the attacker could have read. If the attacker created a new account, you remove that account, because it is part of the cause. Disabling the employee's stolen account already happened in containment. Resetting that password so the old secret cannot be reused is part of removing the cause, and it is not the same act as the earlier disable.\n\nAisha Diaz works a manufacturer incident, ticket 6102. The image of server PLANT-7 is already hashed and stored. She copies the scheduled task named HealthCheck into the ticket, including its command line, and then deletes the task. She removes the dropped binary from the ProgramData folder. She applies the missing patch for the remote-management service the initial exploit used. She resets the password on the service account the attacker had used and revokes the API token that account could present. The host stays off the production network until recovery says it is clean.\n\nDefenders verify the cause is actually gone before they call the phase done. Aisha searches PLANT-7 again for the same binary and the same task name, and she confirms the patch is present. She checks that the old password fails. A second host that never received the patch is still a way back in, so this phase includes that host too, not only the one that made noise. Leaving the hole open because the visible file is gone invites the same entry on the next quiet night.\n\nStudents mark 'disable the stolen account' as this phase. That disable is containment. Students also think a rebuild is this phase. Rebuilding from a clean source and watching for a return is recovery. This phase is the removal of malware, the hole, and the reusable secrets, done after the evidence is safe. Blocking a domain is also not this phase. The block already happened when the spread was stopped.",
        'r0': 'Eradication is the NIST phase after containment and before recovery. Evidence comes first. On ticket 6102 the image of PLANT-7 is hashed and stored, and only then does Aisha delete the HealthCheck scheduled task and the dropped binary. She patches the remote-management service that was the hole and resets the service-account password plus its API token. Disabling the stolen user account was containment and is not this phase. Isolating the host and blocking the hostile domain were containment too. Recovery will restore clean service after this removal. A second unpatched host is still in scope. Resetting a stolen password is this removal. The earlier disable only stopped the next sign-in.',
        'r1': "Remove the cause only after you have a copy. Delete the bad program from the saved image's twin. Remove the attacker's scheduled job. Patch the hole that let them in. Reset passwords and keys they could use. Take away any account they created. The earlier disable of a stolen account was a different phase. The network block was a different phase. Check that the old password now fails. Check the other servers for the same hole. Then you may restore service.",
        'r2': 'After a kitchen photographs a spoiled delivery, the crew throws out the bad crates and fixes the cooler that warmed them. They also change the padlock because the old key was on a clipboard the driver could see. They do not reopen the dining room yet. The photos stay in the office. Throwing out crates without fixing the cooler would only pause the smell. Eradication is throwing out the malware, patching the hole, and resetting secrets the attacker could use, after the evidence copy exists. Putting the dining room back in service is the next phase, not this cleanup. Closing the kitchen door earlier only stopped the smell from spreading.',
        'r4': "Confirm a preserved copy or image exists before you delete anything. Record the malware path, the scheduled task, and the command line in the ticket. Delete the malware and the attacker's task. Patch the hole or close the vulnerable setting. Reset passwords, keys, and tokens the attacker could have used. Remove accounts the attacker created. Do not call the earlier account disable or the host isolation this phase. Verify the old secret fails and that peer hosts received the same patch.",
        'exam': 'Eradication removes the cause after evidence is preserved. Delete the malware, patch the hole, and reset secrets the attacker could use. Remove attacker-created accounts and malicious scheduled tasks. Disabling a stolen account is containment, not this phase. Cutting the host off the network is still that earlier phase. A block on the hostile name belongs there too. None of those three deletes the malware. Recovery is the later restore of clean service, not this removal. An answer that stops at the account disable has not finished the cause.',
    },
    'Recovery': {
        'body': "Recovery is the NIST phase that brings the service back from a clean point and watches to see whether the cause returns. Eradication has already removed the malware, closed the hole, and reset the secrets the attacker could use. Containment's isolation and account disable are still part of the story, but they are not the restore. A machine that is merely unplugged is contained, not recovered. This phase is the return to work, done on purpose, with someone watching.\n\nYou return service from something you trust. That can be a backup taken before the harmful change, a rebuild from a known-good image, or a failover to a system that was never touched. You confirm the backup itself does not contain the malware or the attacker's task. You restore the smallest set of data the business needs, then you reconnect to the network only after that check. You watch the same logs that first showed the incident, looking for the same names, file hashes, and accounts, long enough to notice a return. The business owner accepts the service back. Your own sense that it looks fine is not a substitute for their acceptance.\n\nHelen Brooks supports a public library, ticket 2775. Ransomware encrypted the catalog share on Saturday at 19:10. The last clean backup finished Saturday at 18:00. After the cause was removed, Helen restores that share onto a rebuilt server and opens a sample of records with the catalog librarian. Overnight she watches for the domain that was blocked while the spread was being stopped, and for the scheduled-task name that was deleted during removal. At 07:00 the librarian signs the ticket to say lending can resume. One hour of Saturday-evening edits is gone. That loss was the price of a copy made before the encryption.\n\nA defender does not declare victory when the files reappear. Helen leaves extra logging on for the week and keeps the hostile name blocked. If the same binary returns, this phase has failed and the team goes back to measuring scope rather than pretending the restore finished the job. She also checks that a routine script does not quietly re-enable the account that was disabled to stop the attacker. A restore that skips the watch is only a copy, not a completed return.\n\nThe trap is to call the restore eradication, or to call containment a recovery because the bleeding stopped. Eradication removes the cause. This phase returns clean service and watches. A backup taken after encryption began will restore the attacker's files along with the data. Clean means the point in time is before the harm, not merely that the copy job reported success.",
        'r0': "Recovery is the NIST phase after eradication. It restores clean service and watches for a return. On ticket 2775 the catalog share was encrypted Saturday at 19:10, and the last clean backup finished at 18:00, so one hour of edits is the loss. Helen rebuilds the server, restores that 18:00 copy, and has the librarian confirm a sample before lending resumes. She watches overnight for the blocked domain and the deleted task name. Disabling the stolen account was containment. Deleting malware and patching were eradication. A backup taken after 19:10 would put the attack back on disk. The librarian's sign-off is part of the phase, not a courtesy.",
        'r1': 'Bring the service back from a clean copy. Use a backup from before the harm. Make sure that copy is not itself sick. Let the business owner check a sample. Watch overnight for the same warning signs. Keep the extra logging on for a while. Do not call an unplugged machine restored. Do not treat the cleanup as the restore. A copy made too late brings the problem back. Write down what work was lost. Stop if the bad signs return.',
        'r2': 'A dining room reopens only after the spoiled food is gone and a clean delivery is on the shelf. The cook plates one sample for the owner before the door is unlocked. Someone stays late to smell the cooler, because a warm cooler would mean the new food is at risk too. Unlocking the door while the old crates are still inside would invite the same complaint by morning. Recovery is that reopening: clean service, a business check, and a watch for the cause to return. The earlier cleanup removed the bad crates. This phase is the meal service that follows.',
        'r4': "Pick a backup or image from before the harm, and confirm it does not contain the malware or the attacker's task. Restore the smallest set the business needs. Ask the business owner to check a real sample before you call the service back. Reconnect only after that check. Watch the same logs for the old names and accounts. Do not re-enable a disabled account by habit. Record what recent work was lost.",
        'exam': 'Recovery restores clean service after eradication and then watches for a return. It is not the account disable, the host isolation, or the domain block. Those are containment, and they do not delete malware. It is not the deletion of malware, the patch, or the secret reset. Those are eradication, done after evidence is preserved. A backup from after the encryption started is not a clean point. The business owner accepts the service. A restore with no watch is unfinished.',
    },
    'Lessons learned': {
        'body': 'Lessons learned is the last NIST incident phase. It happens after the service is back. The meeting names a change you will actually make, and it names the person who owns that change. A shrug, a slide with no owner, or a promise to be more careful is not this phase. Root-cause analysis may feed the meeting by explaining why the incident was possible. Incident training may carry the result out to the staff later. The phase itself is the decision and the named owner.\n\nYou hold the review soon enough that people remember, and late enough that the restored service is stable. You walk the timeline across preparation, detection and analysis, containment, eradication, and recovery. You ask which of those phases lacked a tool, a contact, or a practiced step. Each gap becomes a task with a name and a date. You update the written plan so the next incident meets a better ready state. You do not turn the meeting into blame, and you do not skip it because the outage is already over.\n\nOwen Chen facilitates the review for a warehouse company two days after the shipping app returned. The timeline shows a backup existed but had not been restored in a year, so the team lost hours proving it would work. The review adds a backup restore test to the monthly checklist. Dana Ibekwe in operations owns the test, and the first due date is the first Monday of next month. Owen writes that sentence into the ticket and into the operations calendar. The old poster that told staff to call IT, without a number, comes off the wall.\n\nA defender checks that the action happened, not only that it was said aloud. At the next monthly meeting Owen asks Dana for the result of the restore test. If the test slipped, the lesson is not yet in the work. He also sends the new on-call number to the shipping desk so the ready state matches the paper. The phase fails when the document changes and the behavior does not. A calendar entry with a name is the smallest proof that the meeting did something.\n\nStudents confuse this phase with preparation, because both mention plans. Preparation is what you built before this incident. This phase is what you change because of this incident. They also confuse it with root-cause analysis. That analysis asks why the incident was possible. This phase assigns the fix to a person. A meeting with findings and no owner is only a conversation.',
        'r0': 'Lessons learned is the final NIST incident phase, and it starts after recovery. The output is a change plus an owner, not a mood. For the warehouse shipping incident, the gap was a backup that had not been test-restored in twelve months. The review assigns Dana Ibekwe a monthly restore test, first due on the first Monday. Owen records that task in the ticket and on the operations calendar, and he removes the poster that lacked a phone number. Root-cause analysis explains why. Training rehearses the new step. This phase names who will do the change. Twelve months without a restore test is the fact that earned a named owner.',
        'r1': 'Meet after the service is back. Name one change you will really make. Name the person who owns that change. Give the change a date. Walk the whole timeline, not only the scary hour. Skip the blame. Update the written plan. Take down instructions that are wrong. Check later that the owner did the work. A meeting with no name is only talk. This is not the rehearsal beforehand. This is not the hunt for why. Check the calendar next month. A date makes the promise real.',
        'r2': "After a dinner rush goes badly, the kitchen lead waits until the doors are locked and the guests are gone. The crew names one change, such as tasting the soup before service, and they name which cook will do it every night. They write it on the station card and they look at that card next week. A speech about trying harder, with nobody's name on it, changes nothing by Friday. Lessons learned is that station card after the service is back: a real change and a named owner. Asking why the soup failed is a different conversation. Practicing the taste test beforehand is training.",
        'r4': 'Schedule the review after the service is stable and while memories are fresh. Walk each phase and write the gap you can prove. Assign one change to a named person with a date. Put that task on a calendar, not only in a slide. Update or remove any instruction that the incident showed was wrong. At the next meeting, ask whether the task was done. Do not close the review on a promise with no owner.',
        'exam': 'Lessons learned is the last NIST phase, after recovery, and it names a change plus an owner. It is not preparation, which is the ready work done before the incident. It is not root-cause analysis, which asks why the incident was possible. It is not incident training, which rehearses roles before the night. Disabling an account, isolating a host, and blocking a domain were containment, earlier, and did not delete malware. A meeting with no owner has not finished this phase.',
    },
    'Incident training': {
        'body': "Incident training teaches people their role before the night it happens. Each person learns who they call, what they are allowed to do, and what they must not touch. It is practice for the NIST phases, especially preparation and the first minutes of detection and analysis. It is not the lessons-learned meeting, which happens after an incident and assigns a change to an owner. A poster nobody has rehearsed is not practice. The drill is where you discover that the after-hours number on the wall is a year out of date.\n\nThe mechanism is a realistic exercise with a safe prop. A facilitator reads a worsening scenario and each seat says the next action. The help desk practices reporting a suspicious message without opening the attachment and without forwarding it to a personal mailbox. The on-call analyst practices saying which host would be isolated, and practices saying that files will not be deleted. You time how long it takes to find the contact list. Afterward you fix the plan where people hesitated. Slides alone do not reveal a wrong phone number, because nobody has to dial it.\n\nEvan Silva runs the quarterly drill for a help desk of twelve. The scenario is a payroll clerk who received a message asking her to approve a password reset she did not request. The desk must report it to the on-call number printed on their card and must not click the link. During the drill, two analysts cannot find the number. Evan stops, has them open the binder, and writes the gap: the card on the west wall still shows last year's number. He replaces that card the same afternoon. The finding is useful because nothing was actually compromised.\n\nA defender treats a failed drill as a successful finding. Evan does not grade people for punishment. He updates the ready state: the number, the binder, and a one-page rule that says do not power off a machine that still needs its memory copied. He schedules the next drill on the calendar so the practice is not a one-time speech. Staff who have rehearsed are less likely to delete the only evidence while trying to be helpful. The rule about what not to touch is as important as the number to call.\n\nThe trap is to call this rehearsal lessons learned, or to call a single announcement complete. Lessons learned names a post-incident change with an owner, after the service is back. This practice happens before the night. Root-cause analysis asks why a past incident was possible. Training asks whether the next person will take the right first step. If the desk has never practiced, the plan is still unread, however neat the document looks.",
        'r0': "Incident training rehearses roles before the night, including who to call and what not to touch. Evan's quarterly drill uses a fake password-reset message. Two of twelve analysts cannot find the on-call number, and the west-wall card still shows last year's digits. He replaces the card that afternoon and adds a one-page rule: do not power off a host whose memory still needs capture. This is not the NIST lessons-learned phase, which assigns a post-incident change after recovery. It feeds preparation. It does not isolate a real host, disable a real account, or delete malware. Twelve people on the desk is the whole audience for this drill, and two of them missed the number.",
        'r1': 'Practice the role before the night arrives. Teach each person who they call. Teach what they must not touch. Use a fake message, not a real attack. Time how long the phone number takes to find. Fix the card that shows an old number. Say not to turn the computer off. Say not to delete strange files. Do not punish a missed step in a drill. Schedule the next practice. A slide show is not a rehearsal. This is not the meeting after recovery.',
        'r2': 'A school holds a fire drill on a sunny morning, when nothing is burning. Students walk the route, and a teacher notices that one stairwell sign points the wrong way. The sign is replaced before any real smoke. Nobody grades the children as failures for the bad sign. The drill did its job by revealing it. Incident training is that sunny-morning drill for an incident: who you call, and what you do not touch, practiced before the night. The meeting after a real fire, which assigns a repair to a named person, is a different phase. The drill is how the plan gets read.',
        'r4': 'Run a drill with a safe prop, such as a fake suspicious message. Ask each role to say the next action out loud. Time the search for the on-call number. Note every hesitation and every outdated card. Replace wrong numbers the same day. State clearly what people must not do: do not click, do not power off a machine that still holds memory, and do not delete files. Schedule the next drill. Do not treat a slide deck as finished practice.',
        'exam': 'Incident training is rehearsal before the night: who to call, and what not to touch. It is not lessons learned, which names a change and an owner after recovery. It is not root-cause analysis, which asks why a past incident was possible. A drill may reveal a bad contact card, and fixing that card supports preparation. The drill does not itself contain, eradicate, or recover a live incident. Disabling an account and isolating a host remain containment if a real case starts. Slides with no practice leave the plan unread.',
    },
    'Root-cause analysis': {
        'body': "Root-cause analysis looks for why the incident was possible, not only which file was bad. The bad file is evidence of what arrived. The cause is the condition that let an attacker turn that file into harm. You can delete malware during eradication and still leave the cause in place for the next visitor. This inquiry often feeds lessons learned, but it is not the same step as assigning a monthly task to a named owner. It is the question behind the task. Stopping at the file name feels complete and is not.\n\nYou keep asking why until you reach a condition you can change. A service account had standing administrator rights. That account had no second factor. A vendor installer requested those rights, and nobody removed them after the install finished. The malware then ran as that account. Removing the binary does not remove the standing rights. You write the chain in the ticket so a later reader can see which fact is the cause and which facts are only the path the attacker walked. The path matters for the timeline. The cause matters for the fix.\n\nChris Okeke reviews a hospital incident after the medical-imaging servers are back in service. The dropped file was a remote-control tool in a temporary folder. The log shows it ran as svc-backup, an account with permanent administrator membership and no multi-factor sign-in. The backup product needed that account only at night, and only on two servers, but the membership covered every server all day. Chris writes the cause as standing admin on a shared service account with a single factor. He does not write the cause as the temporary folder or as the file's name.\n\nA defender turns the cause into a control that matches it. Chris recommends removing the standing admin right, granting the backup right only for the night window and the two servers, and requiring a second factor or a managed credential that staff cannot type from memory. Eradication already deleted the tool. If svc-backup stays as it was, the next tool will work the same way. He records the recommendation so the lessons-learned owner can assign a person and a date. The recommendation without that later assignment is an insight, not yet a change in the environment.\n\nStudents stop at the malware name and call that the cause. The name is what arrived. The cause is why it could run with power. Students also confuse this work with containment. Disabling svc-backup during the incident stopped further use of that account. That disable was containment, and it did not delete the tool. Understanding why the account had standing admin is this analysis. Lessons learned is the later decision to change the standard and to name who will do it.",
        'r0': 'Root-cause analysis asks why the incident was possible, not only which file landed. In the hospital case, a remote tool ran as svc-backup, a service account with standing administrator rights on every server and no second factor. The backup job needed that power only at night and only on two servers. Deleting the tool was eradication, after evidence was kept. Disabling the account during the incident was containment. This analysis names the standing privilege as the cause. Lessons learned, the last NIST phase, still has to assign an owner to remove that standing right. Two servers at night was the real need. All-day rights on every server were the cause.',
        'r1': 'Ask why the incident was possible. Do not stop at the bad file name. Look at the account that ran it. Look at the rights that account kept all day. Ask why those rights were still there. Write the chain in the ticket. A deleted program can leave the cause behind. A disabled account only stopped the spread. The cause is the condition you can change. Hand that condition to the person who will assign a fix. The folder name is not the cause.',
        'r2': 'A clinic keeps throwing out spoiled milk and finally asks why the milk spoils. The fridge door does not latch, and the night cleaner was never shown how to close it. The milk is the mess. The latch is the reason the mess can return tomorrow. Replacing cartons without fixing the latch wastes the week. Root-cause analysis is the question about the latch, not the name of the spoiled item. Removing the bad program is a different phase, already done once evidence was saved. Naming who will fix the latch belongs to the meeting after service returns. The spoiled milk alone never explains the next morning.',
        'r4': 'Write the harmful file or task, then ask why it could run. Record the account, its rights, and how long those rights stay in place. Note missing second factors and rights that are wider than the job. Separate the path the attacker walked from the condition that made the path powerful. Do not stop when the malware name is known. Do not describe the earlier account disable as this analysis. Hand the condition you can change to the review that will name an owner.',
        'exam': 'Root-cause analysis asks why the incident was possible. The malware name is what arrived, not the cause. Standing admin on a service account with no second factor can be the cause even after the file is gone. Deleting that file is eradication, and only after evidence is preserved. Disabling the account during the incident is containment, not this analysis, and it does not delete malware. Lessons learned assigns the fix to an owner after recovery. Stopping at the file name misses the condition that will allow the next file.',
    },
    'Threat hunting': {
        'body': "Threat hunting looks for trouble that alerts have not named. The hunter starts with a hypothesis, then searches logs and host records for the pattern that hypothesis predicts. It is a deliberate look, not a wait beside the console. Detection begins when something has already raised a notice. A hunt can begin from a peer warning, a fresh report, or a gap you know your alerts miss. It is not a legal hold, and it is not a forensic image. No one has declared an incident until a hunt actually finds something worth a ticket.\n\nThe mechanism is a question you can test and also fail. You write the hypothesis in one sentence, name the log that could confirm or deny it, and set a time window. You search. You record hits and you also record the absence of hits. A hit becomes a detection, and then analysis measures how far it reaches. A miss is still a result: you note what you searched so the next hunter does not repeat a dead path. Hunting without a hypothesis is only browsing, and browsing produces stories you cannot explain to a reviewer on Friday.\n\nMara Nguyen hunts at a retail company after a peer in the same sector warns that intruders create scheduled tasks whose names look like vendor health checks. No alert has fired in her environment. She hypothesizes that a task with a health-check name, running from a user-writable folder, will show on at least one register server in the last fourteen days. She searches the endpoint task inventory. Store server REG-44 has a task called VendorHealth that runs a binary from a cashier profile's application-data folder. She opens a ticket with that task name, the host, and the exact search she ran.\n\nA defender treats the find as the start of detection and analysis, not as permission to wipe REG-44. Mara captures the task command line and the binary's hash, then asks that containment isolate that one server and disable the account that owns the task. She does not expand the search into every employee's mailbox. A hunt has a scope written in advance. Widening it without a new hypothesis turns the work into a sweep through private data that the original question never justified.\n\nThe trap is to say hunting and alerting are the same control, or to say a hunt is a chain of custody. An alert detects a pattern you already encoded in a product. A hunt looks for a pattern you have not encoded yet. Chain of custody records who held evidence later, if the find becomes a case that must be proved. A legal hold stops ordinary deletion for counsel. Hunting is the search you chose to run before any alarm.",
        'r0': "Threat hunting tests a hypothesis against logs before any alert fires. Mara's hypothesis was specific: a scheduled task with a health-check name, started from a user-writable folder, inside a fourteen-day window. The search hit REG-44, task VendorHealth, binary under a cashier profile. She records the search, the miss list, and the hit, then opens detection so analysis can measure scope. Containment may then isolate that host and disable the owning account. Those acts are not the hunt. Eradication, deleting the task after a copy, comes later. A hunt is not a legal hold and not a custody form. Fourteen days is the window she wrote down before she searched, not a window she invented after the hit.",
        'r1': 'Go look before an alarm names the problem. Start with one clear question. Pick the log that can answer it. Set a time window. Write down hits and misses. A hit starts a normal investigation. Do not wipe the machine you just found. Do not read every mailbox on a hunch. Keep the search as wide as the question. An alert waits for a known pattern. A hunt looks for a pattern you chose. Record what you searched. A miss is still a result worth keeping.',
        'r2': 'A warehouse supervisor walks the aisles before the alarm panel lights, because a neighboring warehouse described a particular kind of damaged crate. She checks only the rows where that damage would show, and she writes down the rows that were fine. One crate matches. She does not empty every shelf in the building out of curiosity. The walk had a reason and a boundary. Threat hunting is that walk: a hypothesis, a log, and a written result, done before an alert exists. Finding a crate starts the ordinary response. The walk itself is not the lock on the evidence room.',
        'r4': 'Write the hypothesis in one sentence before you search. Name the log and the time window. Save the query and the result, including a clean miss. If you find a host, record the artifact and open a ticket for detection and analysis. Ask containment to isolate and to disable accounts only after the scope is clear enough to name them. Do not image the whole company, and do not read mailboxes the hypothesis never mentioned.',
        'exam': 'Threat hunting searches for trouble alerts have not named, using a hypothesis and a log. It is not detection, which starts when a notice already exists. It is not a legal hold, which stops ordinary deletion. It is not chain of custody, which records who held evidence. A hit moves into detection and analysis. Isolating a host and disabling an account are later containment, and they do not delete malware. Deleting the task after a copy is eradication. Browsing without a hypothesis is not a hunt.',
    },
    'Legal hold': {
        'body': "A legal hold is an instruction to stop the normal deletion of records that a case may need. Counsel, or a person counsel names, issues it. The hold overrides the usual retention schedule for the people, systems, and date range it lists. Mail that would have expired on schedule stays. A nightly cleanup job must skip the held mailboxes. The hold is not a search through those mailboxes, and it is not a forensic copy of a disk. It is a pause on destruction, scoped to what the instruction names.\n\nThe mechanism is a list plus a change in daily operations. You identify the custodians, the systems, and the date the duty starts. You tell the owners of mail, file shares, chat, and backups to suspend deletion for those items. You acknowledge the hold in writing so there is proof the instruction arrived. You do not collect every mailbox just in case, and you do not leave the hold as a quiet email while staff keep emptying trash. When counsel releases the hold, ordinary deletion may resume. Until that release, the pause stands even if disk space is tight.\n\nA company receives notice of a contract dispute. Counsel tells records management to hold mail, chat, and home-folder files for three named people: the project manager, the salesperson, and the engineer who signed the design. Usual mail retention is one year. The hold keeps those three mailboxes past that year, including messages the employees would otherwise delete. The backup team marks the related backup sets so a capacity script does not expire them. Everyone else's mail follows the ordinary schedule. The hold does not authorize reading the mail. It only forbids destroying it.\n\nA defender checks the hold the way they check any other control that can fail silently. The records lead confirms the cleanup job excludes the three accounts, and tests that check by watching one message pass its ordinary expiry and remain. Staff in scope get a short notice: do not delete, and do not start a personal archive that walks the data off the held system. A hold that lives only in a lawyer's mailbox, while the server still purges on the ninety-day job, has failed in the only way that matters.\n\nStudents mix a hold with chain of custody, with preservation and e-discovery, and with hunting. Chain of custody records who had an evidence item and what they did with it. Preservation keeps a specific item unchanged once you are relying on it. E-discovery is the organized search and production of what counsel asked to see. Hunting looks for intruders with a hypothesis. A hold is the legal stop on ordinary deletion, limited to the names and systems counsel listed.",
        'r0': 'A legal hold pauses ordinary deletion for records a case may need. Counsel names three people, and mail, chat, and home folders for those people are kept past the one-year mail schedule. Backup sets for the same custodians are marked so a capacity job cannot expire them. The hold does not permit reading the mail and does not by itself produce a search export. Release comes only from counsel. A ninety-day purge that still runs on those mailboxes means the hold failed. This is not chain of custody, not a forensic image, and not a hunt. One year is the normal mail life. The hold is what stretches it for the three named people.',
        'r1': 'Stop deleting records a case may need. Wait for counsel to name the people. Include mail, files, chat, and backups. Skip those items in the nightly cleanup. Keep them past the normal schedule. Do not read the mail just because it is held. Do not grab every mailbox in the company. Tell the named people not to delete. Test that one old message remains. Lift the pause only when counsel says so. A note in a drawer is not enough. The server must stop purging too.',
        'r2': 'A lawyer sends a note that three filing drawers must not go to the shredder on the usual Friday. The office manager marks those drawers and tells the night crew to skip them. Other drawers still follow the normal schedule. Nobody reads the folders as entertainment, and nobody hauls the whole archive into a truck. The note is a pause on destruction, not a search. A legal hold is that marked drawer: named people, named systems, and a stop to ordinary deletion. Who later carries a box, and what search is allowed, are later duties with their own records. The Friday shredder is the ordinary retention job.',
        'r4': 'Read the hold and list the custodians, systems, and start date. Suspend deletion for mail, chat, files, and backups in that scope. Acknowledge the hold in writing. Tell the people in scope not to delete and not to move copies off the held system. Test that a message past its normal expiry is still there. Do not search contents unless a separate request says to. Do not release the hold because disk space is tight.',
        'exam': 'A legal hold stops ordinary deletion of records a case may need. It is scoped to the people and systems counsel named. It is not permission to read everything, and it is not a company-wide fishing trip. Chain of custody records who held an item. Preservation keeps a relied-on record from later edits. E-discovery searches and produces what was asked. Threat hunting looks for intruders. A purge job that still deletes held mail means the hold failed. Release of the hold comes from counsel, not from a full disk.',
    },
    'Chain of custody': {
        'body': "Chain of custody records who had the evidence, when they had it, and what they did, so the story of the evidence stays trustworthy. A hash can show that the bits did not change. The chain shows which people held the drive, the image, or the log export between collection and the moment someone relies on it. A perfect copy left unsigned on a desk has a broken story even when the hash still matches. Courts and internal reviewers both ask the same question: whose hands, in what order.\n\nYou start the record at the moment of collection, not the next morning. The item gets a unique label, a short description, the place and time it was taken, and the name of the person who took it. Each transfer is a line: who released it, who received it, the date and time, the purpose, and where it will sit. If someone makes a copy, the copy gets its own line and its own hash. You store the item in a locked cabinet, not in an open cube overnight. You do not browse the original out of curiosity. Work happens on a copy, and the chain says so.\n\nOn ticket 7330, investigator Renee Cho takes the disk from register server REG-44 only after memory has already been captured. She labels the drive E-7330-A, records the SHA-256 of the image she just wrote, and signs the drive into the evidence cabinet at 14:20. At 16:05 she signs it out to Aisha Diaz so a second copy can be made, and Aisha signs it back in at 17:40. The drive does not sit on a desk overnight. The form, not anyone's memory, is the record of those hands. A verbal 'I think Nora had it' is not a line.\n\nA defender audits the form the way they audit a log. Gaps are the finding. A missing overnight line, a hash with no reader name, or a drive discovered in a backpack are failures even when the case feels obvious. Renee refuses a quick look at the original once the image exists. If the form and the hash disagree, she stops and reports the break instead of quietly rewriting the paperwork to match a hoped-for story. Repairing the form after the fact is itself an event that must be visible.\n\nThe trap is to think the hash is the chain, or that making the image is the chain. Forensic acquisition creates the copy, in an order that respects how fast evidence vanishes, and a write blocker protects the original disk during that copy. The hash checks integrity. This record is the sequence of people and places. A legal hold is a different duty: it stops routine deletion of records a case may need. A hash with no names does not say who had the evidence.",
        'r0': 'Chain of custody is the record of who had the evidence, when, and what they did. On ticket 7330 drive E-7330-A is labeled, hashed with SHA-256, signed into the cabinet at 14:20, signed out to Aisha Diaz at 16:05, and signed back in at 17:40. It does not stay on a desk overnight. SHA-256 shows the bits still match. It does not name the hands. A write blocker belongs to acquisition, where it protects the original disk. The volatility order, registers and cache then memory then disk then remote logs, is also acquisition. This form is the people. Two signatures, out at 16:05 and back at 17:40, are the whole point of the afternoon.',
        'r1': 'Write down every person who held the evidence. Start the form when you collect it. Give the item a label. Note the time and the place. Sign it to the next person. Sign it back when they return it. Store it locked, not on a desk overnight. Work from a copy. Do not browse the original for curiosity. A file fingerprint is not the list of hands. If a line is missing, say so. Do not rewrite history quietly. Lock the cabinet before you leave.',
        'r2': 'An evidence locker in a clinic has a clipboard on the door. Each vial is labeled, and each person who takes a vial signs the time out and the time back. A vial left on a counter overnight breaks the story even if the liquid inside looks unchanged. The clipboard is not the lab test. It is the list of hands. Chain of custody is that clipboard for a drive or a log export: who had it, when, and what they did. A fingerprint of the contents can show the bits did not change. The clipboard is still required, because bits do not remember people.',
        'r4': 'Label the item when you collect it. Record who took it, where, and at what time. Log every handoff with both names, the purpose, and the new location. Store it locked overnight. Hash the copy and write the hash on the same form, but do not treat the hash as a substitute for the names. Work on the copy. If a gap appears, document the gap. Do not fill a missing hour from memory after the fact.',
        'exam': 'Chain of custody records who had the evidence, when, and what they did. A hash such as SHA-256 shows whether the bits changed. It does not replace the names. Acquisition makes the copy, uses a write blocker on the original disk, and follows volatility: registers and cache, memory, disk, then remote logs. A legal hold stops ordinary deletion. A hunt is a hypothesis search. Leaving a drive on a desk overnight breaks the chain even if the hash still matches. Do not call the hash the chain.',
    },
    'Forensic acquisition': {
        'body': "Forensic acquisition copies evidence in a way that preserves it, and the investigation then works from the copy. You record a hash of the copy so a later person can show the copy still matches what you took. You do not practice on the original disk. Order matters because some evidence disappears on its own. Collect the fastest-fading sources first: registers and cache, then RAM, then disk, then remote logs. A write blocker protects the original disk by refusing writes while that disk is read. Chain of custody, a separate record, will say who held the result.\n\nThe order follows how quickly each source dies. Registers and cache vanish if you wait or if you power the machine down. RAM vanishes when power is lost, so if the computer is still on you capture memory before you pull the plug. Disk remains after power is gone, but a normal boot or a casual mount can change timestamps and create files. The original disk is therefore attached only through a write blocker, or it is imaged by a method that does not alter it. Remote logs live on other systems and can be rotated away by those systems' own schedules, so you export them before retention deletes the window. You hash the image, preferably with SHA-256, and you store that hash beside the custody form.\n\nOn ticket 7330 the register server REG-44 is still powered on when the hunt hands it over for collection. The collector captures RAM to an external disk first and records the SHA-256 of that memory image. Only then is the machine shut down. The internal disk is connected through a hardware write blocker, and a bit-for-bit image is written to a clean evidence drive. The SHA-256 of the disk image is printed on the label and in the ticket. Analysts open the image on a lab machine. The original disk goes into the cabinet. Nobody mounts the original to take a quick look, because a quick look is a write waiting to happen.\n\nA defender checks completeness against the order, not against convenience. If someone imaged the disk and skipped RAM on a machine that was still on, the memory is gone and the report must say so. If the write blocker was left in the kit, the original may have been changed and you cannot honestly claim an untouched source. Remote logs are part of the set: the firewall and the authentication server may hold the only record of the connection, and they are collected before those servers rotate the lines away. Skipping them because they feel less physical is how a timeline loses its first hop.\n\nStudents grab the disk first because it is familiar. Disk comes after registers and cache and after RAM, when those still exist. A copy made by dragging files misses deleted space and can change the source. This work is a controlled image, hashed, taken in volatility order, with the original protected by a write blocker. The list of who then held that image is chain of custody, not the copy itself.",
        'r0': 'Forensic acquisition copies evidence and leaves the original protected. Volatility order is registers and cache, then RAM, then disk, then remote logs. On ticket 7330, REG-44 is still on, so RAM is captured and hashed with SHA-256 before shutdown. The disk is then imaged through a hardware write blocker, and that image is hashed with SHA-256 as well. Analysts work on the image. A drag-and-drop file copy is not this procedure. Chain of custody records who held the drive afterward. It does not choose the order. Skipping RAM on a live host is a loss you must document. The write blocker is what keeps the original disk from being changed during the read.',
        'r1': 'Copy the evidence and work on the copy. Take the fastest-fading data first. Capture processor state before you wait. Capture memory before you turn the power off. Image the disk after that. Collect logs that live on other machines before they roll away. Use a device that blocks writes to the original disk. Fingerprint the copy so you can check it later. Do not poke around on the original. A normal file copy can change the source. Say so if you skipped a step. The list of hands is a separate record.',
        'r2': 'A cook photographs a plated dish before anyone tastes it, because the steam disappears first, then the arrangement on the plate, and the pantry log can be copied last. A glass sheet over the recipe card lets her read the card without smudging the ink. She files the photos and works from those, not from the plate that guests are about to eat. The glass sheet is what keeps the original clean. Forensic acquisition is that sequence: capture what vanishes first, then memory, then the disk, then logs stored elsewhere, while a write blocker guards the original disk. Who signs the photos out of the drawer is a separate list of hands.',
        'r4': "If the host is on, capture registers, cache, and memory before you power it off. Then image the disk through a write blocker. Export remote logs before their retention deletes them. Hash each copy and store the hash with the label. Work only on the copy. Do not boot the original disk to 'see what happens.' If memory was skipped on a live machine, write that gap in the ticket. Do not treat a drag-and-drop of visible files as a full image.",
        'exam': 'Acquisition order is registers and cache, then RAM, then disk, then remote logs. A write blocker protects the original disk from writes. Hash the image, and investigate the image. A file drag is not a forensic copy. Chain of custody is the separate record of who had the evidence. It is not the imaging step. Skipping memory on a powered-on host loses evidence you cannot recreate. Disabling an account or isolating a host is containment, not this copy. Deleting malware is eradication and waits until the copy exists.',
    },
    'Preservation and e-discovery': {
        'body': "Preservation keeps evidence unchanged. E-discovery is the organized search and production of electronic records for a case. The two travel together, and they are not the same act. Preservation is the lock on the record. E-discovery is the scoped retrieval and the handoff of what was asked. A legal hold is often what starts the lock, by stopping ordinary deletion. Chain of custody then records who handled whatever the search produced. None of that work is a hunt for intruders.\n\nKeeping a record unchanged means the file you rely on is the file that existed, not a later edit. You suspend cleanup. You stop people from tidying a mailbox. Where a copy is required, you store it with a hash and you keep the original timestamps. You do not convert every message into a new format that drops attachments unless the production rules require a format, and if you convert you keep the native file beside it. The search then uses limits counsel wrote down: which people, which keywords, and which dates. The export contains that set. It does not contain every mailbox in the company just because a search box made a wider query easy.\n\nCounsel on a contract dispute asks for messages about the Harbor project, for three named people, from March through May. A hold is already in place so those mailboxes are not purging. The discovery lead exports only messages that match those names, the project keyword, and that date range, including attachments. The export is hashed, labeled, and placed where the custody form can track it. The lead does not open a fourth person's mailbox while the tool is already connected. An unbounded pass through every mailbox is not a proper production. It is a fishing trip, and it breaks the scope the hold and the request described.\n\nA defender checks both halves separately. For the lock, confirm the cleanup job is still skipping the held data and that the hash of the export matches what was handed over. For the search, confirm the keywords and the custodian list are the ones counsel wrote, and that the production log names who created the export and when. If an analyst added a keyword out of curiosity, the set is no longer the set that was requested. Curiosity is how a narrow duty becomes an unauthorized browse.\n\nThe trap is to treat the lock as the search, or the search as permission to alter sources. Preservation does not mean read everything. E-discovery does not mean browse until something looks useful. Forensic acquisition is the bit-level copy of a system, taken in volatility order with a write blocker on the original disk. This topic is the legal handling of records: keep them unchanged, then produce only the slice that was requested.",
        'r0': 'The lock keeps the chosen records from being edited or purged. E-discovery searches and produces only what a case requested. Counsel asks for Harbor-project mail for three named people, March through May, attachments included. The export is hashed and logged. A fourth mailbox is out of scope even if the tool can open it. A legal hold is the earlier pause on deletion. Chain of custody records who held the export. Acquisition, with a write blocker and the order registers, cache, RAM, disk, then remote logs, is a different copy. Adding a keyword the request never named breaks the production. March through May is the whole window, not an open calendar.',
        'r1': 'Keep the records from being changed. Stop cleanup on what must stay. Search only the people you were given. Search only the dates you were given. Include the attachments if they were requested. Hash the export before you hand it over. Write who made the export and when. Do not open an extra mailbox. Do not add a keyword out of curiosity. A wide search is not more thorough. It is a different job than the one you were given. The pause on deletion is a related duty, not this search.',
        'r2': 'A clinic keeps a locked cabinet of old charts, and a clerk pulls only the three folders a hearing asked for, from one season, not the whole alphabet. She copies those folders, notes the time, and leaves every other chart untouched. Opening a fourth chart because the key is already in her hand would be a different errand. The lock keeps the paper from being rewritten. The pull is the narrow handoff. Preservation is that lock on electronic records. E-discovery is the scoped pull and the production of only what was requested. Who signs the folder out is a further record of hands.',
        'r4': 'Confirm the hold or other lock is still preventing deletion. Export only the custodians, keywords, and dates counsel named. Keep native files if a converted copy would drop attachments. Hash the export and label it. Log who produced it and when. Do not add custodians or keywords for curiosity. Do not edit timestamps or tidy mailboxes before the copy. Hand the set over under a custody record.',
        'exam': 'The lock means those records stay as they were. E-discovery is the scoped search and production counsel requested. A legal hold only stops ordinary deletion. It is not the search. A custody form names who held that export. Acquisition images a system in volatility order and uses a write blocker. Opening every mailbox is a fishing trip, not a proper production. Adding a keyword the request never named changes the set. Do not treat the lock as permission to read everything.',
    },
    'Firewall logs': {
        'body': "Firewall logs show what the firewall allowed or denied. Each line names the decision and the parties to it: source, destination, port, time, and the action. They are often the first place you see a scan or a blocked path, because the firewall sits on a route the packet had to cross. They do not show which program on the computer created the packet. They do not show what an application did after a connection was allowed. Those facts live in endpoint logs and in application logs. Reading a deny as if it were a full story of the host is how investigations stall.\n\nYou read a deny differently from an allow. A deny is a path that failed at this device. It can be an attack that was stopped, a misconfigured host, or a scan. An allow is a path that passed this device. An allow is not a blessing on the payload that followed. You group lines by source. One internal computer trying many destination ports on a database it should never touch is a different story from a single refused packet. You note the time and whether the same source appears on other devices. The log is a decision record. It is not the packet contents, and it is not a proof that the destination was changed.\n\nDana Ibekwe reviews the nightly deny summary for a clinic and stops on a cluster at 02:17. Host 10.4.12.18, a front-desk computer, attempted connections to 10.4.1.20, the patient-record database, on many ports, including 1433. The action on each line is deny. That computer has no job that speaks to the database on any port. Dana opens ticket 4410 with the source, the destination, the time, and the fact that the firewall refused the path. She does not write that the database was breached. The lines show attempts that were blocked at the firewall, nothing more.\n\nA defender uses the line to aim the next question, not to close the case. Dana checks whether 10.4.12.18 also has allowed connections to other internal servers in the same hour, and she asks what process was running on that computer at 02:17. If the endpoint record shows a new binary, the firewall line was early smoke. If she only adds a tighter rule and never looks at the computer, the process remains. She also avoids the opposite mistake. A deny is not, by itself, a reason to wipe a server that the packets never reached.\n\nThe trap is to read these lines as application logs or as endpoint logs. Application logs show the app's own events, such as a user exporting a report. Endpoint logs show processes and files on one computer. A firewall deny does not name the process, and an allow does not name a database statement. Intrusion-detection logs, a neighbor record, store a pattern the sensor matched. A firewall line stores a permit or a refusal. Blocking a hostile domain during an incident is containment. It is not the same thing as reading this log, and it does not delete malware.",
        'r0': 'Firewall logs record allows and denies: source, destination, port, time, and action. At 02:17, host 10.4.12.18 tried many ports on database 10.4.1.20, including port 1433, and every line says deny. Ticket 4410 records a refused path, not a breach. The front-desk computer should never reach that database. Ask the endpoint record which process ran at 02:17. An allow would not prove the payload was safe. These lines are not application exports and not process starts. A domain block during an incident is containment, a response, not a substitute for reading the deny. The cluster is many destination ports, not a single mistake.',
        'r1': 'Read who was allowed and who was refused. Note the source, the destination, and the time. A refused path is not a break-in by itself. An allowed path is not a clean bill of health. Group many tries from one computer. Some computers should never call a database. Those tries deserve a closer look. Ask what program on that computer made the tries. Do not wipe a server the packets never reached. Do not expect this log to name a report the app exported. The decision at the door is all this record holds.',
        'r2': 'A building guard writes who was turned away at the door and who was waved through, with the time and which entrance. The book does not say what the visitor did after sitting down inside, and it does not name the tool in a backpack that never crossed the threshold. A page of refusals at one door is a reason to ask about that visitor, not a reason to remodel the offices upstairs. Firewall logs are that door book: allowed or refused, from where, to where, and when. What a program did on a computer, or what an application did with a file, is written in other diaries. The door book only records the door.',
        'r4': "Filter for denies and allows around the time you care about. Write the source, destination, port, action, and time into the ticket. Group repeated tries from one internal host. Compare that host's job with the destinations it touched. Check allows to other internal systems in the same window. Ask for the endpoint record of the process, and do not invent one. Do not call a deny a successful breach. Do not wipe a system the refused packets never reached.",
        'exam': 'Firewall logs show what was allowed or denied, including source, destination, port, and time. They do not name the process, and they do not name an application export. Endpoint logs show programs and files on one computer. Application logs show what the app itself did. A deny is a refused path, not proof the destination was changed. An allow is not proof the payload was harmless. Blocking a hostile domain is containment, not a log, and it does not delete malware. A scan from one internal host is a question, not a finished case.',
    },
    'Application logs': {
        'body': "Application logs are the diary the application itself writes. They show events inside the product: a sign-in to the app, an error, a transaction, or an export. They sit above the operating system and beside the network. A firewall log can say a connection was allowed. An endpoint log can say a process ran on a laptop. Only this diary can say that a particular user ran a particular report inside this product. If the app does not record an action, that action will not appear here, however complete the firewall record is.\n\nYou learn the fields the product actually emits before you trust a gap. A useful line has a timestamp, the application account, the action, the object, and a success or failure. You compare the action with the person's job. An export of every customer, by an account that has never exported anything, at an hour when the office is closed, is a business anomaly even when the sign-in itself succeeded. You also learn the silences. Some products log failures and skip successes. Some let an administrator turn history off. An empty stretch can mean a quiet night or a diary that was disabled at 02:00 and never turned back on.\n\nThe billing product Ledger writes one line for each report. At 03:14 it records that account m.hale exported all-customers, forty thousand rows, successfully, from a session that signed in at 03:11. That same account shows no export in the prior year. The firewall allowed the session because the billing server accepts the clinic network. The laptop's endpoint record may show only a browser, which is ordinary for this user. The strange fact is inside Ledger: the export itself, the row count, and the hour. Analyst Ken Adeyemi opens ticket 4688 and attaches that line rather than a theory about the network.\n\nA defender treats the line as a question about the account and the data. Ken asks whether m.hale was on shift, whether the account is shared, and whether the export landed on the laptop or on a remote address the product also recorded. He does not close the ticket because the firewall shows only allows. He also does not assume the operating-system security log will repeat the export. That log may show a laptop sign-in and never mention a report name. If Ledger's history was turned off last week, the absence of lines is itself the finding, and he writes the disabled setting into the ticket.\n\nThe trap is to swap this diary for the firewall or for the endpoint. Firewall logs answer who was permitted or refused on the network. Endpoint logs answer what one computer ran. These lines answer what the app did for a user. A successful sign-in to Ledger is not the same event as a successful sign-in to the laptop. If you investigate only the network deny list, you will never see the 03:14 export, because nothing at the firewall was refused. Containment may later disable the account. That disable is not a property of the log, and it does not delete malware.",
        'r0': 'Application logs record what the app did: sign-ins, errors, and transactions. Ledger shows account m.hale exporting all-customers, forty thousand rows, at 03:14, after a sign-in at 03:11. The account had no export in the prior year. Ticket 4688 starts from that line. The firewall allow only proves the network path was open. The laptop process list may show a browser and still miss the report name. Operating-system security logs may show a machine sign-in and not the export. Disabling m.hale, if analysis justifies it, is containment. It is not something the log itself performs. Forty thousand rows is the size that makes the line matter.',
        'r1': "Read the diary the application writes. Look for sign-ins, errors, and exports. Compare the action with the person's normal job. A huge export at a closed hour deserves a ticket. Note the account, the time, and the size. Do not expect the door log to name the report. Do not expect the computer log to name it either. An empty diary may mean logging was turned off. Ask whether the account is shared. Ask where the file went. A successful sign-in is not the whole story.",
        'r2': "A shop keeps the register tape, not just the door tally. The tape says who rang a sale, which item left, and whether the drawer opened at a strange hour. The door tally can say a person walked in. It cannot say they printed the entire price book at closing. A quiet tape can also mean someone unplugged the register, which is its own problem. Application logs are that register tape: the app's own record of sign-ins, errors, and transactions. The network door and the single computer's notebook answer different questions. The tape is where an odd export becomes visible.",
        'r4': "Find the application's own history for the account and the hour. Write the action, the object, the result, and the timestamp into the ticket. Compare the action with that account's normal work. Note row counts or amounts when the product records them. Check whether logging was disabled. Do not stop at a firewall allow. Do not assume the operating-system sign-in log repeats the business action. Ask where the exported data went before you close the question.",
        'exam': 'Application logs show what the app did: sign-ins, errors, and transactions such as a report export. They are not firewall allows and denies. They are not endpoint process and file events. A successful app sign-in is not the same fact as an operating-system sign-in. An empty log can mean history was turned off. Disabling the account that exported data is containment, done later, and it does not delete malware. Investigating only network refuses will miss an export that was allowed. The row count and the hour belong in the ticket.',
    },
    'Endpoint logs': {
        'body': "Endpoint logs show what one computer did: processes, file changes, and sign-ins on that machine. They are local. A firewall log can show that the computer tried a network path. An application log can show that a server-side product performed a transaction. Neither one shows that a binary started from a user's download folder on this laptop. These records, whether the operating system wrote them or an added agent did, are how you see that local sequence. The host name belongs in every note so one laptop is not blended with its neighbor.\n\nYou read them as a timeline on a single machine. A process start names the program, the folder it came from, the parent that launched it, and the user. A file event names what was created or renamed. A sign-in names the account and how the logon happened. The useful question is order. A new process from a download folder, followed by a burst of renamed files, is one story. The same program name in a vendor folder, started by an installer you expect, is a different story. You keep both the path and the parent. A name alone, without a folder, is how a normal tool gets blamed for a downloaded copy.\n\nOn laptop FIN-12, user r.cho, the record shows a process start at 13:02. The path is that user's Downloads folder and the file name is invoice.exe. The parent process is the mail program. At 13:04 the same record shows a burst of renames in the local documents folder. A few minutes later the firewall denies that laptop's attempts to reach other internal servers. Ken Adeyemi writes both times into ticket 4722. The process line is why the firewall refuses make sense. Without it, the refuses are only a noisy computer with no program attached.\n\nA defender preserves those lines before anyone rebuilds the laptop. Ken exports the process and file events for the hour around 13:02 and stores the export with the ticket. Containment can isolate FIN-12 and disable r.cho without deleting invoice.exe. Eradication, later and only after a copy exists, removes the program. If the agent was silent because someone had excluded the Downloads folder from collection, the gap itself is a finding he writes down. He does not invent a path the record does not contain, and he does not power the laptop off while memory of the running process may still matter.\n\nThe trap is to treat these lines as firewall logs or as application logs. A deny does not name invoice.exe. A billing export on a server does not prove what ran on the laptop. Operating-system security logs, a close neighbor, emphasize authentication and privilege changes. A process record emphasizes the program and the file. If you only read the firewall, you will know the laptop was refused and you will not know which file was launched. Isolating the laptop is containment. It is not a kind of log, and it does not delete the program.",
        'r0': "Endpoint logs record processes, files, and sign-ins on one computer. On FIN-12 at 13:02, invoice.exe starts from r.cho's Downloads folder, parented by the mail program. At 13:04 local documents are renamed. Ticket 4722 keeps both times. Firewall denies a few minutes later do not name that file. A server application's export log does not either. Export the hour before any rebuild. Containment may isolate the laptop and disable r.cho. That response does not delete invoice.exe. Eradication deletes it only after a copy exists. Operating-system security logs focus on authentication and rights, not this process path. The two events sit two minutes apart, and that order is the point.",
        'r1': "Look at what one computer actually ran. Note the program and the folder it came from. Note which program started it. Note the user and the time. Watch for file renames just afterward. Save those lines before anyone rebuilds the machine. Leave the power on if memory still matters. Do not invent a path you did not see. A refused network try does not name the program. A server report does not name it either. The download folder is a different story from a vendor folder. Write the computer's name on every note.",
        'r2': "Each workbench in a shop keeps its own notebook of which tool was picked up, from which drawer, and what was scratched next. The front door's book only says someone tried a locked gate later. The stock room's tape says a report was printed on a different machine. None of those pages names the tool on this bench. Endpoint logs are that bench notebook: processes, files, and sign-ins on one computer. A program launched from a personal drawer, just before files change, is the local story. The door book and the stock-room tape remain different records. The bench notebook stays with that one bench.",
        'r4': "Open the process, file, and sign-in history for the named computer only. Write the program path, the parent program, the user, and the time. Note file renames that follow. Export that window before anyone rebuilds the machine. Do not power it off if memory of the running program still matters. Do not invent a path the record lacks. Do not treat a firewall deny as the name of the program. Separate this timeline from a server application's own diary.",
        'exam': 'Endpoint logs show processes, files, and sign-ins on one computer. A program starting from a download folder, then file renames, is this record. Firewall logs show allows and denies and do not name the program. Application logs show what a product did, such as an export, and do not prove what the laptop launched. Operating-system security logs emphasize authentication and privilege. Isolating the host and disabling the account are containment. They do not delete the program. Eradication removes malware only after a copy is preserved.',
    },
    'Operating-system security logs': {
        'body': "An operating-system security log is the computer's record of authentication, privilege use, and security-policy changes. It shows who signed in, whether the sign-in worked, and who changed a right. Windows keeps it in the Security log. Linux writes it from the login path and from sshd. It is not an application's diary of business actions, and it is not a firewall decision to allow or deny.\n\nOn Windows, event 4624 is a successful logon and event 4625 is a failed logon. The record includes a logon type: 2 for the console, 3 for a network logon, 10 for a remote interactive session such as Remote Desktop, and 5 for a service. Event 4672 means that logon received special privileges. Event 4719 means the audit policy changed, and event 1102 means the Security log was cleared. Linux authentication messages are read with journalctl or in /var/log/auth.log. Failed password records the failure. Accepted password or Accepted publickey records the success.\n\nPriya, on the clinic night shift, filters the payroll server at 02:14. She finds forty-one 4625 events for svc-payroll from 10.8.4.19, logon type 3, then one 4624 for the same account and source. That address is a reception PC, and the account is supposed to run only as a service on the payroll host. She copies the UTC time, account, source, logon type, and both event identifiers into the ticket and does not clear the log. The same week, journalctl on a Linux jump host shows twelve Failed password lines for root and no Accepted line, so she records a failed attempt rather than a completed intrusion.\n\nForward the Security log and the Linux authentication records before local retention overwrites them. Alert on a run of 4625 events for one account, on a 4624 from a desk that should never use that account, on an unexpected 4672, and on 4719 or 1102. Copy the lines before disabling an account, because disabling a stolen account is containment and should follow preserved evidence. A single 4625 from a mistyped password is not an incident. A 4624 does not say which files were opened afterward. Process starts and file detail belong to endpoint logs.\n\nThe neighboring traps are the application log, the firewall log, and the intrusion-detection log. An application log records an export or an error inside the product. A firewall log records allow or deny. An intrusion sensor records a pattern it matched, not a logon success. Network metadata names who talked to whom and omits whether a password was accepted. Remember the pair: 4624 is a successful logon, and 4625 is a failed logon. Linux authentication evidence lives in journalctl or in /var/log/auth.log.",
        'r0': 'The Windows Security log separates a successful logon from a failed one. Event 4624 records success. Event 4625 records failure. Read the logon type beside that identifier: 2 is the console, 3 is a network logon, 5 is a service, and 10 is a remote interactive session such as Remote Desktop. Event 4672 means special privileges were assigned to the new logon. Event 4719 means audit policy changed. Event 1102 means the Security log was cleared. On Linux, journalctl and /var/log/auth.log carry Failed password for a failure and Accepted password or Accepted publickey for a success. Copy a burst of failures followed by a success from an unexpected source. One isolated failure is usually a mistype. Preserve those lines before anyone disables the account.',
        'r1': 'The computer writes a note when someone signs in.\nA success and a failure are different notes.\nRead the account, the source, and the time.\nRead whether the sign-in came across the network.\nA service account should not sign in from a front desk.\nMany failures and then one success deserve a ticket.\nOne mistyped password usually does not.\nLinux keeps these notes in the system journal.\nSome hosts also keep a file called auth.log.\nCopy the lines before anyone clears them.\nDo not close the account until those lines are saved.\nThis note does not list every file that was opened.',
        'r2': 'A clinic keeps a paper book at the staff door. Each line holds a name, a time, and whether the door actually opened. A refused try is still written down, because the refusal is part of the story. The book does not record the talk inside the exam room, and it does not record which supply cabinet a guard locked later. Many refusals and then one entry for a payroll name at the front door are a page the supervisor copies before anyone tears it out. A single smudged try by a tired nurse is a different kind of page. That door book is the operating-system security log of sign-ins, extra rights, and changes to the rules. A refused try and a completed entry are different lines, just as a failed sign-in and a successful sign-in are different events.',
        'r4': 'Open the Windows Security log and separate event 4624 from event 4625. Write down the time, the account, the source address, and the logon type. On Linux, read journalctl or /var/log/auth.log and contrast Failed password with Accepted password. Copy those lines into the ticket. Leave the log in place. Do not disable the account until the copy is stored. Do not treat one mistyped password as a completed intrusion. Do not look in this log for the list of files that were opened.',
        'exam': 'Do not swap the Windows identifiers. Event 4624 is a successful logon, and event 4625 is a failed logon. An application log describes what the product did. A firewall log states allow or deny. An intrusion-detection log states a pattern a sensor matched. Network metadata does not show whether a password was accepted. Linux authentication records are read with journalctl or from /var/log/auth.log. Disabling the account is containment and comes after the lines are saved.',
    },
    'Intrusion-detection logs': {
        'body': "An intrusion-detection log is the note a sensor writes when traffic or a host event matches a signature or an anomaly rule. The note names the pattern the sensor believed it saw, plus the addresses, the time, and the action taken. That note is not, by itself, proof that a host was taken over. A sign-in success or failure belongs in the operating-system security log instead.\n\nA signature is a named pattern with an identifier and a priority. The line carries the source, the destination, the ports, and an action. A sensor that only detects will alert while the packets continue. An inline prevention sensor can drop the matching packets and should record the drop. A sensor fed by a span port or a tap sees a copy and cannot stop the original packets. A short snippet stored with the alert is not a full capture of the bytes on the wire. The alert is also not the firewall's separate policy decision to allow or deny a port.\n\nJordan reviews the clinic Suricata log after breakfast. One line names a SQL pattern aimed at the public website, destination port 443, action allowed, because this sensor sits on a tap. The source address is the vulnerability scanner scheduled at 01:00, so Jordan records the signature identifier and marks that line as an expected test. A second line in the same hour names a similar pattern from an address that is not the scanner, and the action is still allowed. Jordan copies only the second line into a ticket. The sensor observed the pattern and did not block it. The website application log is the next place to check.\n\nRead the action field before briefing anyone. Allowed, on a tap, means the pattern was observed. Dropped, on an inline sensor, means that packet was stopped, which is not the same as repairing a host that already changed. Keep a written exception for the scanner address instead of disabling the signature for every source on the internet. If a rule fires all day on harmless traffic, raise the threshold and keep a sample. Deleting the rule only to quiet a chart throws away the next real match. Align the sensor clock with the servers. Silence from a sensor that has stopped writing is its own problem.\n\nStudents treat every match as a breach, or they describe the match as a firewall deny. A firewall can refuse a port even when no attack signature matched. A packet capture holds the bytes on the wire. Flow metadata names who talked to whom and leaves the payload out. Event 4624 in the Windows Security log means a successful logon, and event 4625 means a failed logon. Those identifiers are not what this sensor decides. An intrusion-detection log holds the pattern name and whether the sensor only saw the traffic or also blocked it.",
        'r0': "A signature match records an identifier, a priority, the source and destination, the ports, and an action. On a tap or span, the action stays an alert because the sensor sees a copy and cannot drop the original, as with a SQL pattern aimed at port 443 that is only observed. An inline prevention sensor can record a drop instead. Separate the clinic scanner's scheduled 01:00 hits from a match whose source is not that scanner. A snippet stored with the alert is not a full packet capture. A firewall deny can exist with no signature match at all. The pattern name plus the action is the fact this log contributes.",
        'r1': "A sensor writes a note when traffic matches a known pattern.\nThe note names the pattern and the two addresses.\nAn inline sensor may stop the matching packets.\nA copy of the traffic cannot stop the original.\nA test scanner will trip patterns on purpose.\nMark those test hits so they are not a new case.\nA match is not proof the machine was taken over.\nA match is not the door's allow or refuse decision.\nOpen the application diary when the site was the target.\nKeep the sensor clock in step with the servers.\nSilence from the sensor is a problem of its own.",
        'r2': "A warehouse hangs a sensor over the loading bay that rings only when a shape matches a posted picture of a pry bar. The bell names the picture and the bay. It does not play back every word spoken on the dock, and it does not decide which trucks the guard's clipboard already refused. A night drill with a cardboard pry bar rings the same bell, and the supervisor marks that ring as practice. A second ring, from a truck that was not in the drill, is copied onto a form before anyone claims the warehouse was emptied. The bay sensor's bell is an intrusion-detection log. It records the pattern that matched and whether anyone actually stopped the truck, not the goods that were or were not stolen.",
        'r4': 'Open the sensor log and write down the pattern name, the identifier, both addresses, the ports, and the action. Note whether the sensor sits on a tap or inline. Mark hits that come from the known scanner address and its scheduled window. Copy any other match into the ticket before you tune the rule. Do not turn the signature off for every source to make a chart look quiet. Do not call an alert a blocked attack unless the action says the packets were dropped. Check the sensor clock.',
        'exam': 'A signature hit is not automatically a breach. The action field separates a pattern that was only seen from packets the sensor dropped. A tap or span can see traffic and still be unable to stop it. Do not call this log a firewall deny, because a policy can refuse a port with no attack signature in play. Do not call it a packet capture, which holds the bytes, or a flow record, which omits the payload. Do not confuse it with Windows 4624 or 4625, which record logon success and logon failure.',
    },
    'Network logs and metadata': {
        'body': 'Network logs and metadata describe a conversation without the words. They name who talked to whom, when the talk happened, which ports were used, and how many packets and bytes moved. NetFlow and IPFIX are the usual exports from routers and switches, and a cloud flow log is the same idea. The record does not contain the payload. A packet capture is the bytes on the wire. A firewall log is the allow or deny decision. Those three artifacts answer different questions.\n\nA flow record is built from packet headers and counters, then exported on a timer or when the conversation ends. You learn the addresses, the ports, the protocol, the start and end, the interfaces, and the byte counts. You cannot read a password, a command, or a file from that record. Domain-name logs are metadata of another sort: they show which names a host asked for, still without the page that came back. A proxy log may add the address that was requested and still omit the body. None of these replaces the packets themselves when the question is what was said.\n\nOn a Tuesday review, Luis sorts flow records for the nursing subnet. Host 10.4.8.22 opens a session to 185.22.14.9 on port 443 every hour, about 200 bytes out and 40 bytes back, and it has done so for ninety days. No other clinic host uses that address. Luis cannot see a command, because the flow has no payload. He can see the pair, the hourly regularity, and the tiny size, which is enough to open a question. He writes both addresses, the port, the byte counts, the interval, and the first-seen date on the ticket, and he asks for a short capture rather than inventing the contents.\n\nKeep flow longer than full packets, because the summary is small enough to store for months. Baseline new destinations and oddly regular intervals. Use the metadata to decide where a capture is worth the disk and the sensitivity. Do not claim a flow proves what was said. Do not ignore a flow because no firewall denied it. An allowed beacon is still a conversation. Check that the exporter is still sending, since a gap can look like a quiet network when the network was merely unobserved. Open the firewall log when you need the allow or deny. Take a capture, under an evidence rule, when you need the bytes.\n\nStudents call a flow record a packet capture, or they treat a firewall deny as if it were the metadata. A capture shows the bytes on the wire, including a secret when the session was in the clear. NetFlow or other metadata shows who talked to whom without the payload. A firewall log shows the allow or deny, and it may exist for a session the flow exporter never saw, or be missing for a session recorded on an internal link. Operating-system security logs say whether a logon worked. They do not draw this map.',
        'r0': "NetFlow and IPFIX summarize a conversation: addresses, ports, protocol, start, end, and byte counts, with no payload. Luis's nursing host 10.4.8.22 talks to 185.22.14.9 on port 443 every hour, about 200 bytes out and 40 bytes back, for ninety days. That regularity is enough to ask for a capture. It is not enough to quote a command. A firewall allow or deny is a third record and can be absent on a path the flow still counted. Domain-name logs add which names were asked for, still without the page body. Store the summary long enough to see a slow pattern.",
        'r1': 'These notes say who talked to whom.\nThey say when the talk happened.\nThey say how much was sent.\nThey do not hold the words that were sent.\nA repeated tiny talk to a new place is a question.\nYou still do not know the command.\nA door that allowed the talk is a different note.\nThe bytes themselves are a different, heavier note.\nKeep the summary long enough to see a slow pattern.\nCheck that the notes are still arriving.\nA quiet chart can mean the notes stopped.\nUse the summary to decide where a short recording is worth taking.',
        'r2': "An office building keeps a visitor slip for every room entered. The slip lists two names, the room, the hour, and how long the visit lasted. It does not record the sentence spoken at the table. A guard's separate stamp says whether the visitor was admitted at the street door, and a tape machine in one hallway can record the actual words if someone turns it on. A slip that shows the same guest in the same closet every hour for months is enough to ask a question. It is not enough to quote the guest. Those visitor slips are network metadata. They show who talked to whom and how large the talk was, while the words themselves stay out of the slip.",
        'r4': "Sort flow records for the host, the peer address, the ports, the byte counts, and the timing. Write the first-seen date and the interval on the ticket. Do not invent a payload the flow does not contain. Ask for a short capture only after the pair and the window are known. Open the firewall log when the question is allow or deny. Check that the exporter's newest record is recent. Do not delete the long flow history just because a capture now exists. Leave the capture request narrow.",
        'exam': 'Do not call a flow record a packet capture. A capture shows bytes on the wire. NetFlow or other metadata shows who talked to whom without the payload. Do not call either of those a firewall log. A firewall log shows allow or deny and may be missing on a path the flow still recorded. Byte counts and a destination do not reveal the command or the password. A quiet flow report can mean the exporter stopped, not that the network was idle.',
    },
    'Vulnerability-scan output': {
        'body': "Vulnerability-scan output is a list of possible flaws a scanner reports after it probes hosts. Each line is a candidate, not a confirmed incident. Read the port, the evidence the check printed, and whether anyone outside can reach the host before you treat the line as an emergency. A severity word is a sorting aid. It does not replace those three facts.\n\nAn unauthenticated scan sees what a stranger can see: open ports, banners, and answers to safe probes. An authenticated scan signs in and can compare installed fixes with what the vendor shipped. The evidence string matters because a banner can stay old after a backport, so version text alone can lie. A useful line names the host, the port, the finding identifier, and the proof. A critical label on a machine that is powered off in a closet is not the same situation as the same label on a site the public can reach. The scanner does not understand your business unless you verify the exposure yourself.\n\nMina reads Monday's report for the clinic. One critical line cites a web-library flaw on FIN-CLOSET-04, and that host has been powered off since the office move. The same check fires on the public website, which listens on port 443 from the internet. She tickets the public host the same day, quotes the evidence string, and notes that the closet machine can wait for the next maintenance window. A third line reports an old banner on an internal printer. The vendor has backported the fix, and the only evidence is the banner, so she marks a manual check instead of an emergency change. She does not open an incident solely because the report used the word critical.\n\nSort findings by exposure, by whether the evidence is a banner or a real check, and by the data stored on the host. Confirm likely false positives before a panic change, and confirm real findings before closing them as noise. Rescan after the change, with the same scan account, or you will not know the line is gone. Scanner output is not an intrusion-detection alert. The scan asked a question on a schedule. A sensor alert means a pattern was seen, and that pattern might be the scanner itself. Give the sensor team the scanner's address so those matches are expected. A powered-off finding and a public-site finding can share a name and still deserve different speed.\n\nStudents want every critical line to be an incident, and they want an old banner to prove the product is still vulnerable. The output stays a list of maybes until the port, the evidence, and the exposure have been read. A packet capture is the traffic, not this list. A dashboard that claims zero critical findings is not this list either, especially when the scan scope skipped a subnet. The closet host and the public site are not the same urgency just because one plugin name appears on both.",
        'r0': "Read each line for host, port, evidence, and exposure before accepting the severity word. Mina's critical web-library finding on powered-off FIN-CLOSET-04 waits, while the same check on the public site listening on port 443 is ticketed the same day. An unauthenticated scan mostly sees banners and open ports. An authenticated scan can see installed fixes. A banner that survived a backport is weak evidence. Rescan with the same account after the change. Quote the evidence string in the ticket so the proof survives. Tell the sensor team the scanner address so scheduled probes are not treated as a surprise attack.",
        'r1': 'A scan writes a list of possible problems.\nEach line is a maybe until you read the proof.\nLook at which door on the machine was checked.\nLook at whether anyone outside can reach that machine.\nA harsh word on a machine that is turned off can wait.\nThe same harsh word on the public site should not wait.\nAn old name on a sign can outlive the repair.\nSign in with the scanner when you need a deeper look.\nRun the scan again after the repair.\nDo not open a full case from the harsh word alone.\nWarn the watchers which address is your own scanner.',
        'r2': 'A building inspector walks the halls and hangs a tag wherever something might be wrong. One tag says a closet extinguisher looks outdated, on a closet whose door has been bricked shut since the remodel. Another tag names the same issue beside the street entrance that every visitor uses. A third tag complains about a sign that still shows an old date after the part behind it was already replaced. The crew does not treat the bricked closet and the street door as the same rush, and they read the tag before they order parts. Those tags are vulnerability-scan output. Each one is a possible flaw, and the location plus the proof decide the hurry.',
        'r4': 'Open the scan line and write down the host, the port, the evidence text, and whether the host is reachable from outside. Separate a powered-off or isolated host from a public service that shows the same finding. Mark banner-only hits for a manual check when a backport is plausible. Quote the evidence in the ticket. Do not open an incident from the severity word alone. Rescan after the change with the same scan account. Tell the sensor team the scanner address before the next scheduled run. Leave unrelated hosts out of the emergency change.',
        'exam': 'Scan output is a list of possible flaws, not a pile of confirmed incidents. A critical line on a host that is powered off is not the same urgency as the same line on a public site. An old banner can remain after a fix was backported, so the evidence string matters. Do not confuse this list with an intrusion alert, a packet capture, or a dashboard total. A green report can hide a subnet the scanner never touched. Rescan after the change or the line is only a hope.',
    },
    'Dashboards and automated reports': {
        'body': 'A dashboard, and the automated report mailed from it, summarizes logs and scans. Tiles, trends, and totals compress many records into something a busy team can see. Summaries are how you notice shape. They also hide gaps. When the picture looks too calm or too wild, open the underlying record instead of holding another meeting about the tile.\n\nA tile is only as honest as its feed. If a collector stopped yesterday, a green count of failed sign-ins can mean that no events arrived, not that nobody failed. If a filter drops a noisy host, the chart improves while that host stays noisy. A weekly average can bury a one-hour spike. Automated reports add a further risk: they go out on schedule even when the query is stale, and readers treat the file as a measurement. Counting the same events twice can paint a red tile that looks like an emergency. None of that is visible until someone opens the query, the last-ingest time, and one raw event.\n\nSam is about to show the Monday security page to the clinic leads. The failed-sign-in tile is green and reads zero. Before the meeting, Sam checks the collector and finds the Windows Security feed last received an event yesterday at 16:40. The zero is a broken pipe. Sam says so, pulls a direct count from a server that still holds its local Security log, and writes the collector name and the last event time on the board. A separate weekly vulnerability file claims no critical findings. Its scan scope still excludes the imaging subnet added last month. Sam does not let that file close the question. The scan configuration is the record that matters.\n\nPut a heartbeat beside every chart you are proud of. When a number will be used for a decision, compare it with a raw query. Sample one event and confirm it is the kind of event the tile claims to count. If a report looks wild, check for double collection before you page the whole company. If it looks calm, check collectors, agents, and scan scopes against the asset list. Do not brief leaders from a tile whose feed time you have not seen. Fix the feed, and keep a copy of the misleading green tile in the ticket so the gap stays visible. A summary may be short. It may not pretend that missing records were zeros.\n\nThe trap is to treat the dashboard as the log. The log is the underlying record. Vulnerability-scan output is the list of possible flaws, while a report about that scan can skip a subnet and still look finished. A packet capture is the traffic itself, not a sparkline of volume. A green tile over a collector that stopped yesterday is a broken feed, not evidence that the week was quiet.',
        'r0': "A tile counts only what arrived. Sam's green failed-sign-in total of zero sat on a Windows Security collector whose last event was yesterday at 16:40, so the zero meant a dead feed. A weekly vulnerability file claimed no critical findings while its scope skipped the imaging subnet added last month. Compare a decision number with a raw query and with one sample event. Check for double collection before treating a red spike as twice the incidents. Record the collector name and the last-ingest time beside any total you repeat to leaders. Sample one raw event before that number is repeated upstairs.",
        'r1': 'A board and a mailed report are summaries.\nThey squeeze many notes into one number or picture.\nA calm picture can mean the notes stopped arriving.\nA wild picture can mean the same notes were counted twice.\nOpen the note underneath before you announce the number.\nCheck the time of the newest note.\nCheck that the search still covers every place you care about.\nA pretty zero is not proof of a quiet week.\nKeep a copy of the misleading picture with the ticket.\nFix the feed before you trust the next mailing.\nDo not brief bosses from a number whose feed you skipped.',
        'r2': 'A clinic lobby hangs a board with one lamp for each ward. Green is supposed to mean the ward called in healthy at dawn. One morning every lamp is green, and a visitor would think the night was quiet. The cord behind the board was unplugged yesterday afternoon, so no ward could have changed a lamp. Another week the board flashes red because the same call was plugged in twice. The nurse on duty reads the ward book before repeating either story at the morning meeting. That lobby board is a dashboard or an automated report. The book underneath is the record, and a calm lamp does not fill a missing page.',
        'r4': "Check the last-ingest time before you trust a green or red tile. Open one raw event and confirm it is the kind of event the tile claims to count. Compare the tile's number with a direct query before a decision. Write the collector name and the data time on the ticket. Do not brief leaders from a tile whose feed you have not seen. Look for a dropped subnet or a doubled source if the picture looks wrong. Keep a copy of the misleading summary. Do not delete the underlying records to match the tile.",
        'exam': 'A dashboard or automated report is a summary, not the log. A green zero can mean the collector stopped, as when the last event was yesterday and the tile still looks healthy. A finished-looking vulnerability report can skip a subnet. A red spike can be the same events counted twice. Open the underlying record when the picture looks too calm or too wild. Do not confuse the tile with scan output, a packet capture, or proof that nothing happened.',
    },
    'Packet captures': {
        'body': "A packet capture is the bytes on the wire, or a faithful file of those bytes. It is the traffic itself, so a session can be reassembled and anything left unencrypted can be read. The file is heavier than a flow record, and it often contains secrets, so it is handled as evidence rather than as an ordinary log to spray across a shared folder.\n\nA capture is taken on a host interface, a span port, or a tap. A filter and a short time limit keep the file small. Headers and payloads are both present. A password, a cookie, or a transferred file can sit in the clear. There is no field labeled allow or deny. You infer a reply only if a reply appears in the bytes. An encrypted session still shows the handshake, the addresses, and the sizes, and it hides the password inside that session. A capture left on a shared drive is a weakly guarded copy of those secrets. If the file enters a case, hash it, limit who can open it, and do not mail it around for convenience.\n\nDee takes a five-minute capture on the clinic server switch, filtered to the file server, after a complaint about odd logins. The file shows a file-transfer session in the clear, with the account name and the password readable in the payload. That service was supposed to be encrypted. Dee stops the capture, hashes the file, stores it in the case folder, and writes the hash and the path on the ticket. The password is not pasted into the ticket body. Flow records for the same minute show the two addresses, the port, and the byte counts, and they do not show the password. The firewall log shows the session was allowed. Only the capture shows the secret that crossed the wire.\n\nCapture narrowly, for a stated reason, and for a short window. Use metadata first so you know which host and which minute deserve the bytes. Protect the file the way you protect the data inside it. Use the capture to confirm what a flow could only suggest, then remove it on the evidence schedule instead of keeping every span forever. If the session is encrypted end to end, do not claim you read the password. You saw sizes and a handshake. Fix the clear-text service so the next capture cannot harvest the same secret. A capture does not replace the sign-in result in the operating-system security log, and it is not the scanner's list of possible flaws.\n\nStudents call a flow record a capture because both describe traffic. A packet capture shows the bytes on the wire. NetFlow or other metadata shows who talked to whom and omits the payload. A firewall log shows the allow or deny and omits the payload as well. A capture can contain a password when the session was in the clear, which is why the file is evidence. A capture of an encrypted session does not reveal the password protected inside it. Scan totals and dashboard tiles are not the packets.",
        'r0': 'A five-minute capture on the server span showed a clear-text file-transfer login, account and password both sitting in the payload. Flow records for that same minute held addresses, a port, and byte counts, not the password. The firewall log only showed the session was allowed. Hash the file, store it with the case, and keep the secret out of the ticket body. Encrypted sessions in a capture show the handshake and sizes, not the inner password. Filter and time-box the capture so the evidence file stays small enough to protect. A span or a tap is the usual place to copy the server link without breaking it.',
        'r1': 'This file is the traffic itself.\nYou can read whatever was sent in the clear.\nA password can sit in that file.\nTreat the file as sensitive evidence.\nA short, narrow recording is safer than a huge one.\nWrite down a fingerprint of the file.\nDo not paste the secret into the ticket.\nA summary of who talked to whom will not show the password.\nAn allow note from the door will not show the password.\nAn encrypted talk hides the words and still shows the sizes.\nDo not claim you read a secret that encryption covered.\nStop the clear-text service so the next recording is less dangerous.',
        'r2': "A hallway in a clinic has a recorder that stores every sound, not a list of who walked by. Five minutes of that tape can hold a password someone spoke at the desk, or a chart number read aloud. The visitor slips at the door still only say who entered and how long they stayed. A guard's stamp says the person was let in. Neither slip nor stamp contains the spoken password. The nurse labels the tape, locks it in the case drawer, and refuses to write the password on the incident form. That tape is a packet capture. It holds the bytes from the wire, including secrets, which a list of who met whom does not hold.",
        'r4': 'Capture a short window with a filter aimed at the host and the time you already suspect. Write the interface or span, the filter, the start time, and the file hash on the ticket. Store the file where the case lives, not on a shared drive. Do not paste passwords or cookies from the payload into the ticket. Do not mail the file around. Read flow records and the firewall decision separately. Do not claim an encrypted session revealed its inner password. Leave the capture running only as long as the question requires.',
        'exam': 'A packet capture shows the bytes on the wire. Flow records name the parties and leave the payload out. A firewall log shows allow or deny and does not show that payload either. A clear-text password in the capture is evidence and a secret. Do not paste it into the ticket. An encrypted session reveals sizes and a handshake, not the password inside. Do not confuse a capture with scan output or with a dashboard sparkline.',
    },
    'Guidelines': {
        'body': "A guideline is recommended practice. It is advice, and it is not mandatory. People should not be disciplined for setting that advice aside, and the page should not be written as if they would. When leadership wants a duty enforced, the duty belongs in a policy or a standard. A page that says should in one line and threatens dismissal in the next teaches staff to ignore every line on it.\n\nAdvice helps where several safe choices exist. A passphrase guide can suggest a short sentence a person can remember, and it can suggest using a password manager. It can explain why reusing a passphrase is a bad habit. Those lines stay suggestions. The required length and the check against known-breached passwords live in the standard, which is what an audit measures. Mixing a style tip with a required setting hides which sentences actually count.\n\nThe clinic identity team publishes a one-page passphrase guide. It suggests four ordinary words and a password manager, and it points readers to the separate standard for the required length. A nurse manager asks whether someone can be written up for a different memorable pattern that still meets the standard. The answer is no, because the guide is only advice. The same week a draft adds a sentence saying failure to follow the word-choice tip is grounds for dismissal. The identity lead deletes that sentence. A real consequence belongs in the account policy, which is management's intent, not in a tip about phrasing.\n\nLabel the document as a guideline at the top. Keep mandatory settings in the standard and ordered tasks in the procedure. Review the guide whenever the standard changes, so the advice does not contradict the required setting. Do not score a department for skipping a suggestion. If a suggestion is already being enforced as a rule, either promote it into the standard and name an owner, or stop enforcing it. Optional advice that pretends to be a firing offense makes the real duties harder to see.\n\nStudents call a guideline a policy or a standard. A policy is management's intent: what must be true, who owns that intent, and the consequence of ignoring it. A standard is the required setting, such as full-disk encryption on every laptop. A procedure is the step-by-step work. A guideline is advice a person may decline without missing the required setting. A tip about passphrase style is not the page an auditor uses to record a finding.",
        'r0': "A guideline recommends and does not bind. The clinic passphrase guide may suggest four ordinary words and a password manager, while the standard, not the guide, requires fourteen characters and a breached-password check. An audit samples the standard. It does not score the optional style. If a sentence threatens discipline, it does not belong in the guide. Move a duty you intend to enforce into the standard or into the policy that states management's intent. Review the guide when that fourteen-character setting changes so the advice does not contradict it. Keep the word should in the guide and the word must in the standard.",
        'r1': 'A guide gives advice.\nThe advice is not a firing rule.\nPeople may choose another safe way.\nDo not write a punishment into a tip.\nThe measured rule lives on a different page.\nA style idea for a passphrase is only a style idea.\nMeeting the real rule is what gets checked.\nPoint readers at that real rule.\nDelete threats that were pasted under a suggestion.\nIf you want the tip enforced, move it.\nGive the moved rule an owner.\nReread the guide when the real rule changes.',
        'r2': 'A kitchen pins a card of plating ideas beside the pass. The card suggests a sprig of herb and a certain swirl of sauce. Cooks who plate a safe meal another way are not sent home. The health rule and the required holding temperature live on a different sheet, and that sheet is what the inspector measures. One afternoon someone scribbles a dismissal threat under the herb suggestion. The head cook crosses the threat out the same shift, because a tip is not a punishment. That plating card is a guideline. It is advice you may set aside, while the required setting stays on the sheet the inspector actually checks.',
        'r4': 'Read the page and mark each sentence as advice or as a real duty. Move any threat of discipline out of the guide. Point the guide at the standard that states the required setting. Write down which suggestions people are already treating as rules. Promote those into the standard with an owner, or stop enforcing them. Do not audit staff against a suggestion. Check that the guide does not contradict the required setting. Leave the step-by-step work in the procedure.',
        'exam': "A guideline is advice and is not mandatory. Do not treat it as a policy, which is management's intent, with an owner and a consequence. Do not treat it as a standard, which is the required setting an audit measures. The ordered work lives in a procedure, which is a separate document. A suggested passphrase style is not the finding an auditor writes. A dismissal threat does not belong under optional advice. If the organization will enforce the sentence, it has to move to the standard or the policy.",
    },
    'Policies': {
        'body': "A policy states management's intent. It says what must be true, names who owns that intent, and says what happens if people ignore it. Later arguments can cite the page instead of reconstructing a chat. The policy is not the measured setting, and it is not the sequence of clicks that carry the work out.\n\nIntent is kept short for a reason. An account policy can require one account per person, forbid sharing that account, and name discipline as the consequence. It names the executive owner. It does not pick a passphrase length, a cipher, or a disk-encryption product. Those required settings belong in a standard, so they can change when the technology changes without a rewrite of the intent. Portal clicks belong in a procedure. A guide may suggest a memorable way to choose a passphrase, and the policy does not pretend the suggestion is the rule.\n\nThe clinic's chief information officer signs the account policy. The signature, the owner, and the consequence for a shared account sit on the first page. When a shared nurse login appears at the imaging desk, the investigation cites that policy for the ban and for the consequence. A different document is cited for the fourteen-character minimum the shared password also missed. The skipped approval is a procedure miss, because the steps said who had to approve the new login. Three documents, three jobs. Intent came from the policy alone.\n\nKeep the set small enough that people will actually read it. Every policy needs an owner and a review date. A page nobody has signed is still a draft. A product setting buried deep in a policy goes stale and is hard to audit. When intent changes, revise the page and take the old copy out of the folder staff open. Teach the intent in plain language. Do not issue one binder that stirs advice, settings, and steps together and calls the whole binder a policy. Write only a consequence the organization is willing to apply.\n\nStudents name every security document a policy. Advice that is not mandatory is a guideline. The required setting, such as full-disk encryption on every laptop, is a standard. Ordered work that two people can follow is a procedure. Management's intent, carrying an owner and a consequence, is the policy. A signed list of product settings is still a standard. Tips on a poster do not become intent because the poster hangs near a door.",
        'r0': "Management's intent names what must be true, the owner, and the consequence. The clinic account policy, signed by the chief information officer, forbids shared accounts and names discipline. It does not contain the fourteen-character minimum. That number is a required setting in the standard. It does not list the portal clicks. Those steps are the procedure. A suggested passphrase style stays in the guideline. An unsigned page is a draft. Put a review date on the policy so intent does not quietly expire. Cite the policy for the shared-account ban and cite the standard for the length. Train people on the intent in plain words before the first incident.",
        'r1': 'A policy says what leadership wants to be true.\nIt names the person who owns that want.\nIt names what happens if people ignore it.\nIt stays short on purpose.\nIt does not pick the exact setting.\nIt does not list each click.\nA shared account can be banned here.\nThe length of a passphrase belongs elsewhere.\nThe buttons to press belong elsewhere.\nA style tip belongs elsewhere.\nNo signature means the page is still a draft.\nPut a date on it so someone rereads it.',
        'r2': "A restaurant owner frames one paragraph on the kitchen wall. The paragraph says every diner gets a safe meal, names the owner, and says a cook who serves food they know is unsafe will be dismissed. It does not state the fridge number, and it does not tell anyone which dial to turn. Those details sit on other cards. When a bad tray goes out, the investigation points at the framed paragraph for the ban and the consequence, then at the other cards for the missed temperature and the skipped check. That framed paragraph is a policy. It is management's intent, with an owner and a consequence, not the required setting and not the steps.",
        'r4': 'Read the policy for the intent, the named owner, and the consequence. Confirm a leader has signed it. Write down any measured number or click-path hiding in the prose. Move numbers into the standard and clicks into the procedure. Leave style tips in the guideline. Do not add a product setting just to make the policy look detailed. Put a review date on the page. Remove retired copies from the folder people actually open. Do not invent a harsher consequence than the signed text states.',
        'exam': "A policy is management's intent. It says what must be true, who owns it, and the consequence of ignoring it. Do not call the required setting a policy. That setting is the standard, such as a minimum length or full-disk encryption. Do not call the steps a policy. Those steps are the procedure. Do not call optional advice a policy. That advice is a guideline and is not mandatory. An unsigned page is a draft. A shared-account ban belongs in the policy even when the character count belongs in the standard.",
    },
    'Standards': {
        'body': 'A standard is the required setting that makes management\'s intent measurable. Leaders can intend that laptops be protected. The standard states the protection an auditor can check: full-disk encryption with AES-256 on every laptop, and the recovery key escrowed in the directory. A guideline cannot be checked that way, because advice is not mandatory.\n\nNumbers and named configurations belong in the standard once they are duties rather than tips. A minimum passphrase length, a required cipher, a screen-lock interval, and a logging level are standards when failing them is a real miss. The standard should point at the policy it serves, so the intent sits above the setting. It should not narrate every click. The procedure tells a technician how to turn encryption on and where to confirm the key was escrowed. Two people who follow the procedure should arrive at the setting the standard names. A local team may choose a stricter setting. It may not choose a weaker one and still claim it met the standard.\n\nClinic language says laptops must be protected. That sentence is intent. The endpoint standard says every laptop uses full-disk encryption with AES-256 and stores the recovery key in the directory, not on a slip stuck to the lid. Auditor Rhee exports the encryption report and finds four imaging laptops with encryption off. Those four fail the standard. They do not fail a passphrase guide that only suggested a memorable sentence. A separate identity standard requires fourteen characters and a rejection of known-breached passwords. A different memorable pattern is acceptable when both of those settings are met. Fourteen characters is the required setting. The pattern of words is not.\n\nIf you cannot tell whether a host complies, the sentence is not a standard yet. Change the setting when the product changes, and leave the policy\'s intent alone. Do not hide a mandatory cipher inside a guide, or the audit will miss it and staff will treat it as optional. Do not write the standard as a tour of buttons, or nobody will know which sentence is the requirement. Written exceptions need an owner and an end date. A quiet skip is not a second standard.\n\nStudents fold the standard into the neighboring documents. Management\'s intent is the policy. The required setting is the standard. The step-by-step is the procedure. Advice you may skip is the guideline. "Protect the laptops" cannot be marked pass or fail on a sample of machines. "AES-256 full-disk encryption on every laptop, key escrowed" can. The technician\'s clicks remain a procedure even when those clicks are the approved way to reach the setting.',
        'r0': "The required setting is what an audit can mark pass or fail. The clinic endpoint standard demands full-disk encryption with AES-256 on every laptop and escrow of the recovery key in the directory. A separate identity standard demands fourteen characters and a block on known-breached passwords. The policy only says to protect laptops. The procedure only tells the technician which screens reach that AES-256 setting. A local rule may be stricter. It may not drop AES-256 and still count as compliant. A sentence you cannot test is not yet a standard. Rhee's four imaging laptops with encryption off fail this setting.",
        'r1': 'A standard is the setting you must use.\nSomeone can check whether each machine meets it.\nProtect the laptops is too vague to check.\nEncryption turned on for every laptop can be checked.\nA minimum length can be checked.\nA required way of scrambling data can be checked.\nThe wish from leadership is a different page.\nThe clicks that reach the setting are a different page.\nAdvice you may skip is a different page.\nA site may add a stricter setting.\nA site may not quietly use a weaker one.\nIf you cannot test the sentence, it is not ready.',
        'r2': "The cold line in a clinic kitchen has a posted temperature the food must meet. An inspector can read the thermometer and mark the line pass or fail. A framed sentence in the office that says keep food safe cannot be marked that way, because it never says the number. A friendly card that suggests a garnish is not checked at all. The cook's card of which dial to turn is how you reach the temperature, and it is not the temperature itself. A second cafe may keep food colder. It may not run warmer and claim it met the card. That posted temperature is a standard. It is the required setting, and the intent and the steps are other papers.",
        'r4': 'Read the standard and write down the setting that can be checked on a real host. Note the policy it serves. Confirm the procedure lands on that setting rather than on a different one. Look for mandatory numbers hiding in a guide, and move them here. Sample a few machines before you call the report done. Record exceptions with an owner and an end date. Do not accept a local setting that is weaker than this one. Do not rewrite the standard as a tour of buttons. Leave intent language in the policy.',
        'exam': "A standard is the required setting. An audit can mark it pass or fail, as with AES-256 full-disk encryption on every laptop or a fourteen-character minimum. Do not call management's intent a standard. That intent is the policy. Do not call the click path a standard. That path is the procedure. Do not call optional advice a standard. A guideline is not mandatory. A vaguer sentence such as protect the laptops cannot be sampled. A local rule may be stricter and may not be weaker.",
    },
    'Procedures': {
        'body': "A procedure is the step-by-step way to do a job so two careful people reach the same safe result. It orders the work. It names who starts, who approves, what gets checked, and what gets recorded. It is not management's intent, and it is not the required setting those steps are trying to hit.\n\nPut the steps in the order they must happen, and name the role on each one. A new-hire procedure can say the manager opens the ticket, human resources confirms the start date, the identity team creates the account, only the role written on the approved ticket is granted, and the manager confirms the first sign-in. Skipping the approval is a broken procedure even if the new account works. The policy may intend individual accounts and least privilege. The standard may name the role catalog and a passphrase length. The procedure is how Tuesday's work actually happens. A friendly script for explaining passphrases can sit in a guideline. That script is not a step.\n\nOwen and Grace both handle clinic hires. Owen follows the page and refuses a request for local administrator on a nursing PC, because the approved role is clinical user. Grace works from memory and grants administrator after a supervisor asks in chat. Both accounts get created. Only Owen's result matches the procedure. The chat never replaced the approval step. A later review shows the missing approval and the extra right. The repair removes that right and reteaches the skipped step. It does not rewrite the chat into the procedure after the fact.\n\nTry the draft on someone who did not write it. If they have to guess, a step is missing. Name the record that proves the step, such as the approval saved on the ticket. Change the text when the portal changes, or people will follow ghosts. A procedure that says to grant administrator to every hire fights the standard and should be corrected rather than obeyed out of habit. Staff are expected to follow a procedure. They are not expected to treat a guideline the same way, because a guideline is advice and is not a required sequence. Keep those labels where a tired reader can see them.\n\nStudents rename the steps as a policy or a standard. Management's intent, with its owner and its consequence, is the policy. The required setting, such as the role catalog or full-disk encryption, is the standard. Optional phrasing advice is the guideline. The procedure is the ordered work. Intent alone does not say who must approve the ticket. The setting alone does not say which button to press. The steps do both, and two people who follow them should land on the same safe result.",
        'r0': 'Five ordered steps make the clinic new-hire procedure: the manager opens the ticket, human resources confirms the start date, identity creates the account, only the approved role is granted, and the manager confirms the first sign-in. Skipping approval is a miss even when the account works. The policy supplies the intent of least privilege. The standard supplies the role catalog. A chat message is not a substitute for the approval step. Test the page on a person who did not write it. If a step contradicts the standard, correct the step instead of following it. Record the proof of each step on the ticket.',
        'r1': 'A procedure is the ordered way to do the job.\nTwo careful people should get the same safe result.\nName who does each step.\nPut the steps in the order they happen.\nSay where the proof of each step is saved.\nA wish from leadership is not the steps.\nA measured setting is not the steps.\nA chat message does not replace an approval.\nTest the page on someone who did not write it.\nFix a step that fights the required setting.\nUpdate the page when the screens change.\nAdvice you may ignore is not a step.',
        'r2': 'A clinic kitchen keeps one recipe card for the evening soup. The card says who fetches the stock, who tastes before service, and which pot is used. Two cooks on different nights should finish with the same safe soup. A framed note that says feed people well does not tell them the order. A posted temperature does not tell them which ladle to pick up. A suggestion about a pretty bowl is not a step, and a cook may ignore the bowl. A shouted change in the rush does not rewrite the card. That recipe card is a procedure. It is the step-by-step, and the intent and the required setting live on other pages.',
        'r4': 'Walk the procedure from the first step to the last and write down the role on each step. Note the record that proves the step happened. Ask a person who did not write it to try the page. Mark every place they have to guess. Compare the steps with the standard and correct any step that would miss the required setting. Do not let a chat message stand in for an approval. Update the text when the screen changes. Leave intent and consequences in the policy. Leave optional tips out of the numbered steps.',
        'exam': "A procedure is the step-by-step that lets two people reach the same safe result. Do not call it management's intent. That intent is the policy. Do not call it the required setting. That setting is the standard. Optional advice belongs in a guideline, and skipping that advice is allowed. A guideline is not a required sequence. A chat approval does not replace the written approval step. If a step contradicts the standard, the step is wrong. Knowing who wants least privilege does not tell you which role the ticket may grant.",
    },
    'Regulatory and legal considerations': {
        'body': "Regulatory and legal considerations are the statutes and the regulators that apply to your data and your sector. They are duties from outside the company. Management's intent cannot waive them. Counsel decides which statutes attach to a data set. The service desk does not make that decision.\n\nA clinic that holds health records is the usual picture. A federal health-privacy rule applies, and the state where the clinic operates may add its own health statute. When the two disagree, the stricter duty wins. Federal notice to an individual has an outer bound of 60 days, and a shorter state clock replaces that bound. Retention, a patient's right to a copy, and who may receive a record can split the same way. Judge each data set on its own. Payroll, card payments, and charts do not share one statute by default. A public company may also owe financial-reporting duties that barely touch a nurse's chart and bear heavily on the ledger. Record which regime counsel named, so the next project does not start over from a blog.\n\nAde maps a new imaging archive before the clinic buys it. The archive will store diagnostic images. She writes that both the federal health rule and the state health statute apply, and that the state's access deadline is the shorter one, so the working steps will use the state deadline. She does not let the vendor contract erase a patient right. She also refuses to park the card-brand rule on this legal map. The clinic takes some card payments, and those brand rules are a contract. They are real, and they are not a statute passed by a legislature. No border crossing is required. Every server can sit in one state and the regulatory duty is still full.\n\nAsk which law applies before you design retention, sharing, or breach notice. Bring counsel in when a new kind of record appears. Do not treat the handbook as a stand-in for a statute. Put the stricter clock into the steps so night staff are not comparing statutes from memory. Separate a regulator's demand from a customer's preference. A preference can be declined. A statute cannot. Teach the clinic's actual regimes rather than a generic list that skips the state.\n\nStudents treat every outside rule as a statute, or they treat company intent as though it outranked the law. A policy is management's intent, and a standard is the required internal setting. Card-brand duties are an industry contract, not an act of a legislature. A second country's claim appears when data or staff cross a border. A clinic that never sends charts abroad still has regulatory work to do. Follow the stricter of the federal rule and the state statute.",
        'r0': 'Statutes and regulators attach by data type and sector. For clinic images, Ade records both a federal health-privacy rule and the state health statute, then follows the stricter duty. Federal notice to a person has an outer bound of 60 days, so a shorter state clock replaces it. Counsel, not the service desk, names the regime. A vendor contract cannot delete a patient right the statute gives. Card-brand rules are a contract and are not this map. No border is required for the duty to exist. Write the chosen clock into the steps before the archive is purchased. Name the counsel who signed that note.',
        'r1': 'Laws and the offices that enforce them come from outside.\nThey depend on the kind of records you hold.\nA company wish cannot cancel a law.\nAsk the lawyer which laws apply.\nA clinic can face a federal health law and a state one.\nWhen the two disagree, follow the stricter duty.\nThe people at the desk do not pick the law.\nA customer wish is not the same as a law.\nPut the stricter clock into the steps people follow.\nCard brand contracts are a different kind of duty.\nYou can have these duties without sending records abroad.\nWrite down which law was chosen and who chose it.',
        'r2': "A county code book sits in the clinic office, separate from the owner's framed wishes. One chapter is the health board's rule for how fast a patient must be given a copy of a chart. A state chapter is stricter about that same copy. The office follows the chapter that asks more, and the framed wish cannot cross that chapter out. A mall tenant handbook in the drawer is a contract with the mall, not a chapter of the code. Nothing in the book requires the chart to leave the county. That code book is the regulatory and legal duty. It is the statutes and the regulators, and the stricter chapter wins.",
        'r4': 'List the kinds of records in the new system. Ask counsel which statutes and regulators apply to each kind. Write down the stricter duty where two rules overlap. Put that clock or right into the procedure people will follow. Do not let a vendor contract delete a right the statute gives. Do not treat a card-brand contract as a statute. Do not ask the service desk to choose the law. Record who in counsel made the call. Leave border questions for a separate review if no data will leave the country.',
        'exam': 'Regulatory work is the statutes and regulators for your data and sector. Where a federal health rule and a state statute both apply, the stricter duty wins. A 60-day federal notice bound yields to a shorter state clock. Do not treat company intent as able to waive a statute. Do not call a card-brand contract a statute. That contract is an industry duty. Do not require a border crossing before a domestic health duty exists. Counsel names the regime. The service desk does not.',
    },
    'Industry and local considerations': {
        'body': "Industry and local considerations are the extra duties that come from your sector and from the place you operate. They can be stricter than a generic textbook, and they differ by city and by line of business. They sit on top of company intent. They are not a license to undercut the company standard.\n\nA merchant who takes cards shows the industry side. Beside the company policy, the merchant follows the card-brand rules. Those rules limit which card data may be stored after a payment and put controls on the systems that touch that data. The brands impose the rules by contract. A legislature did not pass them. Calling them a statute sends you hunting for the wrong enforcer. A city shows the local side. An ordinance can require notice to a city office on a clock that no national textbook prints. A hospital network or a defense customer can add contract duties the company's own policy never listed. Ask whose document it is, and what happens if you ignore it. A brand can fine a merchant or refuse the merchant. A city can enforce its ordinance. The policy owner cannot erase either one.\n\nNoor handles copay cards for the clinic. Company intent says to protect payment data. The brand rules go further and forbid keeping the card security code after authorization. A vendor ticket asks to store that code for convenience. Noor refuses and cites the brand rule, while the company encryption setting stays in force as well. The same month the city tells merchants to notify the city clerk faster than the clinic's generic poster. Noor adds that local clock to the notice steps and does not delete the state or federal clocks. The ordinance was an addition. A clinic one town over does not assume it shares that ordinance.\n\nKeep an inventory of the sector contracts and the local ordinances on each service, and name a person to read the revisions. When a brand changes a rule, compare the internal setting and the steps to the new text. A site may go stricter than the company baseline. It may not adopt a weaker local habit and label the habit a local consideration. Do not file a brand rule inside a guide. A guide is advice you may skip, and a merchant who takes cards may not skip the brand rule.\n\nStudents call the card-brand rule a law, or they call a city ordinance an internal procedure. Health statutes and their regulators are the regulatory map. Sector requirements, usually contractual, are the industry map. City and site additions are the local map. Crossing a border is a national and global question. A merchant can carry a heavy industry duty and never leave the country. Steps explain how staff comply. They do not create the brand's rule.",
        'r0': 'Card-brand rules sit on top of company intent and are imposed by contract, not by a legislature. They forbid keeping the card security code after authorization. Noor refuses a vendor request to store that code and still keeps the company encryption setting. A city ordinance can add a notice to the city clerk on a clock the generic poster omitted, and that clock is added without deleting the state or federal clocks. Many merchants also owe an external scan each quarter under the brand rules. A local habit may be stricter than the company standard and may not be weaker.',
        'r1': 'Your trade can add rules on top of company wishes.\nThose rules can be stricter than a textbook.\nThey can differ from the next city or the next trade.\nA card brand rule is a contract, not a passed law.\nDo not keep the card security code after a payment.\nA city office may want its own notice.\nAdd that local clock without erasing the others.\nA site may be stricter than the company.\nA site may not be weaker and call it local custom.\nName a person to watch for revisions.\nThe steps tell staff how to comply. They do not create the outside rule.',
        'r2': "A shop inside a mall follows the owner's own poster, and it also follows the mall's tenant book and the city's license rules. The mall book says what may be stored in the back room after a sale. The city license adds a call to the city clerk that the national handbook never mentioned. The next town's license does not include that call. None of this lets the shop drop a safety rule the owner already required. A clerk who files the mall book under statutes will look for a lawmaker who is not the enforcer. The tenant book and the city license are industry and local duties. They add to company intent, and they can be stricter without becoming the legislature's statute.",
        'r4': 'List the sector contracts and the city ordinances that touch the service. Write the consequence of ignoring each one, such as a brand fine or a city filing. Compare those texts with the company standard and keep the stricter duty. Do not store a card security code after authorization if the brand forbids it. Add a local notice clock without deleting the others. Do not call the brand rule a statute. Do not weaken the company standard and label the change local. Name who will read the next revision. Leave how-to steps in the procedure.',
        'exam': 'Industry rules and local rules can be stricter than a textbook and can differ by city or sector. Card-brand rules are a contract. They are not a statute. Do not keep the card security code after authorization when the brand forbids it. A city ordinance can add a notice clock the company poster never had. Do not delete the state or federal clocks when you add the city one. A local rule may be stricter than the company standard and may not be weaker. Steps do not create the outside duty.',
    },
    'National and global considerations': {
        'body': "National and global considerations appear when data or staff cross a border and more than one country's rules can apply. The border is what separates this topic from a domestic statute. A second country is in the problem: the records are there, the worker is there, or a government there can demand access.\n\nA transfer is not a friendly extra copy. The home country may forbid a data set from leaving, or it may allow the move only through a mechanism or a contract clause that counsel has accepted. The other country may require its own notice, its own place of storage, or access for its authorities that the home country will not grant. Where the staff sit matters as much as where the servers sit. A support desk employed abroad can read any export placed in its queue. If the home rule says the set must stay home, that desk does not get the export, even inside the same company. Two privacy laws can also clash on retention. Someone has to meet the stricter duty or refuse the move.\n\nCounsel in the home country tells Idris that diagnostic images must remain in country. A vendor offers overnight support from a desk staffed abroad and asks for a full image export. Idris refuses the export. Support for the application can continue, and the images stay home. He records the refusal, the data set, and the counsel note on the ticket. A research file later in the month is different, because counsel has already cleared that single file under a named clause. Idris releases that file and no other. The decision is per data set. It is not a general feeling about foreign vendors.\n\nReviews should ask where the data will live, which countries the users will sign in from, and which laws counsel listed. A sister office abroad is not invisible just because the logo matches. Write the transfer mechanism, or write that there will be no transfer. Open the decision again when hiring begins in a new country. A health statute can be strict without any border, and that question is regulatory. Brand rules can bind a merchant who never exports data, and that question is industrial. This topic begins when the home country limits the crossing, or when the crossing adds another country's claim.\n\nStudents treat every privacy duty as international, or they assume a foreign office may see whatever the home office sees. National and global work is about borders and about more than one country's rules at once. A state statute beside a federal one can be entirely domestic, and the stricter of those two still wins at home. A policy remains management's intent. It cannot approve a transfer the home law forbids.",
        'r0': 'The border is the marker. Diagnostic images that counsel says must stay in the home country are not exported to an overnight desk staffed in a second country, even when that desk is a vendor of the same project. A different file may move only when counsel has named the clause that allows it. Staff location counts as much as server location, because a queue the foreign desk can open is a transfer. Record the refusal or the mechanism per data set. A domestic health statute needs no border. A card-brand contract needs no border either. Revisit the note when hiring starts abroad.',
        'r1': 'This topic starts when records or people cross a border.\nMore than one country can have a say.\nThe home country may say a set must stay home.\nA support desk abroad must not receive that set.\nWhere a person sits can matter as much as the machine.\nSome files may move and others may not.\nAsk the lawyer before you send the copy.\nWrite down why a file was allowed to move.\nA law inside one country is a different question.\nA trade contract can bind you with no border at all.\nA company wish cannot approve a move the home law forbids.',
        'r2': "A clinic prepares a crate of charts for a helper who works across a river, in another country. Customs at the bridge asks which country's rules ride with the crate. The home office has already said these charts do not leave. The helper can still advise by phone about the software, and the crate stays on the home dock. A different, smaller envelope has a stamped permission slip, so that envelope may cross. The helper's badge from the same company does not wave the crate through. The bridge is the national and global problem. More than one country's rules are in play, which a rule that never leaves home does not involve.",
        'r4': "Ask where the data will be stored and which countries the users will sign in from. Read counsel's note before any export. Write the data set, the decision, and the clause or the refusal on the ticket. Do not send a full image set to a foreign support queue when counsel said it stays home. Do not treat a sister office as automatically allowed. Separate this question from a domestic statute that never crosses a border. Reopen the note when a team is hired in a new country. Do not let wishes overrule home law.",
        'exam': 'National and global considerations start when data or staff cross a border and more than one country has a claim. Do not treat a foreign support desk as entitled to a data set the home law says must stay home. Staff location counts, not only server location. Do not treat every privacy duty as international. A state health statute beside a federal one can be domestic, and the stricter duty still wins. A card-brand contract can apply with no border. Company intent cannot authorize a forbidden transfer.',
    },
    'Monitoring and revising governance': {
        'body': 'Monitoring and revising governance means rereading the rules on a calendar and again after incidents, then changing the paper until it matches the practice you actually want. Documents that nobody opens drift. People follow a poster, a memory, or a mailbox that was retired, and the signed page becomes a relic.\n\nThere are two triggers. The calendar trigger is a date on each policy, standard, and procedure. On that date the owner checks that the names, the systems, and the duties are still true. The incident trigger is any event that showed a document was wrong or unused. Each trigger ends with a choice: keep the text, or revise it and pull the old copy from the place staff read. History belongs in the file. Three copies that all claim to be current are a defect. A lessons-learned meeting can name a change and a person. Editing the governing document is the later act, the one that lets a new hire see the rule without having sat in that meeting.\n\nA phishing wave shows the clinic the gap. The reporting steps still say to forward odd messages to a mailbox retired in March, and a break-room poster repeats the old line. Staff obeyed the poster. The messages went nowhere useful. The steering group rewrites the steps: use the portal, do not forward the lure, and do not click. The service-desk lead owns the rewrite. That same day the old poster comes down and the new steps go up. Intent did not change. Suspicious mail is still supposed to be reported. The procedure changed. At the quarterly review a counsel number still points at a person who has left. That correction does not wait for another incident.\n\nPut a review date on every governing document, and keep a list of every place a copy is posted. After an incident, assign someone to compare the document with what people really did. Revise the document when it was wrong. When the document was right and was ignored, treat the gap as training or access, not as a silent edit that blesses the workaround. This habit is not the same as watching logs. A chart can count the clicks. It does not rewrite the steps. Do not leave the retired wording online next to the new wording. Two live versions are how the old mailbox survives.\n\nStudents confuse this habit with log monitoring, or they think a lessons-learned note is already the revised rule. Watching logs is about events. Lessons learned name a change and a person. Revising governance edits the policy, the standard, or the procedure, and it takes the stale poster down. A committee may approve the edit. Having a committee on a calendar is not the same as doing the review.',
        'r0': 'Review governance on a date and again after an incident. The clinic reporting procedure still named a mailbox retired in March, and the break-room poster repeated it, so staff forwarded phishing mail into a void. The rewrite says to use the portal, not to forward the lure, and the old poster comes down the same day. A quarterly pass then fixes a counsel number for a person who already left. Lessons learned can name the owner. The revised procedure is what the next person reads. A log chart does not perform that edit. Two copies marked current are a defect.',
        'r1': 'Reread the written rules on a set date.\nReread them again after something goes wrong.\nChange the page when the page is wrong.\nTake the old poster down the same day.\nA meeting note is not yet the new page.\nName who owns the rewrite.\nDo not leave two current copies in the folder.\nA quiet chart of events does not edit the rules.\nIf people ignored a correct page, teach them.\nDo not rewrite a rule just to bless a bad shortcut.\nCheck phone numbers and mailbox names while you are there.\nThe wish can stay the same while the steps change.',
        'r2': 'After a spill, the kitchen crew looks at the card above the sink. The card still says to call a number that was disconnected in the spring, and a stained copy on the fridge says the same thing. People followed the fridge. The call never arrived. The lead reprints the card with the new number, pulls the stained copy down before the next shift, and writes her name on the change. A tally of how many spills happened does not, by itself, replace the card. The reprinted card and the removed copy are monitoring and revising governance. The rules are reread and fixed so the paper matches the practice you want.',
        'r4': 'Open each policy, standard, and procedure and write down its review date. After an incident, compare the text with what people actually did. Revise the text when it taught the wrong step. Remove the old poster or the old wiki copy the same day. Name the owner of the rewrite. Do not leave two versions marked current. Do not treat a log chart as the revision. If the text was already right, plan training instead of weakening the text. Check retired mailboxes and departed names. Keep the lessons note, and still edit the governing page.',
        'exam': 'Governance is reviewed on a schedule and after incidents so the paper does not drift from practice. Rewriting the reporting steps and taking down the old poster is this work. Do not call a log chart governance monitoring. A chart counts events. It does not edit the procedure. Do not treat a lessons-learned note as the revised rule. Lessons learned name a change and a person. The governing document still has to change. Two copies both marked current are a defect. A committee may approve the edit and is not a substitute for doing it.',
    },
    'Boards and committees': {
        'body': "Boards and committees give governance a room where a decision can be made and a record of what was decided. Without that room, the rule lives in one memory and disappears when that person is out. A board holds oversight. A security committee makes the closer calls and writes them down.\n\nA steering committee that works has a charter, a quorum, a calendar, and minutes. Monthly is a common rhythm. Each decision receives an owner. The minutes say what was approved, what was refused, who owns the work, and when it will be checked again. Directors on a board may accept a risk the committee is forbidden to accept, and the committee should send that risk upward instead of quietly accepting it. A chat thread is not the record. A sentence that the group agreed, with no names and no text of the decision, is not the record either. A person who missed the meeting has to be able to find the outcome.\n\nIn March the clinic's steering committee sits. The minutes say the laptop encryption standard is approved, the endpoint manager owns the rollout, and any exception comes back to this committee rather than being granted in a hallway. A director who was absent can still read the decision. The next month the committee refuses to exempt a whole department, and that refusal is written down too. A clinical device that cannot meet the standard is risk the committee may not accept, so it goes to the board. The board's yes or no is minuted at board level. Nothing that matters stays only in the chair's head.\n\nSend the agenda out before the meeting and the minutes out after it. Chase actions until they close. Keep oversight separate from the help desk. The committee should not become the place that resets passwords, and the help desk should not rewrite a standard the committee has approved. Put people in the room who are allowed to decide, not only people who need to be told. When the decision changes intent, update the policy itself. Minutes do not replace that document. The committee is where the intent was argued, approved, and stored.\n\nStudents treat the committee as the policy, as the data owner, or as proof the model is centralized. Management's intent is the policy. A data owner decides classification, access, and retention for a set of data. One body setting rules for everyone is centralized governance, and units writing more of their own rules is the decentralized model. A committee is the forum and the minutes. Either model can use one. A monthly gathering with no minutes is a conversation, not yet governance.",
        'r0': "A steering committee needs a charter, a quorum, and minutes. The clinic's March minutes approve the laptop encryption standard, name the endpoint manager as owner, and send exceptions back to the same committee. A refusal to exempt a whole department is minuted the next month. Risk the committee cannot accept, such as a device that cannot meet the standard, goes to the board and is minuted there. Chat is not the record. Monthly is a common cadence. Update the policy itself when intent changes. Minutes do not replace that policy page. A person who missed March can still read those minutes.",
        'r1': 'A committee decides the closer questions and writes them down.\nGive the group a purpose, enough people present, and a calendar.\nWrite minutes that say what was approved or refused.\nName who owns each decision.\nA chat line that says we agreed is not the record.\nSend hard risk upward when this group may not accept it.\nA person who missed the meeting should still read the result.\nDo not let the group become the help desk.\nDo not let the help desk rewrite what the group approved.\nUpdate the real rule page when the decision changes it.\nA meeting with no notes is only a conversation.',
        'r2': 'Once a month the warehouse leads sit in a side office with a written agenda. The notes say the new lock rule is approved, name the shift lead who will fit the locks, and say any skip must come back to this table. A request to skip the rule for a whole aisle is refused, and the refusal is in the notes. A broken door the group is not allowed to live with is taken to the owners upstairs, who write their own yes or no. A text that says we talked is thrown out. Those notes and that table are a board or a committee. The decision has a place, and it is written down instead of living in one memory.',
        'r4': 'Read the charter, the quorum rule, and the last minutes. Write down each decision, its owner, and its review date. Confirm exceptions are sent back to the committee rather than granted in a hallway. Escalate risk the committee is not allowed to accept. Do not treat a chat agreement as the record. Publish minutes a person who was absent can find. Update the policy or standard when the decision changed it. Do not let operations quietly rewrite the approved text. Leave classification to the data owner unless the minutes assign it.',
        'exam': "Boards and committees are where governance is decided and recorded. Minutes that name the decision and the owner are the record. A chat agreement is not. Do not call the committee a policy. Management's intent still has to be written as a policy. Do not call the committee a data owner. An owner decides classification, access, and retention for a data set. Do not treat one committee as proof the model is centralized. Units can still write local rules. A monthly meeting without minutes is a conversation.",
    },
    'Centralized and decentralized governance': {
        'body': 'Centralized governance means one body sets the rules for the whole organization. Decentralized governance means business units write more rules of their own. The center supplies a common floor. Local pages can fit local work, and they can also contradict the floor or one another. The choice is about who may decide. It is not a choice about whether any rules exist.\n\nA floor plus a stricter option is the pattern that holds. The company keeps one encryption standard. A clinic inside the company may add a local rule that a laptop needs a second approval before it leaves the building. The addition is stricter, so it stands. The clinic may not issue a local rule that turns encryption off. Weaker local rules are how decentralization fails, especially when someone calls the weakness flexibility. Centralization fails on a different path. A rule that is too vague, or too slow to change, gets ignored because the unit still has to finish the day. Write which topics are central, such as identity, logging, and encryption, and which topics a unit is allowed to extend.\n\nCompany text requires full-disk encryption on every laptop. The downtown clinic adds a local rule, in writing, that devices holding diagnostic images do not go to home offices at all. The clinic director owns that stricter rule. A satellite office then drafts a page that makes encryption optional on temporary laptops. The governance office rejects the draft. Optional encryption cuts the company floor. The satellite office may add a tighter rule about locked cabinets. It may not subtract the required setting. The conflict rule is printed where drafters will see it: on the named topics the company standard is the minimum, and a unit may only go stricter.\n\nShow unit leaders the conflict rule before they draft. Read local pages for clashes, not only for typos. Offer a real exception path, with an owner and an end date, so a unit is not tempted to hide a weaker setting inside a local guide. A guide is advice, and it cannot smuggle a lower duty past the floor. People who write the central rule should understand the local work, or the rule will be impossible and will be bypassed. Units should not invent private meanings for incidents the whole company has to handle in one way.\n\nStudents hear decentralized and picture a place with no rules, or they see one committee and assume everything is centralized. Decentralized governance still has rules. It lets units write more of them, which can match the work and can also create contradictions. Centralized governance keeps the floor with one body. A local rule may be stricter than the company standard. It is not supposed to be weaker. Owners, custodians, and controllers are roles about data. They are not this choice between one rulebook and several.',
        'r0': 'One body sets the company floor, and a unit may add a stricter local rule. The company requires full-disk encryption on every laptop. The downtown clinic adds a written ban on taking image-bearing devices to home offices. A satellite draft that makes encryption optional on temporary laptops is rejected, because it cuts the floor. Identity, logging, and encryption stay central. A dated exception, with an owner, is the honest path when a unit cannot meet the floor yet. A local guide cannot hide a weaker setting. Decentralized does not mean ungoverned. Contradictions are the risk when several units draft alone.',
        'r1': 'One group can set the rules for everyone.\nOr each unit can write more of its own.\nLocal rules can fit local work.\nLocal rules can also clash.\nA unit may add a stricter rule.\nA unit may not add a weaker one.\nCall the weaker draft what it is, a cut in the floor.\nWrite down which topics stay with the center.\nGive units a dated way to ask for a real skip.\nDo not hide that skip inside a book of tips.\nPeople who write the center rule should know the local work.\nHaving many rule writers is not the same as having no rules.',
        'r2': 'A chain of cafes keeps one book that every cafe must follow for the locks on the cold room. The downtown cafe adds a house rule that those keys never leave the building. That house rule asks more, so it stays. Another cafe writes that temporary keys need no lock at all. The chain office throws that page out. The second cafe may add a stricter count of who holds keys. It may not drop the lock. A suggestion card on the counter cannot whisper a lower duty. One shared book, with local pages that only add care, is centralized and decentralized governance. The floor stays common, and a local rule may be stricter but not weaker.',
        'r4': 'Read which topics the company keeps central, such as identity, logging, and encryption. Compare each local rule with that floor. Allow a written local rule that is stricter. Reject a local rule that is weaker, even if it is labeled flexible. Write the conflict rule where drafters will see it. Offer exceptions that name an owner and an end date. Do not let a local guide smuggle a lower setting. Check that central authors understand the local work before you add another vague rule. Leave data-owner decisions and vendor processing roles out of this choice.',
        'exam': 'Centralized governance means one body sets the rules. Decentralized governance lets units write more of their own, which can fit local work and can create contradictions. A local rule may be stricter than the company standard, such as a travel ban on top of required encryption. It may not be weaker. Do not treat decentralized as a lack of rules. Do not treat one committee meeting as proof the model is centralized. Do not confuse this choice with data owners, custodians, or controllers. A guide cannot hide a cut in the floor.',
    },
    'Data owners': {
        'body': "A data owner decides for a named set of data. The decisions are the classification, who may have access, and how long the set is kept. Accountability stays with the owner even when other people run the systems. Being able to query a database is not the same act as owning the data.\n\nThe role sits with the business, not with whoever holds an administrator login. The finance director can own payroll. An export then needs that director's approval on the ticket, even if a database administrator could produce the file before morning. The people who store the database, take the backup, and restore the file are custodians. A custodian stores and operates. A custodian does not decide that an analyst may walk out with a copy. When the director leaves the job, a new owner is named. The role does not silently stick to the person who remembers the restore. Classification, access, and retention remain the owner's calls, written so a custodian can carry them out without making them again.\n\nPayroll at the clinic makes the split obvious. The finance director sets the classification, sets the retention period, and approves one export for an auditor. After a disk failure, information technology restores the database and does not treat the restore as a wider grant of access. A help-desk lead who resets passwords is not the owner either. A researcher who wants a de-identified extract waits for the director's decision. The custodian may build the extract after that yes, and should refuse to build it before the yes. The ticket holds the request, the decision, and the date, which is how the story stays visible after the people change.\n\nName an owner on each important set in the inventory, and name a replacement when the person changes jobs. Send access requests and export requests to the owner, not to the operator who answers fastest. Custodians record the change they made. They do not invent a looser classification because the restore window is short. An internal owner is not the same role as a controller. A controller decides why and how personal data is processed, often speaking for the organization. The director can own payroll inside the company while the company is the controller of that personal data. A processor handles the personal data for the controller and under the controller's instructions. None of this turns the custodian into the person who decides.\n\nStudents promote the custodian to owner because the custodian can touch the server. The owner decides classification, access, and retention. The custodian stores and operates. The controller decides why personal data is processed and how. The processor does that processing for the controller. Signing a payroll export is an owner act. Restoring the database is custodial work. Mix those up and access follows whoever has the keys instead of whoever is accountable.",
        'r0': "The finance director owns payroll and must approve an export on the ticket before a custodian builds it. Classification, access, and retention are the owner's decisions, including the retention period written for that file. Information technology may restore the database after a disk failure and still may not widen access. Reassign the owner when the director leaves. The company can be the controller that decides why and how that personal data is processed, while the director remains the internal owner. A processor would handle the data only under instructions. Administrator rights are not ownership. The ticket is the record of the yes.",
        'r1': 'An owner decides for a named pile of records.\nThe owner picks how sensitive the pile is.\nThe owner picks who may see it.\nThe owner picks how long it is kept.\nSomeone else may store the pile and run the backup.\nThat helper does not make the access decision.\nA restore is not permission to hand copies out.\nIf the owner leaves, name a new owner.\nDo not leave the choice with whoever knows the buttons.\nSend export requests to the owner.\nKeep the yes or no on the ticket.\nDeciding why personal records exist is a different job.',
        'r2': "The finance lead keeps the say over a locked payroll ledger. A clerk in the back room holds the key to the safe, makes the nightly copy, and can put the ledger back after a flood. The clerk does not get to hand a page to a curious friend. That handoff waits for the finance lead's written yes. If the finance lead moves to another town, a new lead is named the same week. The clerk's skill with the safe does not become the decision. The locked ledger's decision-maker is the data owner. The person who stores it and operates the safe is the custodian, and storage is not the same as the decision.",
        'r4': 'Find the inventory row for the data set and write down the named owner. If the row is empty, name an owner before the next export. Record classification, who may have access, and the retention period. Route export requests to that owner and keep the decision on the ticket. Let custodians restore and back up without treating those jobs as permission to widen access. Reassign the role when the person changes jobs. Do not give the decision to the fastest administrator. Do not call the owner the legal controller.',
        'exam': "A data owner decides classification, access, and retention, and stays accountable even when others run the system. A custodian stores and operates and does not make that decision. Do not treat a database restore as an access approval. Do not treat administrator rights as ownership. A controller decides why and how personal data is processed, often as the organization. A processor handles that data for the controller under instructions. The finance director's export approval is ownership. The restore is custodial work. If the owner leaves, name another owner.",
    },
    'Controllers and processors': {
        'body': "A controller decides why personal data is processed and how that processing is done. The why is the purpose. The how is the means. A processor handles the data for the controller and does not pick a new purpose of its own. Instructions bind the processor. A clinic that decides why a chart exists is the controller. A firm that hosts mail only because the clinic hired it is the processor.\n\nPurpose is the reason for the use. Means cover the systems and the kinds of recipients used to carry the purpose out. Those choices belong to the controller. A processor may not start a fresh use for its own gain. If the mail host spends patient addresses on the host's own advertisement, the host has become a controller for that new use. Write the deal in a contract that states the instructions, the data covered, and what happens to the data when the deal ends. An internal data owner can still decide classification and staff access for one set. That inside decision does not retitle the vendor as the controller.\n\nThe clinic decides that appointment records exist so patients can be treated, and it decides that reminders should go out. It hires a hosted mail company to send them. The contract limits the company to that sending and to the safeguards the clinic required. The mail company is the processor. A salesperson then asks to reuse the addresses for the company's own newsletter. Ade says no. The newsletter would be a new purpose, so the company would no longer be handling those addresses only for the clinic. Inside the building, the health-information director remains the data owner for staff access, and the technology team remains the custodian of the internal systems. The mail company does not become the custodian of the clinic database by sending reminders. It processes one file under instructions.\n\nRead a vendor pitch for any use beyond the job you are buying. If the real answer is a new purpose, you are facing another controller, or a processor that is about to become one, and the contract has to say which. Sitting outside the building does not make a vendor a processor. A vendor that chooses why the data is used is making the controller's decision. Approving an internal export does not, by itself, make the data owner into the legal controller. Classification, access, and retention stay with that owner. The controller decides purpose and means. Custodians run storage and restores. The processor carries out personal-data handling for the controller.\n\nStudents shuffle the four roles because every one of them touches records. An owner decides classification, access, and retention. A custodian stores and operates and does not own the decision. A controller decides why and how personal data is processed. A processor handles that personal data for the controller, follows instructions, and does not invent a purpose. The clinic's reason for the records is the controller's choice. Mail sent under the contract is the processor's work. A database restore is custodial work, not a processing contract and not an act of ownership.",
        'r0': "The controller chooses purpose and means. The clinic decides that appointment reminders exist and hires a mail host to send them under a contract that states the instructions. The host is the processor. Reusing those addresses for the host's own newsletter would be a new purpose and would make the host a controller for that use. A subcontractor is not added in secret when the contract requires permission. The health-information director can still be the internal data owner for access. The technology team can still be the custodian that stores and restores. Those inside roles do not retitle the vendor.",
        'r1': 'One party decides why personal records are used.\nThat same party decides the way the use is carried out.\nAnother party handles the records for the first one.\nThe handler follows instructions.\nThe handler does not start a new use for itself.\nPut the instructions in a contract.\nSay what happens to the records when the contract ends.\nAsk before a helper of the handler is added.\nA vendor is not a handler just because it is outside.\nA vendor that picks a new reason has become a decider.\nAn inside owner can still approve who on staff may look.\nStoring a backup is still a different job.',
        'r2': "A clinic decides why a stack of charts exists and which errands those charts may take. A courier is hired to carry reminder notes and is told, in writing, not to read them for a side business. The day the courier copies addresses into the courier's own advertisement, the courier has stopped being only a carrier. A director inside the clinic can still decide which staff may open a chart. A clerk can still keep the cabinet and put a chart back after a spill. Neither the director's yes nor the clerk's key makes the courier the one who chose the purpose. The clinic's choice of why and how is the controller. The courier under instructions is the processor.",
        'r4': 'Name who decides the purpose of the personal data and who only handles it. Read the contract for instructions, the data covered, and the end-of-contract duty. Write down any use the vendor wants besides the job you hired. Refuse a new purpose, or treat the vendor as a controller for that use and say so in the contract. Do not add a subcontractor the contract says must be approved first. Keep internal access decisions with the data owner. Let custodians store and restore. Do not assume an outside firm is a processor.',
        'exam': 'A controller decides why and how personal data is processed. A processor handles that data for the controller under instructions and does not invent a purpose. A new use, such as the mail host advertising with patient addresses, makes the host a controller for that use. Do not call the processor the data owner. Classification, access, and retention stay with the owner. Do not call the processor the custodian. A custodian stores and operates. A contract should state the instructions. An outside firm is not a processor just because it is outside.',
    },
    'Custodians and stewards': {
        'body': "A custodian or a steward cares for data from day to day and does not own the business decision. A custodian usually handles the machinery: backups, restores, account changes, and the servers that store the files. A steward usually watches the records themselves, including field meanings and whether a value still matches the definition the business uses. Small teams blur the titles, and some shops use one word for both jobs. Classification, who may have access, and how long the set is kept stay with the data owner. The caretaker carries those choices out on a real system.\n\nWork should move through a ticket so the split stays visible. The owner approves a named person for a named use, and the custodian then grants that role in the database or on the file share. Backup jobs follow the retention period the owner already chose. A restore test proves the copies open, and the ticket records the backup set name and the hash result. The steward samples rows after a load or a restore and flags a column that has drifted, such as an employee identifier that has turned into free text. When a disk fills, the custodian asks the owner before shortening retention.\n\nThursday payroll shows the split in one place. Maya Chen, the finance director, owns the payroll set and has limited full exports to two clerks and the controller. The nightly job fails, and Jamal Ortiz in IT opens ticket PR-4418 as custodian. Jamal restores the database from backup set PB-2026-10-29 taken at 02:00, notes that the hash matched, and leaves the export role unchanged. Priya Shah, the payroll steward, checks twenty restored rows and confirms the employee identifier is still eight digits. The ticket shows a restore, a matching hash, and an access list that still matches finance. IT recovered the file. Finance still decides who may export it.\n\nDefenders look for a caretaker who has started making owner decisions. Compare the live export group with the last approval Maya signed. A group that grew from three names to thirty is a finding even when the database itself is healthy. Ask for the date of the last restore test, not only the date of the last backup that finished. Refuse a quiet request to add a whole team, keep the request text, and route it to the owner. Remove grants that have no approval, and note who added them.\n\nExams mix this pair up with the data owner, and they mix it up with the controller and the processor. The owner stays accountable for the business decision even when another person runs the server. A controller decides why personal data is processed. A processor handles that personal data only under the controller's instructions, often as a vendor under a contract. A custodian can work for either party and still may not reclassify the set or approve a new export. A restore is care. An approval is ownership. An answer that lets the steward set classification because the steward knows the rows has swapped the roles.\n",
        'r0': 'Caretakers operate the data. Owners decide about the data. On ticket PR-4418, Jamal Ortiz restored payroll from backup set PB-2026-10-29 taken at 02:00 and recorded a matching hash. The export role stayed at two clerks plus the controller, which is the list Maya Chen had already signed. Priya Shah sampled twenty rows and confirmed the employee identifier was still eight digits. \nA controller chooses the purpose of personal data. A processor follows that choice under a contract. The caretaker role can exist inside either organization. Access changes belong in a ticket that names the approving owner. A backup that has never been restored in a test is an unfinished control. Quality drift, such as an identifier column turning into free text, goes to the steward and then to the owner if the definition must change.\n',
        'r1': 'Custodians run backups, restores, and access changes.\nStewards watch whether the records stay correct.\nNeither role owns the business decision.\nThe owner still decides who may export a file.\nFinance can own payroll while IT restores it.\nA restore ticket should name the backup set.\nThe hash on that copy should match.\nThe export list should stay as finance signed it.\nA steward can check a sample of rows.\nIT must not invent a new export rule.\nSend new access requests to the owner.\nA controller decides why personal data is used.\nA processor follows that decision under a contract.\nCaretakers can work for either party.\n',
        'r2': 'In a warehouse, the goods owner decides who may wheel a pallet out the door. The night crew labels, locks, and moves stock, and they do not rewrite the release rule when an aisle gets crowded. A quality checker looks for torn labels and wrong counts without setting the price. The crew can put a misplaced pallet back on the shelf and still refuse an unsigned pickup. A crowded aisle is pressure, not permission to change who may take the pallet. Custodians and stewards are that crew and that checker for data. The data owner remains the person who signs the release.\n',
        'r4': "Open ticket PR-4418 beside the signed export approval.\nWrite down the backup set name and whether the hash matched.\nCount the names in the live export group.\nWrite down the owner's name on the last approval.\nDo not add people to the export group yet.\nDo not shorten backup retention to free disk space yet.\nAsk the steward which field proves a good restore.\nRecord any grant you cannot tie to an approval.\n",
        'exam': "The trap is calling the custodian the owner because the custodian can restore the database. Restore work is care. Export approval is the owner's decision. A second trap swaps the caretaker for the controller or the processor. The controller decides why personal data is processed. The processor handles it under instructions. A steward who knows the rows still does not set classification. A quiet access change with no owner on the ticket is a failed split, not proof that IT now owns the data.\n",
    },
    'Risk identification': {
        'body': 'Risk identification is the step that names a concrete way harm can happen, before anyone scores it or chooses a treatment. A useful entry names the asset, the threat, the weakness, and the outcome in ordinary words. "Lost unencrypted laptop" against the customer list is an identification. A one-word label such as "security risk" is not, because nobody can tell what would be lost or how. Identification does not yet say whether the loss is high or low, and it does not yet say whether to buy insurance or change a control. Those later steps need a named risk to work on.\n\nThe mechanism is a sentence you could investigate. Start from an asset inventory: the customer list, the payroll database, the public website, the badge system. For each asset, ask what could act on it, through what weakness, with what result. Theft is a threat. A disk without encryption is a weakness. Disclosure of names and account numbers is the outcome. The same asset can sit in several entries, because fire, theft, and a bad export are different risks. Past tickets, audit findings, and a walk through the office all feed the list. Vague wording is a defect in the list, not a cautious style.\n\nLuis Ortiz runs the workshop for a clinic that issues laptops to visiting nurses. The asset sheet says the customer list on those laptops is the sensitive set. A nurse describes leaving a bag in a car between home visits. Luis writes register row R-14 as: threat, theft from a vehicle; weakness, disk not encrypted; asset, customer list; outcome, names and account numbers disclosed. He does not write "laptop problem," and he does not assign a dollar figure in this meeting. The row is specific enough that next week\'s analysis can price it or rank it. People in the room can point at a real bag and a real file.\n\nDefenders treat a fuzzy register as a detection problem of its own. Read each line and ask whether a new analyst could find the asset and the weakness on Monday. If the line could describe any department, send it back. Compare the register with the asset list and with recent incidents. A stolen-laptop ticket that has no matching row means identification missed a path you have already seen. Mitigation at this stage is a better sentence, not a hurried control. Do not jump to encryption spend until the row says which data would be exposed and on which machines.\n\nThe neighboring step is analysis, and the neighboring failure is a slogan. Qualitative analysis will later mark impact and likelihood with ranks such as high, medium, and low. Quantitative analysis will later use money. Neither ranking is identification. Choosing to encrypt, to insure, or to stop carrying the list is a response, which comes after the risk has a name. An exam item that shows a completed score or a purchased control and calls that identification has skipped the naming. If the words could fit a fire, a theft, and a bad patch equally well, the risk has not been identified yet.\n',
        'r0': 'Identification produces a testable sentence, not a score. Row R-14 at the clinic reads: theft from a vehicle, disk not encrypted, customer list, names and account numbers disclosed. That row names asset, threat, weakness, and outcome. It deliberately stops before a high or low rank and before a dollar estimate. The same list can generate more rows, because fire and a bad export are different weaknesses and different outcomes.\n\nSource material is the asset inventory, recent tickets, and a walk-through. A stolen-laptop incident with no matching row is a gap in identification. "Security risk" fails this step because it names none of the four parts. Scoring with high, medium, or low is the next step. Multiplying value by an exposure factor is also a later step. Do not treat a chosen control as if the naming were already finished.\n',
        'r1': 'Name what can go wrong in plain words.\nName the asset that would be harmed.\nName the threat and the weakness.\nName the outcome you would actually see.\nA lost unencrypted laptop can expose a customer list.\nA vague label such as security risk is not enough.\nDo not score the risk in this step.\nDo not pick insurance or a control yet.\nOne asset can have several different risks.\nUse the asset list and recent tickets.\nA missing row for a known incident is a gap.\nRanking comes later, with words or with money.\n',
        'r2': 'A kitchen that only posts the word danger has not named a hazard. The useful card says which dish can scorch, which burner sticks, and which ingredient is left too close to the flame. People can then walk to that burner and look. A card that says kitchen trouble could mean a fire, a spoiled sauce, or a missing key, so nobody knows where to stand. The cook can then walk to the named burner and see the scorch. Risk identification is that specific card for the organization. A score or a new gadget comes after the card names the asset, the threat, and the weakness.\n',
        'r4': 'Open the risk register and read one line aloud.\nWrite down the asset, the threat, the weakness, and the outcome.\nIf any of those four is missing, mark the line incomplete.\nDo not add a high or low score yet.\nDo not write a dollar loss yet.\nCompare the lines with the asset inventory.\nCompare the lines with incidents from the last quarter.\nSend a vague line back for a concrete rewrite.\n',
        'exam': 'Identification names the asset, the threat, the weakness, and the outcome. It does not assign high, medium, or low, and it does not compute a money loss. A purchased control or an insurance policy is a response, not the identification. The trap is a vague label that would fit any incident. "Lost unencrypted laptop" against the customer list is identified. A one-word slogan is not. Analysis and treatment come after the row can be pointed at in the real office.\n',
    },
    'Ad hoc, recurring, and continuous assessment': {
        'body': 'Risk assessment is not one calendar habit. An ad hoc assessment is a one-time look triggered by a change, such as a new clinic, a merger, or a system going live. A recurring assessment repeats the same questions on a schedule, such as every quarter. A continuous assessment watches conditions all the time through scans, logs, and control checks. All three can be honest. The mistake is pretending that the one-time look retires the calendar, or that a tool which watches every day has already made a decision.\n\nThe mechanism is scope, trigger, and a human close. An ad hoc review has a reason and an end: the new building, the new vendor, the new laptop program. The output is a set of findings dated to that change. A recurring review reopens the register even when nothing dramatic happened, because likelihood and exposure drift. A continuous feed raises signals every day: a failed backup, a new open share, a laptop missing encryption. Those signals are inputs. Someone still has to rate the risk, pick a response, and write the date of the next human look. A dashboard that stays green does not accept risk on leadership\'s behalf.\n\nNora Berg opens a new clinic on 1 March. She runs an ad hoc assessment that week and files the result as review N-1, including the laptop row and the badge process. The same checklist is already on the calendar for 1 June, 1 September, and 1 December, because the opening review does not replace the quarterly one. Between those dates, the encryption console reports each morning which laptops lack full-disk encryption. On 12 April the console shows four nurses\' machines unprotected. That daily fact is continuous watching. It becomes an assessment decision only when Nora updates the register, names an owner, and sets a review date. Leaving the four machines as a chart on a screen is not the decision.\n\nDefenders separate the feed from the judgment. Check that a major change has an ad hoc record, that the recurring date is still in the future, and that continuous alerts have a queue with an owner. A new clinic with only a glossy opening report, and no quarterly date, is under-assessed. A quarterly meeting that ignores the daily encryption failures is a ritual. Mitigation is to put the missed pattern onto the register and to keep the next recurring date even after the emergency review. Do not turn off the daily report because the quarterly meeting felt complete.\n\nThe exam trap is treating the three timings as substitutes, or treating a monitor as a decision-maker. Ad hoc answers "what changed." Recurring answers "what is still true on a schedule." Continuous answers "what moved since yesterday." Qualitative ranks and quantitative money are ways to express the result, not names for these timings. A tool that watches every day is still not a decision. A one-time look at a new site does not prove the risk will stay still for the rest of the year.\n',
        'r0': 'Three timings belong on the calendar, and they do not replace one another. Review N-1 is the ad hoc assessment of the clinic that opened on 1 March. The same checklist is scheduled for 1 June, 1 September, and 1 December. That quarterly set is the recurring assessment. Each morning the encryption console lists laptops without full-disk encryption. On 12 April it listed four machines. That daily list is continuous watching.\n\nThe four machines are not a decision until a person updates the register, names an owner, and sets a review date. A green chart does not accept risk. A one-time opening report does not cancel the June review. Keep the trigger, the schedule, and the feed as separate records.\n',
        'r1': 'Some assessments happen once, for a change.\nA new clinic needs that opening look.\nSome assessments return on a schedule.\nA quarterly checklist is the repeating kind.\nSome watching runs every day.\nA daily list of weak laptops is that kind.\nThe daily list is not yet a decision.\nA person still has to update the register.\nOne opening review does not cancel the quarter.\nA quiet quarter does not cancel the daily feed.\nWrite the next review date before you close the meeting.\nDo not let a green chart accept the risk.\n',
        'r2': "A clinic gives a new patient a first exam, brings people back for a yearly physical, and also runs a monitor beside the bed. The first exam does not cancel next year's physical. The monitor can beep all night and still not choose a treatment. A nurse has to read the beep and decide. The yearly physical is still booked after a careful first exam. Ad hoc, recurring, and continuous assessment work the same way for risk. A one-time review, a dated repeat, and a daily feed are three different jobs, and the feed still needs a person to decide.\n",
        'r4': 'Find the assessment tied to the newest site or system.\nWrite down its date and its trigger.\nFind the next recurring review date on the calendar.\nWrite that date next to the register.\nOpen the daily control report and note what it flagged today.\nDo not close the recurring review because an opening review exists.\nDo not treat a green dashboard as an accepted risk.\nLeave scores unchanged until an owner updates the row.\n',
        'exam': 'The trap is saying a one-time assessment replaces the quarterly one, or that a daily tool has already decided. Ad hoc is triggered by a change and then ends. Recurring returns on a schedule even when the week was quiet. Continuous watching supplies facts every day and is still not a decision. High, medium, and low, or a dollar loss, are ways to record a result. They are not names for these three timings. A new clinic needs the opening look and the later checklist.\n',
    },
    'Qualitative risk analysis': {
        'body': 'Qualitative risk analysis ranks risks with words such as high, medium, and low when solid money figures are not available. Impact is how bad the outcome would be. Likelihood is how probable it is. Each risk gets a rank on both, often from a short scale the organization has defined in advance. The result is an ordered conversation, not a budget. Two honest people can still disagree, so the workshop should record why a rank was chosen, not only the word itself.\n\nThe mechanism is a shared scale applied to named risks. A matrix puts likelihood on one side and impact on the other. A high-impact, high-likelihood cell is handled before a low-impact, low-likelihood cell. The ranks can be words or a small number of steps, such as one through five, as long as everyone uses the same legend. Evidence can move a rank without ever producing a dollar value. After a badge can be revoked in minutes, the chance of a long-lived lost badge drops, so likelihood moves from medium to low while impact stays high, because a live badge still opens sensitive doors. The analysis writes both ranks. It does not pretend the badge now has a precise annual price.\n\nOwen Brooks reviews physical access after the clinic cut badge revocation from a day to ten minutes. The lost-badge risk, row R-22, is marked high impact and low likelihood. The note says why: a lost badge still opens the pharmacy door, so impact stays high, but the new revoke time makes a long misuse unlikely. Owen does not type a dollar loss, because the team cannot defend one. The same sheet marks a stained carpet in the lobby as low impact and low likelihood, so it does not compete with the badge. The words are doing the sorting work that money would do in a quantitative study.\n\nDefenders look for ranks with no reason, and for a sheet where everything says critical. Ask what changed the likelihood, and write the fact, such as the ten-minute revoke. Separate impact from likelihood so a scary outcome is not automatically called frequent. If a facilitator cannot explain the scale, the words are decoration. Mitigation of a high-impact risk still means a real response later: a control, a documented acceptance, insurance, or stopping the activity. The qualitative step itself only sets the order. Do not convert "high" into a fake dollar amount in the margin.\n\nThe neighboring method is quantitative analysis, which uses money: loss per event, how often per year, and a yearly figure. Qualitative analysis is the right tool when those inputs would be guesses dressed as precision. It is the wrong tool when leadership asks whether a control costs less than the yearly loss, because ranks cannot answer a price. Candidates lose this item by calling high, medium, and low a financial result, or by scoring only impact and ignoring likelihood. A lost badge can be severe and still be unlikely after the revoke process improved. Both words belong on the row.\n',
        'r0': 'Qualitative analysis stores ranks, not a budget. Row R-22, the lost badge, is high impact and low likelihood after revocation dropped to ten minutes. Impact stayed high because the badge still opens the pharmacy door. Likelihood fell because a long misuse is now harder. The lobby carpet is low impact and low likelihood, so it sorts below the badge. No dollar figure is attached, and none should be invented in the margin.\n\nThe scale must be written down: what high, medium, and low mean for impact, and what they mean for likelihood. A matrix that marks every row critical has stopped sorting. Moving from words to money is a different method. That method multiplies an asset value by a fraction lost, then by a yearly rate. Do not call a rank of high that product. \n',
        'r1': 'Use words when you cannot defend dollars.\nHigh, medium, and low are the usual ranks.\nImpact means how bad the harm is.\nLikelihood means how probable the harm is.\nPut both ranks on the row.\nA lost badge can be high impact.\nThe same badge can be low likelihood.\nA faster revoke is why likelihood fell.\nThe badge still opens a sensitive door.\nThat is why impact stays high.\nDo not turn the word high into a fake price.\nMoney math is a different kind of analysis.\n',
        'r2': 'A building inspector marks cracks as high, medium, or low without pricing every brick. A wide crack by a supporting wall outranks a hairline crack in a closet, even though nobody has a repair bid yet. The marks let the owner decide what to visit first. People can still argue, so the inspector writes why the supporting wall was marked high. The owner visits the supporting wall first. No repair invoice is required for that ordering. Qualitative risk analysis uses that same kind of ordered words. The words are not a dollar loss, and a later money study is a different job.\n',
        'r4': "Open the scale that defines high, medium, and low.\nRead the impact rank and the likelihood rank separately.\nWrite down the fact that justifies each rank.\nDo not type a dollar amount beside a word rank.\nDo not mark every row critical to be safe.\nNote any row that has impact but no likelihood.\nLeave the response column for a later decision.\nCompare today's ranks with last quarter and record what changed.\n",
        'exam': 'Qualitative analysis uses ranks such as high, medium, and low. It does not produce a dollar loss. The trap is treating "high" as if it were a computed yearly cost. A second trap scores only impact and calls the risk critical, with likelihood left blank. Impact and likelihood are both required. A lost badge can stay high impact and become low likelihood after revocation gets faster. Quantitative analysis is the neighbor that uses money. Do not mix the rank with that money result.\n',
    },
    'Quantitative risk analysis': {
        'body': "Quantitative risk analysis states risk in money: how much one event costs, how many times a year you expect it, and what that comes to per year. The inputs have to be ones you can defend. The one-event figure is single loss expectancy. The yearly count is the annualized rate of occurrence. The yearly money figure is annualized loss expectancy. Qualitative ranks such as high, medium, and low are a different method. They sort when dollars would be a guess. Quantitative analysis is for the conversation that asks what a control is worth buying.\n\nThe arithmetic has a fixed order. Exposure factor is the fraction of the asset lost in one event. A factor of 0.40 is 40 percent, not a count of events. The one-event dollar figure is the asset value multiplied by the exposure factor. An asset value of 80000 times 0.40 equals 32000, the expected loss of one event. The annualized rate of occurrence is how many times per year you expect the event. A rate of 0.5 means you expect the event once every two years. Annualized loss expectancy equals the one-event loss times that rate, so 32000 times 0.5 equals 16000 dollars per year.\n\nAisha Okeke prices the visiting-nurse laptops. The customer list is valued at 80000 dollars. Hardware on each machine is about 900 dollars, and she refuses to use 900 as the asset value, because the loss that matters is the disclosed list. She expects a theft to expose 40 percent of the list's value, so one event is 32000 dollars. She expects that theft 0.5 times a year, so the yearly figure is 16000 dollars. Full-disk encryption for the fleet is quoted at 9000 dollars this year. The yearly loss is higher than the quote, so encryption is a candidate. Aisha writes the assumptions beside the math. Whatever loss remains still needs an owner.\n\nDefenders challenge the inputs before they challenge the multiplication. Ask where 80000 came from, why the fraction is 0.40 rather than the whole asset, and why the yearly rate is 0.5 rather than a story. Recalculate 80000 times 0.40, then 32000 times 0.5, in the margin. If someone multiplied 80000 by 40, or multiplied by 0.5 and called that the one-event loss, the figure is wrong even if the spreadsheet looks tidy. Compare the control's yearly cost with the yearly loss, and say plainly what loss remains. Do not replace this sheet with high, medium, and low when the decision is about a price.\n\nThe trap is dressing a guess as precision, and the neighbor is qualitative analysis. If the value, the fraction, and the rate are inventions, the dollars are theater, and ranks would have been more honest. If the inputs are defensible, ranks cannot answer whether 9000 dollars is a sensible spend against a 16000 dollar yearly loss. Candidates also swap the one-event result with the yearly result. 32000 dollars is one event. 16000 dollars is the year. Neither number is a reward for finding the risk, and the one-event number is not a weekly bill.\n",
        'r0': 'Quantitative analysis uses money in a fixed order. Asset value of the customer list is 80000 dollars. Exposure factor 0.40 means 40 percent of that value is lost in one event. Single loss expectancy is 80000 times 0.40, or 32000 dollars. The annualized rate of occurrence is 0.5, once every two years. Annualized loss expectancy is 32000 times 0.5, or 16000 dollars per year. The laptop shell at about 900 dollars is not the asset value.\n\nEncryption is quoted at 9000 dollars this year. That quote sits below the yearly loss, so the control is a candidate, and residual loss still needs an owner. High, medium, and low are not this method. Multiplying 80000 by 40, or calling 80000 times 0.5 the one-event loss, breaks the definitions. Write each assumption beside the product.\n',
        'r1': "Quantitative analysis uses money you can defend.\nStart with the asset value, here 80000 dollars.\nThe fraction lost in one event is 0.40.\nThat fraction means 40 percent.\nOne event then costs 32000 dollars.\nExpect the event 0.5 times a year.\nThe yearly figure is 16000 dollars.\nCompare a control quote with that yearly figure.\nA 9000 dollar quote can be a candidate.\nThe laptop's hardware price is the wrong value.\nRanks like high or low are a different method.\nDo not treat 32000 as the yearly bill.\n",
        'r2': 'A warehouse manager can feel that shrinkage is "bad" and still not know whether a new lock is worth the invoice. Counting the value of one broken shipment, then how many shipments a year, produces a yearly money figure. The lock\'s price can sit beside that yearly figure. A bare feeling that shrinkage is bad cannot price the lock. The invoice for the lock is a number, so it can sit beside the yearly shrinkage figure. Quantitative risk analysis is that counting, done with an asset value, a fraction lost, and a yearly rate. The result is money per year, and word ranks remain a separate way to sort risks when the counts are not ready.\n',
        'r4': 'Write the asset value, the fraction lost, and the yearly rate on one line.\nMultiply 80000 by 0.40 and confirm 32000.\nMultiply 32000 by 0.5 and confirm 16000 per year.\nWrite the source of each input beside the number.\nDo not use the laptop hardware price as the asset value.\nDo not multiply by 40 when the fraction is 0.40.\nPlace the control quote next to the yearly figure only.\nLeave high, medium, and low off this money sheet.\n',
        'exam': 'Quantitative analysis uses money. Qualitative analysis uses ranks such as high, medium, and low. The one-event loss here is 80000 times 0.40, which is 32000. The yearly loss is 32000 times 0.5, which is 16000. The trap is calling 32000 the yearly figure, or multiplying by 40 instead of 0.40. Another trap uses the hardware price instead of the data value. A tidy spreadsheet does not fix a swapped definition. Residual loss remains after a control is bought.\n',
    },
    'Single loss expectancy': {
        'body': 'Single loss expectancy is the expected loss from one event. Single loss expectancy equals asset value times the exposure factor. The exposure factor is the fraction of the asset lost in that one event. A factor of 0.40 is 40 percent. If the asset value is 80000 dollars, then 80000 times 0.40 equals 32000 dollars. That 32000 is the loss you expect if the event happens once. The 32000 dollar figure is not a reward for writing the risk down, and it is not a weekly figure. How often the event happens is a different input and is not part of this product.\n\nThe mechanism is a fraction applied to a value you chose on purpose. Asset value should be the value of what is harmed, which is often the information, not the sticker price of the box. A laptop shell worth about 900 dollars can carry a customer list valued at 80000 dollars. Using 900 would hide the real one-event loss. The exposure factor is not a probability of the event and not a yearly rate. A factor of 0.40 means four tenths of the asset is lost when the event occurs. Write 0.40, not 40. Multiplying 80000 by 40 produces a nonsense figure in the millions and looks like math only because a spreadsheet accepted it.\n\nHelen Nguyen prices one laptop theft for the records office. The customer list is the asset, at 80000 dollars. She sets the exposure factor at 0.40 because a typical theft is expected to expose about 40 percent of that list, not to vaporize every record the company owns. The one-event result is 32000 dollars for that theft. Helen stops there. She does not multiply by a yearly rate on this line, and she does not divide 32000 into weeks. The worksheet labels the cell "one event." Next to it, in a separate cell, she will later place how many times a year the theft is expected. Mixing those cells is how a one-event loss gets reported as a weekly budget.\n\nDefenders recompute the product from the inputs rather than trusting the labeled total. Confirm the asset is the data value. Confirm the factor is a decimal fraction of one event. Confirm nobody has already multiplied by the yearly rate and still called the result a single loss. Do not "correct" the one-event cell by dividing it into seven days. Mitigation choices, such as encryption, come after you know what one event costs.\n\nThe exam neighbor is annualized loss expectancy, which is this one-event loss times how many times a year you expect the event. With a rate of 0.5, the yearly figure is 16000 dollars. Candidates who answer 16000 when asked for the single loss have done the yearly step too soon. Candidates who answer a reward, a weekly allotment, or 80000 times 40 have left the definition entirely. Qualitative words such as high, medium, and low are not a single loss expectancy either. This topic\'s result is one number, for one event, in money: 32000 dollars in the worked example.\n',
        'r0': 'Single loss expectancy equals asset value times exposure factor, and it stops at one event. Exposure factor 0.40 means 40 percent of the asset is lost if the event happens once. Asset value 80000 times 0.40 equals 32000 dollars. The 32000 dollar result is the expected loss of that one event. The 32000 dollar result is not a reward and not a weekly figure. A yearly rate of 0.5 is not applied in this step.\n\nThe asset is the customer list at 80000 dollars, not the laptop shell at about 900 dollars. Writing the factor as 40 instead of 0.40 inflates the product. A factor of 0.40 is also not the chance the theft occurs. Keep the cell labeled "one event" so it is not divided into weeks or multiplied by 52 inside this same number.\n',
        'r1': 'Single loss expectancy prices one event.\nMultiply asset value by the fraction lost.\nA fraction of 0.40 means 40 percent.\n80000 times 0.40 equals 32000 dollars.\nThe 32000 dollars is the one-event loss.\nThe 32000 dollar result is not a reward.\nThe 32000 dollar result is not a weekly figure.\nDo not multiply by a yearly rate yet.\nPrice the data, not the laptop shell.\nWrite the fraction as 0.40, not as 40.\nThe fraction is not how often the event happens.\nThe yearly total is a later step.\n',
        'r2': "Picture one crate that falls off a dock. The crate's full value is known, and the damage ruins part of the contents, not always the entire crate. The loss for that single drop is the value times the ruined fraction. You do not yet care how many crates fall in a year, and you do not call the damage a prize. The dock prices that one drop and does not slice it into the days of the week. Single loss expectancy is that one-drop loss for an asset. The yearly pattern is a separate multiplication, and the fraction ruined is not the number of drops.\n",
        'r4': 'Write the asset value and the exposure factor on the worksheet.\nConfirm the value is the data, not the hardware sticker.\nMultiply 80000 by 0.40.\nConfirm the product is 32000 dollars.\nLabel the cell as one event.\nDo not multiply by the yearly rate yet.\nDo not divide the result into weeks.\nDo not change the factor from 0.40 to 40.\n',
        'exam': "Single loss expectancy is asset value times exposure factor, for one event only. In the worked example, 80000 times 0.40 equals 32000. The exposure factor is the fraction lost in that event, and 0.40 means 40 percent. The 32000 dollars is not a reward and not a weekly figure. The trap is multiplying by the yearly rate and still calling the result the single loss. That extra step produces annualized loss expectancy, 16000 dollars when the rate is 0.5. Using 40 instead of 0.40, or using the laptop's sticker price, is also wrong.\n",
    },
    'Annualized loss expectancy': {
        'body': 'Annualized loss expectancy is the money you expect to lose per year from a stated risk. Annualized loss expectancy equals single loss expectancy times the annualized rate of occurrence. Single loss expectancy is the one-event loss. The rate is how many times per year you expect the event. In the laptop example, the one-event loss is 32000 dollars, from an asset value of 80000 times an exposure factor of 0.40. If that theft is expected 0.5 times a year, annualized loss expectancy equals 32000 times 0.5, which is 16000 dollars per year. The 16000 is a yearly figure. The 16000 dollar yearly figure is not the loss on the day of one theft, and it is not a weekly bill.\n\nThe mechanism is what lets a frequent small loss be compared with a rare large one. A help-desk mistake that loses 500 dollars and happens every week has a rate of 52. The yearly figure is 500 times 52, or 26000 dollars. A warehouse fire with a one-event loss of 100000 dollars, expected once a decade, has a rate of 0.1. The yearly figure is 100000 times 0.1, or 10000 dollars. The smaller event outranks the fire on a yearly basis. That comparison is why you annualize. The laptop\'s 16000 dollars per year can sit on the same sheet and be compared with both.\n\nJonah Patel builds the yearly column for the clinic. For the unencrypted laptop he copies 32000 as the one-event loss and 0.5 as the rate, and he writes 16000 per year. For the weekly records error he uses 500 and 52, and he writes 26000 per year. For the decade-scale fire he uses 100000 and 0.1, and he writes 10000 per year. The column now says the weekly error costs more per year than the fire, and the laptop sits between them. Jonah labels the column "per year" so nobody reads 16000 as the damage from a single theft.\n\nDefenders check the units before they brief leadership. Every yearly figure should show its one-event parent and its rate. Recalculate 32000 times 0.5. If the sheet shows 32000 in the yearly column, the rate was dropped. If it shows 16000 as the cost of one theft, the rate was applied in the wrong cell. A control decision belongs next to the yearly number: encryption quoted at 9000 dollars a year is being compared with 16000 dollars a year, not with the one-event 32000. Do not annualize by multiplying a one-event loss by 12 out of habit. Twelve is right only when the event really happens monthly.\n\nThe neighbor students confuse with this yearly total is single loss expectancy. The one-event loss answers "what does one occurrence cost." The annualized figure answers "what does that cost across a year." Qualitative ranks never produce 16000, because high, medium, and low are not money. Another trap is thinking a weekly event\'s yearly total is itself a single loss. The weekly pattern is a rate of 52 applied to a small one-event loss. Leave 32000 as one laptop theft. Leave 16000 as that theft\'s year.\n',
        'r0': 'Annualized loss expectancy equals the one-event loss times the yearly rate. For the laptop, the one-event loss is 32000 dollars, which is 80000 times 0.40. The rate is 0.5. The product is 32000 times 0.5, or 16000 dollars per year. That 16000 is not the damage from one theft and not a weekly amount.\n\nA separate weekly error loses 500 dollars with a rate of 52, which is 26000 dollars per year. A fire loses 100000 dollars once a decade, rate 0.1, which is 10000 dollars per year. The weekly error outranks the fire only after both are annualized. Compare a control quote with the yearly column. Encryption at 9000 dollars a year is compared with 16000, not with 32000. Do not multiply by 12 unless the event is actually monthly.\n',
        'r1': 'Annualized loss expectancy is the yearly money loss.\nMultiply the one-event loss by the yearly rate.\nThe laptop one-event loss is 32000 dollars.\nThe laptop rate is 0.5 times a year.\n32000 times 0.5 equals 16000 dollars per year.\nThe 16000 dollars is not one theft.\nThe 16000 dollars is not a weekly bill.\nA 500 dollar weekly loss has a rate of 52.\nThat weekly loss is 26000 dollars a year.\nA rare fire can rank lower after you annualize.\nCompare control cost with the yearly column.\nDo not drop the rate and keep 32000 as the year.\n',
        'r2': 'A shop can fear one huge storm and ignore a small leak that returns every week. Pricing the single storm, then pricing a year of leaks, shows which one drains the till across the year. The leak can win that comparison even though one drip looks trivial. The storm is still worse on the day it hits. A year of drips can empty the till faster than one famous storm. Annualized loss expectancy is the year-long comparison for risks. The cost of one event stays a separate number, and a weekly habit is a rate, not a new name for the one-event loss.\n',
        'r4': 'Copy the one-event loss and the yearly rate into separate cells.\nMultiply 32000 by 0.5 and write 16000 per year.\nLabel that cell per year, not per event.\nDo not replace 32000 with 16000 in the one-event cell.\nFor a weekly loss, use a rate of 52, not 1.\nDo not multiply by 12 unless the event is monthly.\nPlace the control quote beside the yearly figure only.\nRecalculate before you brief the yearly column.\n',
        'exam': 'Annualized loss expectancy is single loss expectancy times the annualized rate of occurrence. Here, 32000 times 0.5 equals 16000 dollars per year. The trap is reporting 32000 as the yearly loss, or reporting 16000 as the one-event loss. A second trap calls the one-event figure a weekly amount because some other loss happens weekly. A small weekly loss can outrank a large rare loss only after both are turned into yearly figures. Qualitative ranks are not this product.\n',
    },
    'Annualized rate of occurrence': {
        'body': 'The annualized rate of occurrence is how many times per year you expect an event. That yearly count is a frequency, not a dollar amount and not a fraction of an asset. A rate of 1 means you expect the event once a year. A rate of 0.5 means once every two years. A rate of 6 means six times a year. About once in fifty years, far under one, is written 0.02. The rate becomes useful when you multiply it by the one-event loss to get the yearly loss.\n\nThe mechanism is an estimate grounded in history or a stated reason, then written as a yearly count. Count events over a long enough window and divide by the number of years. If local history is thin, say so, and use a cautious estimate you can explain, such as several clicks a year at a busy clinic, recorded as 6. A flood of the server room is a different event with a different rate. If you expect that flood about once in fifty years, the rate is 0.02. Do not borrow the exposure factor of 0.40 either. That 0.40 is the share of value lost if an event happens, not how often it happens.\n\nChris Diaz fills the rate column on the clinic register. Phishing clicks are estimated at 6 per year, from the mail gateway\'s last two years. Laptop theft stays at 0.5. The server-room flood is 0.02, with a note that the building sits above the recorded flood line and the figure is a judgment, not a count of local floods. He does not write "low" in this column. High, medium, and low are likelihood ranks for qualitative analysis. The rate column is only for numbers you are willing to multiply by a one-event loss.\n\nDefenders test whether the rate matches the story. A rate of 52 means weekly. A rate of 12 means monthly. Once a decade is written 0.1. If the narrative says "far under one" and the cell says 4, the cell is wrong. If the narrative says "several a year" and the cell says 0.02, the cell is wrong. Watch for a rate copied from the exposure factor, which turns 0.40 into a fake frequency and then produces a fake yearly loss. Mitigation and insurance decisions should use a rate that survived this check.\n\nStudents confuse this rate with impact, with exposure factor, and with the yearly money total. Impact is how bad. The exposure factor is the fraction lost in one event, such as 0.40. The yearly money total is the one-event loss times this rate, such as 32000 times 0.5 equals 16000 dollars per year. The 0.5 in that product is the rate. The 16000 is not the rate. Likelihood words such as low are related in spirit, because a rare flood is low likelihood, but the word low is not a number you can multiply. An exam answer that treats 0.5 as 50 percent of the asset has swapped the rate for the exposure factor.\n',
        'r0': 'The annualized rate of occurrence counts expected events per year. A rate of 0.5 is once every two years. A rate of 6 is six times a year. A rate of 0.02 is about once in fifty years. Phishing clicks at the clinic are estimated at 6. Laptop theft is 0.5. A server-room flood is 0.02, far under one, and the note should say the figure is a judgment.\n\nThis column is not dollars and not the exposure factor. Exposure factor 0.40 means 40 percent of value lost in one event. Multiplying a one-event loss by the rate produces the yearly loss: 32000 times 0.5 equals 16000 dollars per year. The rate in that line is 0.5, not 16000 and not 0.40. A rate of 52 means weekly, and a rate of 0.1 means once a decade.\n',
        'r1': 'This rate is how many times a year.\nA rate of 1 means once a year.\nA rate of 0.5 means once every two years.\nA rate above 1 means more than once a year.\nPhishing clicks might be several a year.\nA server-room flood can be far under one.\nThe rate is not a dollar amount.\nThe rate is not the fraction of the asset lost.\nDo not copy 0.40 into the rate cell.\nWord ranks such as low are a different column.\nMultiply by the one-event loss only later.\nWrite a note when the rate is a judgment.\n',
        'r2': 'A loading dock asks how often the river reaches the floor, not how expensive one soaking is. Once a year is a very different plan from once in a lifetime. The dock crew can count wet years and divide by the years on the chart. They still have not priced a single soaked crate. The chart of wet years is a count, and that count is not the price of one ruined crate. The annualized rate of occurrence is that how-often number for a risk. Dollars stay in other columns, and the share of a crate that rots in one flood is not the count of floods.\n',
        'r4': 'Write the rate as events per year, with the unit visible.\nCheck that 0.5 means once every two years in your note.\nCheck that a weekly story uses 52, not 0.5.\nDo not paste the exposure factor into this cell.\nDo not write high or low in the rate column.\nRecord whether the rate came from a count or a judgment.\nLeave the dollar product in the yearly-loss column.\nDo not change other risks to the same rate for neatness.\n',
        'exam': 'The annualized rate of occurrence is how many times per year you expect the event. It is not the exposure factor, and it is not the yearly dollar loss. A rate of 0.5 means once every two years. It does not mean 50 percent of the asset is lost. The exposure factor of 0.40 is that fraction. Multiplying 32000 by 0.5 produces 16000 dollars per year, and only the 0.5 is the rate. High, medium, and low are qualitative likelihood, not a substitute for this number.\n',
    },
    'Impact and likelihood': {
        'body': 'Impact is how bad an outcome is. Likelihood is how probable that outcome is. A risk conversation needs both, or every scary line looks critical and nothing can be ordered. A public website defacement can be high impact because patients and neighbors see it, while its likelihood falls after a patch. The harm if it happens did not shrink just because it became less probable. Teams that record only one of the two words end up treating a rare disaster and a daily nuisance as the same kind of problem.\n\nThe mechanism is two ratings kept independent, then read together. Impact can consider money, safety, reputation, or downtime, but it is still answering "how bad," not "how often." Likelihood can later be expressed as an annualized rate of occurrence when you move into money, but a rank of low is not itself a rate. Changing a control often moves only one side. A patch on the public site lowers likelihood and leaves impact high, because a successful defacement is still a front-page embarrassment.\n\nEvan Silva reviews the clinic\'s public website after last quarter\'s patch. Before the patch, defacement was high impact and medium likelihood. This quarter the row says high impact and low likelihood. The note cites the patch and a drop in successful probes in the web logs. Visibility stays high: the page is what patients see. A separate row for a typo on an internal cafeteria menu is low impact even though editors hit it often, so likelihood there is high and the pair still sorts below the public site. Evan does not erase the defacement row because likelihood fell. A high-impact, low-likelihood event remains a real risk. It is just no longer the same priority as it was when both ranks were high.\n\nDefenders look for a register where impact was copied into the likelihood cell, or where every public-facing item was marked critical on instinct. Ask what would be true if the event happened, and ask how often you expect it, as two questions. After a control change, record which side moved. If both sides moved, demand two reasons. Mitigation planning uses the pair: a high-impact risk may deserve a control even when likelihood is low, and a high-likelihood nuisance may deserve a cheap fix even when impact is low. Do not spend the rare-disaster budget on the nuisance, and do not ignore the rare disaster because the nuisance is louder.\n\nThe quantitative neighbors are exposure and rate, and they are easy to swap with these words. Impact is not the exposure factor. The exposure factor, such as 0.40, is the fraction of asset value lost in one event inside a money model. Likelihood is not that fraction either, and it is not the annualized rate until you are ready to state a count per year. Candidates fail by marking only impact, by lowering impact just because a patch reduced likelihood, or by calling both ideas "severity." Severity in ordinary speech often means impact alone. This topic always keeps the second word.\n',
        'r0': 'Keep two ratings. Impact is severity. Likelihood is probability. The public website defacement stays high impact after the patch because patients still see the page. Likelihood moves from medium to low, with the patch and the drop in successful probes written as the reason. The cafeteria typo is low impact and high likelihood, and it sorts below the public site.\n\nQualitative work uses ranks such as high, medium, and low on both sides. A later money model may turn likelihood into a yearly rate, such as 0.5, and turn harm into a one-event loss, such as 32000 dollars. Those numbers are not the ranks. The exposure factor 0.40 is a fraction of value, not likelihood and not impact. If a control changes only one side, leave the other side where the evidence puts it.\n',
        'r1': 'Impact is how bad the outcome is.\nLikelihood is how probable the outcome is.\nYou need both, or everything looks critical.\nA public defacement can be high impact.\nA patch can make that defacement less likely.\nThe harm did not shrink just because it got rarer.\nWrite which side changed, and why.\nA frequent nuisance can still be low impact.\nDo not copy impact into the likelihood box.\nWord ranks are not dollar amounts.\nA yearly count is a later, separate number.\nKeep the rare severe event on the list.\n',
        'r2': 'A kitchen can have a grease fire that would be terrible and almost never starts, and a small spill that happens every lunch. Treating every hazard as simply urgent hides the difference. The cook writes how bad each one is, and how often each one shows up, on separate lines. Fixing a loose handle may cut the spills without making a grease fire any less serious if it ever starts. Impact and likelihood are those two lines for a risk. A patch or a new habit often moves only one line, and the other line stays until the facts change it.\n',
        'r4': 'Read the impact rank and the likelihood rank as two fields.\nWrite the reason beside each field.\nAfter a patch, record whether likelihood changed.\nDo not lower impact only because likelihood fell.\nDo not mark every visible system critical by habit.\nDo not put a dollar figure in the rank cells.\nLeave the yearly rate in its own column.\nKeep a high-impact, low-likelihood row on the register.\n',
        'exam': 'Impact is how bad. Likelihood is how probable. The trap is using only one of them, then calling every item critical. A public defacement can stay high impact after a patch lowers likelihood. Do not reduce impact merely because the event got rarer. Ranks such as high, medium, and low are not money. The exposure factor is the fraction lost in one event, not likelihood. The annualized rate of occurrence is a yearly count, not the word high or low. Both sides of the pair have to be present before you prioritize.\n',
    },
    'Risk register': {
        'body': 'A risk register is the working list of risks, with enough structure that a risk can be owned, rated, treated, and revisited. A line that only says "laptops" is a note. A register line names the risk, the owner, the rating, the response, and the review date. The rating may be qualitative ranks or a money figure, but it has to live in the row. The response says what the organization is actually doing. The review date is the promise that someone will check whether that response is real, not merely written.\n\nThe mechanism is one row per risk, updated when facts change, and closed only when the risk is gone or formally accepted with a date to look again. The description should already be specific: lost unencrypted laptop exposing the customer list, not a slogan. The owner is a person accountable for the response, not a department mailbox that nobody reads. The rating records impact and likelihood, or the yearly loss, using the method the organization chose. The response names the path: a control, insurance or a contract, a documented decision to live with it, or a decision to stop the activity. The review date forces a look at evidence. For encryption, the evidence is that encryption is actually on, not that a project was approved.\n\nPriya Shah\'s register row R-14 reads: lost unencrypted laptop, customer list. Owner: Maya Chen, finance. Rating: high impact, medium likelihood, with a linked yearly loss of 16000 dollars. Response: mitigate by full-disk encryption on the nurse laptops. Review date: 15 January, to confirm the encryption console shows the fleet protected. The January check is part of the row, not an optional courtesy. If January arrives and the console still shows four machines unprotected, the row is late even though the response word still says mitigate. A second row with no owner and no date is not a managed risk. It is a sentence waiting to be forgotten.\n\nDefenders audit the register the way they audit a backup: for completeness and for freshness. Sort by review date and list anything in the past. Open one mitigated row and ask for the artifact that proves the control, such as the encryption report. If the response says the risk was accepted, look for the written acceptance, the name, and the date. Do not delete a late row to make the list look short.\n\nStudents confuse the register with a policy, with a scan report, and with the quarterly briefing. A policy states rules. A scan lists possible flaws on hosts. A briefing tells decision makers what changed and what they must decide. The register is the list those other documents should point back to. An exam item that shows a green summary with no owner and no review date has not shown a register. Another trap is a row whose response was chosen but never checked. Mitigation that is not verified by the review date is a wish stored in a table.\n',
        'r0': 'A register row needs five working parts: the risk, an owner, a rating, a response, and a review date. Row R-14 names the lost unencrypted laptop and the customer list. The owner is Maya Chen. The rating records high impact and medium likelihood, and it links a yearly loss of 16000 dollars. The response is mitigation by full-disk encryption. The review date is 15 January, when the console must show the fleet actually encrypted.\n\nA row with no owner, or with a review date already past, is not current. The January check looks at the console, not at the project approval. A policy, a scan, and a quarterly briefing are different objects. They can point at R-14. They do not replace the row.\n',
        'r1': 'A risk register is a list with structure.\nName the risk in plain words.\nPut a person in the owner field.\nAdd a rating beside the name.\nRecord the response you chose.\nSet a review date on the row.\nThe date is when you check the real control.\nAn encryption promise is not enough.\nLook at whether encryption is actually on.\nA row with no owner is only a note.\nA late review date means the row is stale.\nA summary chart does not replace the list.\n',
        'r2': 'A building punch list that says "fix the roof" and never names a person or a recheck day will still be on the wall next winter. A useful card names the leak, the person who owns the repair, how serious it is, what will be done, and the day someone goes back up with a flashlight. The flashlight visit matters more than the word repair. A risk register is that set of cards for risks. A policy or a pretty summary board is not the card, and a card with no recheck date is how a known leak survives the season.\n',
        'r4': 'Open row R-14 and confirm the five fields are filled.\nWrite down the owner, the rating, the response, and the review date.\nIf the review date is past, mark the row late.\nDo not delete a late row to shorten the list.\nDo not change the response during this check.\nFor an encryption response, open the console report.\nWrite down whether the machines are actually protected.\nLeave a scan file or a policy out of the owner field.\n',
        'exam': 'A risk register gives each risk an owner, a rating, a response, and a review date. The trap is a slogan with none of those, or a response that was never checked. "Mitigate with encryption" fails the row if the review date passes and encryption is not actually on. A policy is a rule, a scan is a list of possible flaws, and a briefing is a message to decision makers. None of those is the register. Acceptance has to be written and dated. A green summary with no names is not a managed list.\n',
    },
    'Risk owners and thresholds': {
        'body': 'A risk owner is the person accountable for the response to a named risk. A threshold is the line that forces a bigger conversation instead of a quiet local handling. The owner may delegate the hands-on work. The owner may not disappear. The threshold is agreed in advance, so a high rating is not debated into silence on the day it appears. Below the line, the named owner acts and records the result. At or above the line, the owner escalates, often to a steering group, and the escalation itself is visible.\n\nThe mechanism is accountability plus a trigger. The register already has a name in the owner field. That person makes sure the response happens: a control is applied, a formal acceptance is signed, insurance is bound, or the activity stops. Someone else, such as a custodian, may install the encryption. The owner still answers when the review date arrives and the console is wrong. The threshold is written as a rule about ratings or money. One common rule is that anything rated high is reported to the steering group, while lower items stay with the named owner.\n\nAt the clinic, Maya Chen owns the laptop risk. The threshold policy says any high rating goes to the security steering group in the same month it is rated. The laptop row is high impact, so Maya puts it on the March agenda even though likelihood is only medium. A low finding about a lab printer stays with its owner, facilities lead Owen Brooks, and does not consume the agenda. The minutes show the laptop decision and show that the printer did not cross the line. A high risk handled only in a side chat has missed the threshold even if the chat felt responsible.\n\nDefenders test both the name and the line. Pick a high row and find the steering packet or the escalation ticket. If none exists, the threshold is a poster. Pick a low row and confirm it still has a living owner and a review date, because "below the line" does not mean "unowned." Watch for an owner field that says "IT" or "management" with no person. Watch for a threshold that moves after the rating, which is how bad news stays local. Do not change ratings during this check. Do not treat the custodian who touches the keyboard as the owner just because that person did the work.\n\nThe neighboring ideas are appetite and the four responses. Appetite is how much risk leadership wants to seek, and tolerance is how much variation they allow around that. A threshold is neither of those. It is the escalation trigger. Transfer, acceptance, avoidance, and mitigation are what the owner might choose or propose. The owner is not the same thing as the response. Candidates lose the item by naming the technician as the owner, or by treating every risk as a steering-group topic, or by letting high risks stay quiet. The line exists so the bigger conversation is mandatory, not polite.\n',
        'r0': 'The owner is accountable for the response. The threshold decides who else must hear about it. Clinic policy escalates anything rated high to the steering group in the same month. Maya Chen owns the laptop risk and places it on the March agenda because impact is high. Owen Brooks keeps the low lab-printer finding locally. Jamal may apply encryption, but the minutes still name Maya.\n\n"IT" is not an owner. A side chat is not an escalation. Below-threshold items still need a person and a review date. Appetite is how much risk leadership wants to seek. Tolerance is the variation allowed around that. Neither word is the escalation line. The response, such as mitigate or accept, is what the owner is accountable for seeing through. Do not rewrite a high rating downward to avoid the agenda.\n',
        'r1': 'A risk owner is accountable for the response.\nThe owner can ask someone else to do the work.\nThe owner still answers on the review date.\nA threshold is the line that forces escalation.\nAnything rated high goes to the steering group.\nLower items can stay with the named owner.\nA low item still needs a person.\nA team name is not an owner.\nA side chat is not an escalation.\nDo not drop a rating to avoid the meeting.\nAppetite is a different idea from this line.\nThe technician at the keyboard may not be the owner.\n',
        'r2': 'In a clinic at night, the charge nurse handles small problems and calls the attending when a vital sign crosses a posted line. The line was agreed before the shift, so nobody argues the number while a patient is in trouble. The nurse can ask a technician for a task and still remain the person who must call. The posted number was chosen on a calm afternoon, which is why it holds during the night. Risk owners and thresholds work the same way. The owner stays accountable, and the threshold is the posted line that makes a bigger conversation mandatory rather than optional.\n',
        'r4': 'Pick one high-rated row and write down the owner.\nFind the steering agenda or escalation ticket for that row.\nIf none exists, mark the threshold as missed.\nDo not change the rating during this check.\nPick one below-threshold row and confirm it has a person.\nDo not accept a department name in the owner field.\nWrite down who will do the hands-on work, separately from the owner.\nLeave appetite statements out of the owner field.\n',
        'exam': 'The owner is accountable for the response even when someone else performs the task. The threshold is the escalation line, such as reporting anything rated high to the steering group. The trap is naming the technician as the owner, or calling a department the owner. A second trap treats the threshold as appetite or as tolerance. Appetite is how much risk leadership wants to seek. Tolerance is allowed variation around that. A high risk kept in a side chat has missed the line. Lower risks stay local and still need a named person.\n',
    },
    'Risk tolerance and appetite': {
        'body': 'Risk appetite is how much risk leadership wants to seek in order to do the work. Risk tolerance is how much variation leadership will accept around that aim. Appetite is the target willingness. Tolerance is the wobble they will still live with before the situation is out of bounds. The two words are not interchangeable. An organization can have a low appetite for exposing customer data and still tolerate a short delay on a low finding in a lab, because that delay is variation around a less sensitive objective.\n\nThe mechanism is a pair of statements that later judgments can be tested against. Appetite is set by leadership for categories that matter: customer data, clinical safety, public websites, experimental labs. It is not a single mood for the whole building. Tolerance states the acceptable drift. For customer data, tolerance may be effectively none for missing encryption: unencrypted customer data is outside what leadership will accept. For a lab finding rated low, tolerance may include a short delay if the owner and the date are written down. A local team does not get to widen appetite because a project is late. If a proposal sits outside appetite, it has to be changed, escalated, or explicitly rejected.\n\nThe clinic\'s leadership statement is short enough to test. They will not accept unencrypted customer data on laptops. They will accept a short delay on a low finding in the lab. When Aisha brings the laptop row, appetite already answers the direction: leaving the disks unencrypted is outside appetite, so "do nothing and hope" is not available. When Owen reports a low lab finding that will be fixed two weeks late, tolerance covers that wobble if the delay is written down. The same two-week slip on unencrypted customer data would not be tolerance. It would be a breach of appetite. The category decides which statement applies.\n\nDefenders compare decisions with the written pair, not with the loudest person in the room. Take three recent acceptances and ask which statement allowed them. An acceptance of unencrypted customer data contradicts the appetite statement and should not be filed as tolerance. A delay that has no end date is not the "short delay" leadership described. Update the statements when strategy changes, and do not let old posters contradict the new ones. Do not use appetite as a synonym for the escalation threshold. The threshold says who must be told. Appetite says what risk the organization is willing to seek.\n\nCandidates swap the two words constantly. If the question asks what leadership is willing to pursue or take on, the answer is appetite. If the question asks how far results may drift around that aim before someone must intervene, the answer is tolerance. A threshold is the escalation trigger, such as sending high risks to the steering group. A response such as mitigate or accept is what you do with one risk. Appetite and tolerance are the boundaries those choices are judged against. Calling both ideas "how much risk is okay" throws away the distinction the question is testing.\n',
        'r0': "Appetite is the risk leadership wants to seek. Tolerance is the variation they will allow around that. The clinic will not accept unencrypted customer data. That is an appetite boundary, and it is tight for that category. The clinic will accept a short delay on a low lab finding. That delay is tolerance, and it does not loosen the customer-data boundary.\n\nA two-week slip on unencrypted laptops is not the lab's allowed wobble. A delay with no end date is not a short delay. The escalation threshold, such as sending high ratings to the steering group, is not appetite and not tolerance. Mitigate, accept, avoid, and transfer are responses judged against these boundaries. Local teams do not widen appetite because a project is late. Write the category on the statement so a lab rule is not applied to customer data.\n",
        'r1': 'Appetite is how much risk leaders want to seek.\nTolerance is how much wobble they will allow.\nThe two words are not the same.\nUnencrypted customer data can sit outside appetite.\nA short delay on a low lab finding can sit inside tolerance.\nThe lab delay does not loosen customer data rules.\nA delay needs an end to stay short.\nA local team may not widen appetite.\nThe escalation line is a different idea.\nResponses are judged against these boundaries.\nWrite which category the statement covers.\nDo not use one mood for the whole building.\n',
        'r2': 'A restaurant decides how hot it wants the house sauce, and then how far a single bowl may drift before the bowl is sent back. The target heat is the aim. The allowed drift is not a new aim. A mild special can have more drift without changing how hot the house sauce should be. Sending a bowl back is not a vote to change the house recipe. Risk appetite is the target willingness to take risk. Risk tolerance is the allowed drift around that target. A loose rule for a low lab finding does not rewrite the target for customer data.\n',
        'r4': 'Read the appetite statement and the tolerance statement as two documents.\nWrite down which data or process each one covers.\nTest the laptop decision against the customer-data statement.\nDo not file unencrypted customer data as a tolerated wobble.\nCheck that any allowed delay has an end date.\nDo not change the statements during a single risk review.\nDo not copy the escalation threshold into the appetite paragraph.\nNote any local decision that widens appetite without leadership.\n',
        'exam': 'Appetite is how much risk leadership wants to seek. Tolerance is how much variation they will accept around that. The trap is swapping the words, or folding both into "risk is okay." Refusing unencrypted customer data is an appetite boundary. A short, dated delay on a low lab finding is tolerance. That delay does not authorize the same slip for customer data. A threshold only decides escalation, such as high ratings going to the steering group. Mitigate and accept are responses, not synonyms for appetite or tolerance.\n',
    },
    'Transfer, accept, avoid, and mitigate': {
        'body': 'These four are the responses you can choose once a risk is named. Transfer shifts financial burden by insurance or by a contract, and it does not stop the event. Accept means you live with the risk on paper, with an owner and a date, rather than pretending it is gone. Avoid means you stop the activity that creates the risk. Mitigate means you apply a control that reduces likelihood or impact. Ignoring a risk is not a fifth option on this list. An ignored risk has no owner and no decision.\n\nThe mechanism is a choice recorded on the register, often in combination, with the leftover risk named. Mitigation comes first when a sensible control exists. Full-disk encryption mitigates laptop theft by cutting the exposure of the customer list if the machine is stolen. The theft can still happen, so some risk remains. Transfer can cover part of that remainder: an insurance policy pays money after a covered loss, or a contract makes a vendor carry a stated duty. The insurer does not reach into the parking lot and stop the theft. Avoidance is stricter. If nurses stop storing the customer list on laptops, that particular disclosure path is avoided because the activity stopped. Acceptance is the documented decision to live with what remains, or with a risk that is truly small.\n\nThe clinic\'s laptop row uses two responses and refuses a third. The team mitigates with full-disk encryption. It transfers some residual financial risk with an insurance policy that names device theft. It does not ignore the row. Maya Chen remains the owner, and the review date checks that encryption is on and that the policy is in force. A proposal to "accept" the unencrypted list with no writing would contradict both the response list and leadership\'s appetite.\n\nDefenders read the response word and then demand the matching evidence. For mitigate, look at the control in operation, such as the encryption console. For transfer, read the policy or the contract and see what it actually pays or requires. For avoid, confirm the activity stopped. For accept, find the written decision, the owner, and the date. If the word and the evidence disagree, the register is wrong. Do not call a control a transfer. Do not call insurance a control that prevents the loss. Residual risk after mitigation is still a risk, and it needs its own explicit accept or transfer.\n\nThe exam traps are synonyms that are not synonyms. Accept is not ignore. Avoid is not "be careful," and it is not mitigate. Mitigate is not a promise that the loss is now zero. Transfer is not prevention, and it is not the same as acceptance, because another party has taken a defined financial piece by insurance or contract. Qualitative ranks and yearly loss figures help you choose among the four. They are not themselves responses. Stopping the activity maps to avoid. Applying a control maps to mitigate. A signed decision to live with the risk maps to accept. Insurance or a contract that shifts the money maps to transfer.\n',
        'r0': 'Four responses exist, and ignore is not one of them. Mitigate applies a control: full-disk encryption on the nurse laptops reduces how much a theft exposes. Transfer uses insurance or a contract. The theft policy pays after a covered loss and does not stop the parking-lot theft. Avoid means stopping the activity, such as ending customer-list storage on laptops. Accept means living with a stated remainder, documented, with Maya Chen as owner and a review date.\n\nThe laptop row mitigates and transfers. It does not ignore. Residual risk after encryption is not zero. Evidence must match the word: a console report for the control, a policy document for the insurance, a stopped practice for avoidance, a signature for acceptance. Ranks and dollar figures inform the choice. They are not responses.\n',
        'r1': 'You can transfer, accept, avoid, or mitigate.\nTransfer uses insurance or a contract.\nInsurance pays after a loss.\nInsurance does not stop the theft.\nAccept means you live with it in writing.\nAccept is not the same as ignoring it.\nAvoid means you stop the activity.\nAvoid does not mean you are merely careful.\nMitigate means you apply a control.\nEncryption is a control, so it mitigates.\nSome risk can remain after a control.\nName what remains, and record it.\n',
        'r2': 'A kitchen with a dangerous slicer has four honest choices. Buy a policy that pays if someone is hurt, which is transfer, and the blade is still sharp. Write down that a small leftover chance is accepted, with a name and a date. Stop serving the dish that needs the slicer, which avoids that cut. Or fit a guard, which is a control and mitigates the chance. Hoping nobody notices is not on the list. Transfer, accept, avoid, and mitigate are those four choices for a risk, and a control or a policy still leaves you responsible for whatever the paper says remains.\n',
        'r4': 'Read the response word on the laptop row.\nFor mitigate, open the encryption console and note what is on.\nFor transfer, open the policy and note what it pays.\nFor avoid, confirm the activity has actually stopped.\nFor accept, find the signature, the owner, and the date.\nDo not add ignore as a response.\nDo not mark the risk gone because insurance exists.\nDo not change the rating to zero during this check.\n',
        'exam': 'Transfer uses insurance or a contract and does not prevent the event. Accept means living with the risk in a written decision, not ignoring it. Avoid means stopping the activity, not being careful while continuing it. Mitigate means applying a control, such as encryption, and leftover risk can remain. The trap is swapping these verbs. Insurance is not a control that stops theft. A control is not avoidance. A shrug is not acceptance. Dollar figures and high or low ranks help choose a response. They are not responses themselves.\n',
    },
    'Exemptions and exceptions': {
        'body': 'An exemption waives a requirement. An exception allows a temporary miss of a requirement. Both are written, both have an owner, and both have a date. Neither is a quiet skip. The difference is duration and intent. An exemption says this case will not be held to that requirement, for a stated reason, until the waiver is reviewed or withdrawn. An exception says this case is missing the requirement only until a stated end date, and the miss should close. A compensating control often sits beside either one so the waiver is not a blank check.\n\nThe mechanism is a record that can be audited without interviewing the person who remembers the hallway conversation. The record names the requirement, the system or device, the owner, the reason, the end or review date, and any compensating control. A device that cannot meet the screen-lock standard is a typical case. If the miss is temporary, the record is an exception: ninety days, a named owner, and a locked cabinet as compensation while a replacement is ordered. If a class of medical instruments can never run the company screen lock, leadership may grant an exemption for that class. The exemption is still written, still owned, and still dated for review. It is not an invitation for ordinary laptops to skip the lock.\n\nClinical engineering files exception EX-118 for infusion pump P-14. The pump cannot run the standard screen lock. The owner is Helen Nguyen. The end date is ninety days from 1 March. The compensating control is a locked cabinet, with a key log at the nursing desk. On day ninety the exception should close because the pump was moved, replaced, or covered by a reviewed exemption. A laptop that "feels similar" does not inherit EX-118. If someone copies the exception onto nurse laptops, the copy is invalid because those laptops can meet the lock standard. Quietly disabling the lock with no record is neither an exemption nor an exception. It is a control failure.\n\nDefenders read the dates before they debate the prose. An exception past ninety days with no closure is a miss that became permanent by neglect. An exemption with no review date is a waiver that escaped. Sample the compensating control: walk to the cabinet and see whether the key log is used. Compare the scope with the devices. One pump is not the whole hospital. Do not widen EX-118 during the review. Do not delete it to clear an aging report.\n\nStudents swap the two words and also confuse both with risk acceptance. Acceptance is a response to a stated risk: you live with that risk in writing. An exception is permission to miss a specific requirement for a limited time. An exemption is a waiver of the requirement. A ninety-day screen-lock miss is an exception, not an exemption, and not a vague acceptance of "security risk." If the record has no owner or no date, it is not one of these two, no matter what the title says.\n',
        'r0': 'An exemption waives a requirement. An exception allows a temporary miss. Both need writing, an owner, and a date. Exception EX-118 covers infusion pump P-14, which cannot run the screen-lock standard. Helen Nguyen owns it. It lasts ninety days from 1 March. The compensating control is a locked cabinet with a key log. Nurse laptops that can meet the lock do not inherit this record.\n\nA class of instruments that can never take the lock would need an exemption, still owned and still reviewed on a date. A past-due exception is not an exemption. It is a miss that was not closed. Risk acceptance is a different record: a decision to live with a named risk. Disabling a lock with no paper is neither an exemption nor an exception. Check the key log before you trust the compensating control.\n',
        'r1': 'An exemption waives a requirement.\nAn exception is a temporary miss.\nBoth must be written.\nBoth need an owner and a date.\nA ninety-day screen-lock miss is an exception.\nA locked cabinet can compensate while the clock runs.\nThat cabinet does not erase the standard.\nLaptops that can comply do not inherit the waiver.\nA quiet skip is not an exception.\nA quiet skip is not an exemption.\nA past-due exception should be closed or rewritten.\nAcceptance of a risk is a different record.\n',
        'r2': 'An old clinic stair may never meet a new railing rule, so the safety office writes a waiver, reviews it on a date, and adds a gate as compensation. A broken lock on an ordinary door gets a different paper: a short permission to miss the rule while a lock is ordered, with a cabinet or a guard in the meantime. When the date arrives, the temporary paper should die. Exemptions and exceptions are those two papers for security requirements. A missing lock with no paper is not a waiver of either kind, and one special stair does not excuse every door in the building.\n',
        'r4': 'Open exception EX-118 and write down the end date.\nWrite down the owner and the device name.\nCheck that the device really cannot meet the screen lock.\nWalk to the locked cabinet and look at the key log.\nDo not extend the ninety days during this look.\nDo not copy the exception onto laptops that can comply.\nDo not relabel a past-due exception as an exemption.\nLeave the screen-lock standard itself unchanged.\n',
        'exam': "An exemption waives a requirement. An exception allows a temporary miss. Both are written, owned, and dated. The trap is swapping them, or treating a quiet skip as either one. A ninety-day screen-lock gap with a locked cabinet is an exception, not an exemption. A standing waiver for a device that cannot comply can be an exemption, and it still needs an owner and a review date. Risk acceptance is a response to a risk, not a synonym for these records. One pump's exception does not cover laptops that can meet the standard.\n",
    },
    'Risk reporting': {
        'body': 'Risk reporting tells the people who can decide what the risks look like now, how that picture has moved, and which decisions they need to make. It is a communication, not the register itself and not a raw log dump. A useful report is short enough to be read before the meeting. It names overdue items, trends, and the specific choice being asked. A report that says everything is fine, while review dates have passed, is a failure of reporting even if the slides are polished.\n\nThe mechanism is a selection from the register aimed at a decision maker, with comparison to the last period. Start with what changed: new risks, worsened ratings, responses that slipped, review dates in the past. Then state the ask. "Three risks are past their review date, and one of them needs a decision in this meeting" is a report. If the laptop risk was medium likelihood last quarter and is unchanged this quarter, say it is unchanged. If four machines are still unencrypted after a promise, say the response is late. The report does not quietly lower a rating. It carries the rating the register holds and asks the owner or the steering group to act.\n\nEvery quarter, analyst Luis Ortiz sends the steering packet the week before the meeting. This quarter\'s first page says three risks are past their review date. One of them is the laptop row, and the decision requested is whether to fund the remaining encryption this quarter or to record a time-boxed exception. The other two late rows are listed with owners and dates, and no decision is requested beyond instructing the owners to close the reviews. A small trend table shows one risk improved, one worsened, and the rest held. Luis does not hide the three late dates inside a green pie chart.\n\nDefenders compare the report with the register on the same day. Counts of open risks, counts of past-due reviews, and the names of high items should match. If the register shows three past-due rows and the report shows zero, the report is wrong. If the report asks for no decision while a high item sits outside appetite, the report is incomplete. Mitigation of a bad reporting habit is to fix the template: overdue dates on page one, a single explicit ask, and a trend against last quarter. Do not edit the register to match a rosy draft. Do not add new risk analysis in the slide without writing it back to the row.\n\nThe trap is confusing the report with the register, or confusing it with continuous watching. The register is the list with owners, ratings, responses, and dates. Continuous watching is the daily feed. The report is what leadership sees so they can decide while a choice still matters. A dashboard can feed the report and still hide a collector that stopped yesterday, so the narrative has to say whether the inputs are current. The rows are the record. The report is the ask.\n',
        'r0': "A risk report is a decision memo drawn from the register. This quarter's first page states that three risks are past their review date and asks for a decision on one of them, the laptop row: fund remaining encryption this quarter, or record a time-boxed exception. The other two late rows are named with owners, without pretending they need the same vote. The trend line says what improved, what worsened, and what held since last quarter.\n\nThe report must match the register's overdue count on the same day. A green chart of filled-in response words hides lateness and is not a trend. The register remains the system of record. Daily monitoring is a feed, not the quarterly ask. Do not lower a rating in the slides.\n",
        'r1': 'Risk reporting talks to people who can decide.\nTell them the current risks.\nTell them the trend since last time.\nTell them the decision you need.\nPut overdue review dates on the first page.\nThree late risks should be visible.\nAsk for a decision on the one that needs it.\nDo not hide late dates in a green chart.\nThe report is not the register.\nThe register keeps the owners and the dates.\nMatch the report counts to the register.\nDo not lower a rating just for the slides.\n',
        'r2': 'A clinic board does not read every chart in the building. The medical director brings a short note: what worsened, what is late, and the one choice the board must make this month. The full racks stay in the records room for anyone who needs a detail. A cheerful cover that hides three late reviews would send the board home with the wrong idea. The board can vote only on what the note actually puts in front of them. Risk reporting is that short note about risks. The register remains the rack of records, and a daily monitor beep is not the same as the monthly ask.\n',
        'r4': "Open this quarter's report and the register on the same day.\nCount past-due review dates in both places.\nWrite down any mismatch in those counts.\nFind the decision the report actually asks for.\nDo not edit ratings in the slides.\nDo not mark the quarter green if three reviews are late.\nConfirm the trend compares with last quarter.\nLeave the register rows in place if the report is wrong.\n",
        'exam': 'Risk reporting tells decision makers the current risks, the trend, and the decision needed. The trap is treating the slide as the register, or hiding past-due reviews inside a calm chart. Three risks past their review date belong on the first page, with a decision asked on the one that needs it. The register holds owners, ratings, responses, and dates. A daily feed is monitoring, not the quarterly report. Do not change a rating in the narrative to make the trend look kinder. If the counts disagree, the report is wrong.\n',
    },
    'Business impact analysis': {
        'body': 'A business impact analysis asks which processes matter, how fast each one must return, and how much data loss is tolerable. It is done before an outage, while people can still think. The result is an ordered picture of the work, not a list of hackers and not a yearly dollar loss. Scheduling that must return in four hours is not the same process as a newsletter that can wait a day. Those two facts, written in advance, are what stop a disaster from being prioritized by whoever shouts first.\n\nThe mechanism is interviews with process owners, plus dependencies, written as time limits. For each process, ask what stops in the business if this process stops, who depends on it, and how the harm grows with hours. The output that later becomes a recovery time is how quickly the service must return. The output that later becomes a recovery point is how much data loss is tolerable, measured in time. Scheduling might have to be back in four hours, and it might tolerate only one hour of lost booking changes. The newsletter might tolerate a full day of downtime. The analysis records both processes before anyone argues over a dark screen.\n\nBefore hurricane season, office manager Nora Berg sits with the analyst and walks the Monday morning clinic. If scheduling is down past four hours, nurses cannot see the booked patients and the waiting room fails. If more than about an hour of booking changes is lost, patients are double-booked. The newsletter, by contrast, can wait a day without changing care. Nora writes those limits into the analysis with her name as process owner, dated this quarter, not during a storm. The sheet also lists the scheduling database as a dependency.\n\nDefenders check that the analysis exists before the crisis and that the times are specific. "As soon as possible" is not a result. Four hours and one day are results. Confirm the process owner agreed, because an analyst\'s guess about scheduling is not the business speaking. After an incident, compare what was restored first with what the analysis ordered. If the newsletter returned in an hour and scheduling waited a day, the response ignored the analysis.\n\nThe neighbors are risk assessment, the recovery targets, and the recovery plan. Risk assessment names threats, weaknesses, and likelihood, and it may price a yearly loss. A business impact analysis can live without those threats because a flood, a fire, and a cut fiber can all demand the same return of scheduling. Recovery time and recovery point are the named targets this analysis feeds: how quickly the service must return, and how much data loss is tolerable, measured in time. The plan is the procedure that tries to hit those targets. Candidates swap the analysis for the plan, or they treat a four-hour scheduling need as a likelihood. The four hours is a limit on downtime, chosen before the outage, not a guess about how often the outage occurs.\n',
        'r0': 'A business impact analysis sets priorities before the outage. Scheduling must return within four hours, and about one hour of booking changes is the most data loss that process can tolerate. The newsletter can wait a day. Nora Berg, the office manager, owns the scheduling statement, and the write-up is dated this quarter.\n\n"As soon as possible" is not an output. Threat frequency is not an output either. A yearly money loss belongs to quantitative risk analysis. How quickly a service must return, and how much data loss is tolerable in time, are the targets this analysis feeds. The recovery procedure is a later document that tries to meet those targets. If a real event restores the newsletter first and leaves scheduling down all day, the response contradicted the analysis.\n',
        'r1': 'A business impact analysis ranks the work.\nDo it before anything is down.\nAsk which process must return first.\nScheduling may have to return in four hours.\nA newsletter may be allowed to wait a day.\nAlso ask how much recent work can be lost.\nWrite the times in hours, not in slogans.\nName the process owner on the sheet.\nList the systems that process depends on.\nThis study is not a threat catalog.\nHow often a storm hits is a different question.\nThe recovery steps come after these time limits.\n',
        'r2': 'Before a storm, a clinic decides which rooms must reopen first and which paperwork can wait until tomorrow. The decision is taped up while the lights are still on, with a name beside it. Arguing about the waiting room during the blackout is how the gift shop gets power first. Power goes to the waiting room because the note said so while the lights were on. A business impact analysis is that beforehand ordering of processes. It states how fast the important work must return and how much recent work can be lost, and the step-by-step rebuild is a separate set of instructions written to hit those limits.\n',
        'r4': 'Open the business impact analysis and check the date.\nConfirm it was written before the latest outage.\nWrite down the return time for scheduling and for the newsletter.\nWrite down how much scheduling data loss is allowed.\nConfirm a process owner signed those times.\nDo not accept "as soon as possible" as a time.\nDo not add threat frequencies to this sheet during the check.\nLeave the recovery procedure in its own document.\n',
        'exam': 'A business impact analysis says which processes matter, how fast they must return, and how much data loss is tolerable. It is written before the outage. Scheduling in four hours and a newsletter that can wait a day are examples of those priorities. The trap is calling this a threat list, a yearly loss calculation, or the recovery procedure itself. Likelihood belongs to risk assessment. The procedure comes later. "As soon as possible" is not an analysis result. Recovery time and recovery point are the named targets this work feeds, not synonyms for the workshop.\n',
    },
    'Recovery time and recovery point': {
        'body': 'Recovery time, often called RTO, is how quickly the service must return. Recovery time is the limit on downtime. Recovery point, often called RPO, is how much data loss is tolerable, measured in time. Recovery point is the limit on how much recent work you can afford to lose. A four-hour recovery time and a one-hour recovery point are two different promises. The spare or the failover must be able to carry the service within four hours. The copies of the data must be frequent enough that you never have to roll back more than one hour. Meeting one promise does not meet the other.\n\nThe mechanism is a pair of design constraints that come from the business impact analysis and then get tested. Scheduling at the clinic must return in four hours, so RTO is four hours. Losing more than one hour of booking changes causes double-booked patients, so RPO is one hour. Backups taken once a night miss that recovery point even if the restore is fast, because a theft of the server at 16:00 rolls the desk back to 02:00, a fourteen-hour gap. Replication or backups at least every hour are what make a one-hour recovery point possible. A hot spare that can be serving in ninety minutes meets a four-hour recovery time only if someone has actually practiced the switch.\n\nOn a Tuesday drill, engineer Chris Diaz restores scheduling from a replica that is twenty minutes old, and the service is taking bookings again in ninety minutes. That drill meets both limits: ninety minutes is inside four hours, and twenty minutes of data loss is inside one hour. A second drill restores last night\'s 02:00 backup in two hours. The two-hour restore meets the recovery time and fails the recovery point, because six or more hours of bookings are gone. The ticket for the second drill is marked "RTO met, RPO missed," which is the sentence the team needs. A fast restore of a stale copy is not a success.\n\nDefenders compare the last successful test with both numbers, not with a vendor\'s brochure. Write the achieved downtime and the age of the data that was restored. If the newest restorable copy is older than the recovery point, the design misses RPO no matter how proud the restore speed is. If the practiced restore takes longer than the recovery time, the design misses RTO even when copies are continuous. Do not change the business limits to match a weak backup product. Change the copy frequency or the spare. Mean time to repair is the average of past fixes, not this requirement, so a day-long average misses a four-hour recovery time.\n\nThe classic trap is swapping the two letters. Recovery time is about the service returning. Recovery point is about data loss, measured in time, not about how fast the technician works. A second trap calls either number the mean time to repair or the mean time between failures. Those averages describe failure and fix history. They do not set the business limit. Speed of restore addresses recovery time. Age of the copy addresses recovery point.\n',
        'r0': "RTO is how quickly the service must return. Scheduling's RTO is four hours. RPO is how much data loss is tolerable, measured in time. Scheduling's RPO is one hour. A Tuesday drill that returned service in ninety minutes from a twenty-minute-old replica met both. A restore that finished in two hours from the 02:00 backup met the four-hour downtime limit and missed the one-hour data-loss limit.\n\nNightly copies cannot satisfy a one-hour recovery point. A spare still in a box cannot satisfy a four-hour recovery time. The newsletter's one-day allowance is a looser recovery time for a different process. Do not apply it to scheduling. Mean time to repair is an average of past fixes, not the recovery time objective. If that average is a day, the four-hour requirement is being missed.\n",
        'r1': "Recovery time is how fast the service must return.\nRecovery point is how much data loss you can stand.\nMeasure that data loss in time.\nScheduling may allow four hours of downtime.\nScheduling may allow only one hour of lost work.\nA fast restore can still use a stale copy.\nA stale copy misses the recovery point.\nA nightly backup is too old for a one-hour limit.\nA spare in a box misses a four-hour return.\nThe newsletter's longer wait is for the newsletter.\nDo not swap the two limits.\nAn average repair time is not the requirement.\n",
        'r2': "A lunch counter promises to be serving again within a set number of hours, and it also promises not to lose more than a set slice of recent orders. Reopening quickly with yesterday's order pad throws away today's tickets. Keeping a fresh pad does not help if the kitchen stays closed all afternoon. Yesterday's pad can reopen the counter on time and still fail the promise about today's tickets. Both promises have to be true. Recovery time is how quickly the service must return. Recovery point is how much data loss is tolerable, measured in time. A fast return from an old copy meets only the first promise.\n",
        'r4': "Write down the recovery time and the recovery point for scheduling.\nOpen the latest restore drill ticket.\nRecord how long the service was down.\nRecord the age of the copy that was restored.\nDo not call the drill a pass if only one number was met.\nDo not loosen the one-hour data limit to fit a nightly backup.\nDo not borrow the newsletter's one-day wait for scheduling.\nLeave mean repair averages in their own notes.\n",
        'exam': 'RTO is how quickly the service must return. RPO is how much data loss is tolerable, measured in time. The trap is swapping them. A four-hour recovery time and a one-hour recovery point can diverge: a two-hour restore from a night-old backup meets the time limit and misses the data limit. Nightly backups do not satisfy a one-hour recovery point. Mean time to repair is not RTO, and mean time between failures is not RPO. Those are averages from failure history. The business limits come from the impact analysis and are tested in drills.\n',
    },
    'Mean time to repair and between failures': {
        'body': 'Mean time to repair, MTTR, is how long a fix takes on average. Mean time between failures, MTBF, is how long you typically run before something breaks. The two averages answer different questions. A higher MTBF means fewer failures, because the stretches of normal running are longer. A lower MTTR means faster repairs. Raising one number does not raise the other. MTTR is not MTBF. A disk array that fails rarely and then takes a day to rebuild has a high MTBF and an MTTR of about a day, and both facts belong in the capacity plan.\n\nThe mechanism is two averages from history, then a comparison with the recovery requirement. Collect the gaps between failures and average them for MTBF. Collect the durations of the repairs and average them for MTTR. For array STOR-2, two failures in about four years produced gaps near 17520 hours, so MTBF is about 17520 hours, roughly two years of running. Each rebuild took about a day, so MTTR is 24 hours. A rough availability figure is MTBF divided by the sum of MTBF and MTTR: 17520 divided by 17544, which is about 99.86 percent of hours running. That percentage can look comforting while the day of rebuild is still a full outage when it hits. The capacity plan has to name the spare disks and the person who can start a day-long rebuild. The long quiet gap does not shorten the day you are down.\n\nScheduling\'s recovery time is four hours. STOR-2\'s MTTR of 24 hours is longer than four hours, so the average repair misses the requirement even though the array fails rarely. A high MTBF does not rescue a slow rebuild. The design answer is a failover or a hot spare that can carry scheduling inside four hours, while the failed shelf rebuilds for a day. The capacity plan should show both numbers with their units: hours between failures, hours to repair.\n\nDefenders compute the averages from tickets before they repeat them in a briefing. List each failure time and each return-to-service time for STOR-2. Compare MTTR with the recovery time objective. When 24 hours is greater than four hours, write the gap into the capacity plan as a design problem, not as a sentence that the objective was "basically met." Higher MTBF means fewer failures.\n\nThe exam swaps these two averages with each other and with the recovery targets. MTTR is repair time. MTBF is time between failures. A higher MTBF means fewer failures, not more, and not longer repairs. Recovery time is how quickly the service must return, which is a requirement. Recovery point is how much data loss is tolerable, measured in time. Neither requirement is an average of past breakdowns. A day to rebuild is MTTR. It is not a recovery point, and it is not proof the array is unreliable if the gaps between failures are measured in years. The unreliable move is planning as if the rare failure will be a short one.\n',
        'r0': 'MTTR is mean time to repair. MTBF is mean time between failures. A higher MTBF means fewer failures. MTTR is not MTBF. Array STOR-2 runs about 17520 hours between failures, roughly two years, and takes about 24 hours to rebuild. Availability is roughly 17520 divided by 17544, about 99.86 percent of hours up. The remaining day is still an outage.\n\nScheduling must return in four hours. An MTTR of 24 hours misses that requirement. A hot spare or failover belongs in the capacity plan because the long MTBF does not shrink the rebuild. Do not take MTTR from a brochure when tickets show a day. Do not swap the two figures. Recovery point, the tolerable data loss measured in time, is not MTBF and not MTTR. Both averages need units and a ticket source.\n',
        'r1': 'Mean time to repair is the average fix time.\nMean time between failures is the average run time.\nA higher time between failures means fewer failures.\nThe repair average is not the gap between failures.\nA rare break can still take a day to fix.\nA day-long rebuild belongs in the capacity plan.\nA four-hour return target loses to a day-long fix.\nA long quiet period does not shorten the repair.\nUse ticket history, not a shiny brochure.\nA hot spare may be required to meet the target.\nDo not call a longer gap a worse product.\nKeep data-loss limits in a different note.\n',
        'r2': "An oven can run for years between breakdowns and still take a full day to repair when the element fails. The long peaceful stretch is one fact. The day without cakes is another. Buying parts and training a person for that day belongs on the capacity sheet, because the peaceful years do not bake tomorrow's orders. A plan that stocks parts for that day is ready, and a plan that only brags about quiet years is not. Mean time between failures is the long stretch, and a longer stretch means fewer breakdowns. Mean time to repair is the day with the wrench. Mixing those two clocks makes the kitchen plan for the wrong emergency.\n",
        'r4': 'List STOR-2 failures and the hours from break to return.\nAverage the gaps between failures and label that MTBF.\nAverage the repair durations and label that MTTR.\nConfirm a higher MTBF is described as fewer failures.\nDo not swap the 24 hour repair with the 17520 hour gap.\nCompare the repair average with the four-hour return target.\nDo not edit the return target to match the slow rebuild.\nWrite spare parts and a person into the capacity note.\n',
        'exam': 'MTTR is mean time to repair. MTBF is mean time between failures. A higher MTBF means fewer failures. MTTR is not MTBF. The trap is swapping a day-long rebuild with a multi-year gap, or treating the rebuild average as the recovery time objective. Recovery time is how quickly the service must return. Recovery point is data loss tolerated, measured in time. A 24-hour MTTR misses a four-hour recovery time even when MTBF is about 17520 hours. The percentage of hours up does not erase the day the array is rebuilding. Ticket history outranks a brochure.\n',
    },
}
